var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#The-experimental-protocol","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"This page is the one statement of the evaluation protocol of the stack. A paper's drivers and the package's own sources point here, and their headers state only what is specific to them: their command line, schemas and stages.\n\nThe mechanisms live in this package's modules: Tuning (the cross-validation specification), Stats (the statistics), Evaluation (the composition evaluator and the single cross-validation path), Experiments (run records), Render (figures), and Pools and Datasets (data). Every parameter of record is in configs/default.toml, configs/tuning.toml or configs/splits.toml.\n\nAbbreviations used on this page:\n\nAbbreviation Meaning\nCV cross-validation\nLO local optimization, the refinement step after RANSAC selects a model\nGT ground truth\nBCa bias-corrected and accelerated (bootstrap interval)\nmAA mean average accuracy\nAUC area under the curve\nE, F, H the essential matrix, the fundamental matrix and the homography, and the tracks that estimate them\nETH3D the ETH Zürich 3D reconstruction benchmark","title":"The experimental protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#0.-Cross-validation:-tuning-against-reporting","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Every tuning names its data in its [tuning.<name>] spec, as a cross_validation value (CrossValidation; the schema is in The [tuning] schema). The spec has three parts.\n\nScenes. A RegisteredSceneSplit is a split that the spec names and that is listed once, in configs/splits.toml, the one file of record of every scene list of the stack. Validation scenes are the tuning side, where every hyperparameter is validated. Test scenes are the reporting side. The two lists must be disjoint. The parser writes the resolved lists into the complete spec, so they enter the spec hash, the run key and every tuning artifact.\nUnits. Each side has a UnitSampling over the data's sampling unit. A dataset declares its unit with sampling_unit: an image pair for a two-view dataset, a query for an absolute-pose one. The samplings are ExhaustiveSampling, StratifiedSampling (a fraction of each scene, then at most cap_per_scene, which preserves the mix of scenes) and UniformSampling (a fraction of the side's pooled units, then at most cap). Every sampler permutes, keeps the fraction, applies the cap, and only then sorts back into data order, so a cap never keeps a prefix of the data.\nSeeds. Every draw is seeded by stable_seed, the SHA-256 hash of the spec's seed and of the name it applies to, such as a scene name or a budget and draw index. Base.hash is never used, because it is not stable across Julia versions. seed + index is never used either: with a named seed, adding, removing or reordering scenes leaves every other scene's sample unchanged.\n\nA driver accepts the samplings it supports through the signature of the method it calls, for example cv::CrossValidation{<:SceneSplit, StratifiedSampling}. An unsupported spec is then a MethodError and is never silently reinterpreted. No driver, paper config or package source states a scene list, a split, a pair prefix or a sampling seed in code. Run directories come from the run records (run_key, experiment_dir, results_root).","title":"0. Cross-validation: tuning against reporting"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#1.-The-fairness-axis:-the-scale-multiple","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Methods are compared at the same operating point, not at arbitrary native values. The shared axis is the scale multiple: the number of inlier-noise standard deviations σ at which a method's inlier/outlier decision flips. It is anchored at the reference scale ŝ = √median σ̂² of the validation data (reference_scale). The maps from a scale multiple to a native width are VisualGeometryToolkitCore's (to_native, native_map, decision_width), one per width axis:\n\naxis native width at scale multiple k\nLossWidth (threshold family) loss_width = k·ŝ (the truncated loss: identity)\nOutlierHalfwidth (Student-t marginal) outlier_halfwidth = ŝ·√(π/2)·e^{k²/2} at codimension d_g = 1\na lifted loss the half-weight width of its shape\n\nOne refit path, one scale port. Every refiner runs through the one refit layer (refit_imc! and VisualGeometryToolkitCore's RefitFamily, with the values NativeRefit and ExternalRefit). The deployed native width reaches a refiner only as its width on the axis it consumes (consumed_axis, consumed_native). A loss-width refiner that rides on an outlier-half-width selector grid converts at the refit protocol's fixed OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE (configs/default.toml, table [refit]). No refiner estimates its own scale from a plug-in window.\n\nThe outlier-half-width axis carries a codimension. The marginal map takes the residual codimension d_g = codimension(problem_type): 1 on the E and F tracks and 2 on H. At ŝ = 1 over the window [0.8, 6.0], the upper edge moves from 8.2293e7 (d_g = 1) to 1.0156e4 (d_g = 2), a change of 3.91 decades. A native grid and the window searched inside it must therefore be cut at the same d_g. The coverage window rule cuts the grid through the same map that the search window uses, and this is what makes its coverage guarantee hold. d_g has no default anywhere, and every tuning artifact persists it as \"d_g\".","title":"1. The fairness axis: the scale multiple"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#2.-Tuning:-the-single-CV-path","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Every hyperparameter is cross-validated by one discipline, the coarse-to-fine search (coarse_fine_search, with a [tuning.<name>] spec of scheme joint_coarse_fine, or coarse_fine for one scalar). It runs in three steps:\n\nIt evaluates a coarse product grid. Whenever the joint argmin lies within edge_margin points of a face, the grid extends that axis.\nIt evaluates a fine product grid over the bracket of the coarse argmin on every axis.\nIt takes the free argmin of the validation objective on the fine grid.\n\nWindows are located by expansion; they are never pinned or widened by hand. Several searches share one sweep of a staged validation pool (the search keys of one call, or coarse_fine_cv_batched for scalars), and an expensive axis is evaluated one value at a time. The artifact (save_coarse_fine_artifact) stores the per-unit sufficient statistics of each cell, from which the bootstrap of the selection is exact; deployed_setting reads the selection. A paper tunes a scorer and LO arm as a sequence of such scalar CVs: first the selection width, then the refinement width.\n\nThe tuned point is persisted as a tuning artifact (save_tuning_artifact): the spec, its hash, the grid specs, the sampling unit and the code identity. Only the deployed readers read it (deployed_arm_settings, deployed_budget_settings, deployed_basin_settings). They refuse a tuned point on a boundary of its window (the artifact's boundary_hits, or enforce_probe_interior for a probe artifact), because a window that clips the argmin has not located it.","title":"2. Tuning: the single CV path"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#3.-Miscalibration:-the-fixed-scale-multiple-protocol","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Each method is tuned once at the true validation scale. Then the deployed plug-in scale is corrupted, σ̂ → ζσ̂, while the tuned scale multiple is held fixed (corrupt_native). Every native parameter is homogeneous of degree 1 in σ̂, so the corruption multiplies each method's tuned parameter by the same factor ζ. Each method thus suffers the same relative error in its own units. A tuned scale multiple is anchored on the data's own partition and is not corrupted. Test data is untouched. Evaluation reads the grid column nearest to ζ·native* (nearest_column). This lookup is for evaluation only; a tuning never takes a nearest column.","title":"3. Miscalibration: the fixed-scale-multiple protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#4.-The-validation-budget:-the-tuning-budget-sweep","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Draws come from the band-eligible validation units: those with a finite σ̂² inside the band [ŝ_min², ŝ_max²] of scale_bounds. The draws are the spec's BudgetSampling (budget_units). Draw r of budget n takes n units without replacement from rng(stable_seed(seed, \"budget\", n, r)) and sorts them.\n\nFor each draw, the protocol computes the reference scale ŝ(n) of the drawn units, takes the free argmin inside the window at ŝ(n), and evaluates the chosen point on the test data (WindowedGridMethod, compute_method_rows). The reported point is the mean over draws of the per-draw test medians. The interval is a BCa interval over draws, resampled from resampling_rng(budget, n), that is rng(stable_seed(seed, \"resampling\", n)), a stream of its own and never a draw's.","title":"4. The validation budget: the tuning-budget sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#5.-The-sample-budget","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"evaluate_sample_budget walks each pair's shared hypothesis stream with running-best selectors. Selection checkpoints come every batch_size samples, and LO checkpoints on the lo_every grid (SampleBudgetConfig). Every arm's selection and refinement widths come from its tuning artifact through deployed_budget_settings. There is no fallback and no self-tuning. Every LO call runs within the evaluation's equal budget of model updates (lo_iteration_budget, configs/default.toml, table [local_optimization]).","title":"5. The sample budget"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#6.-The-metric:-pose-error,-with-the-F-upgrade","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"All pose errors use the coherent metric max(ΔR, Δt) in degrees over the single best decomposition (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's imc_pose_error, named after the Image Matching Challenge, IMC). Pool models are pool-frame F containers on both tracks. A pool model is therefore always scored through the intrinsics upgrade model_imc(F, geom): K₂ᵀFK₁ → E → the pose that resolves cheirality. It is never scored with the bare imc_pose_error(θ, R, t), which treats its argument as a metric essential matrix. That misuse once inflated the oracle floor of the ETH3D E track from 0.19° to 0.73°.","title":"6. The metric: pose error, with the F upgrade"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#7.-The-oracle-floor:-the-selection-lower-bound","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"For each miscalibration cell, the floor is floor = median_i min_{θ ∈ pool_i} imc(θ) over the test pairs (the budget engine's OracleSelector). Oracle selection consumes no plug-in scale, so the floor does not depend on ζ. It bounds selection only: a refit may go below it by leaving the discrete hypothesis pool. A miscalibration panel carries its floor (the oracle keyword of emit_miscal_faceted).","title":"7. The oracle floor: the selection lower bound"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#8.-Statistics","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"Point estimates and intervals go through Stats only:\n\nfinite_median. Argmin objectives use finite_median(...; empty = Inf), so that a column of only NaN never wins.\nbootstrap_ci, the BCa interval.\nscene_mean_median_rows!, the mean of per-scene medians, with a BCa interval over scenes resampled from stable_seed(seed, \"scene_mean_median\", j).\n\nThe mAA follows one rule, eligible_maa. A pair with ground truth and enough valid correspondences to run is eligible, and a run on it that yields no model scores 0 in the denominator. A pair that cannot be processed is skipped. pair_outcomes reports the counts beside every mAA. The mAA is the continuous AUC of the accuracy curve up to maa_threshold_deg (configs/default.toml, table [metrics]; VisualGeometryToolkitCore's mean_average_accuracy).","title":"8. Statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"protocol/#9.-Figure-conventions","page":"The experimental protocol","text":"The Render emitters enforce a house style, so that figures are uniform across papers:\n\nConfidence intervals are shaded bands, never error bars: scene-clustered BCa 95% intervals (section 8).\nLabels and markers come from the framework, never from hand-written tables: VisualGeometryToolkitCore's lo_label(lo) and lo_marker(lo). A partition-anchored scale is marked -\\hat s, a fixed cross-validated scale is unmarked, and a self-scaling LO carries no suffix. A renderer never spells out a method's name.\nOne arm has one colour everywhere; the marker shape is the solver family.\nThe oracle (the GT-best model) is a solid black line with no markers.\nBasin panels (emit_basin_faceted): the left panel is the success rate P(e ≤ succ°) on [0, 1]. The right panel is the conditional mean E[e | e ≤ succ°] on a linear axis with degree ticks, masked where the success rate is below min_success_for_cond_mean (configs/default.toml, table [render]). The reference arm without LO is the perturbed seed.\nMedian-error panels keep a log axis, since the median error is unbounded there.","title":"9. Figure conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"reproduce/#Reproducible-figures","page":"Reproducible figures","text":"Every figure, table and number macro in a camera-ready paper is generated by tracked code from declared data. Each one is listed in the paper project's REPRODUCE.md, and a test enforces the list. A hand-made asset is allowed only when it is declared static and carries a provenance note.","title":"Reproducible figures"},{"category":"section","location":"reproduce/#The-asset-index","page":"Reproducible figures","text":"The index is a Markdown table. It has at least the columns asset, kind and generator; the columns label, config, data and provenance are read when present. Cells hold their values in backticks. Paths are relative to the project directory, and an asset may be a glob: * matches within a path segment and ** across segments.\n\n| label | asset | kind | generator | config | data | provenance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `fig:teaser` | `papers/images/intro_overlay.png` | generated | `scripts/make_intro.jl` | `configs/figures/intro.toml` | images tier | |\n| — | `papers/figures/stats.tex` | generated | `scripts/make_stats.jl` | `configs/figures/stats.toml` | promoted run | |\n| `fig:arch` | `papers/images/architecture.pdf` | static | — | — | — | drawn in Inkscape by the authors from the network definition, 2026-05 |","title":"The asset index"},{"category":"section","location":"reproduce/#Generators-and-their-configs","page":"Reproducible figures","text":"A generator writes its asset when it names the file, or when it declares it in a comment line reproduce: writes <path-or-glob>. It reads its config of record when it names the file, or when it declares reproduce: reads <path>. Both paths are relative to the project, and any comment syntax works.\n\nEvery parameter of a generator belongs in its config of record, not in the script. That covers frame picks, crops, seeds, panel lists, sizes and inputs.","title":"Generators and their configs"},{"category":"section","location":"reproduce/#The-enforcing-test","page":"Reproducible figures","text":"The project's suite holds the contract:\n\nusing Test\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce: reproduce_index_violations\n\nvs = reproduce_index_violations(project_dir; papers = [\"papers/main.tex\", \"papers/supp.tex\"],\n                                require_config = true)\nforeach(println, vs)\n@test isempty(vs)\n\nreproduce_index_violations reports each of these:\n\nan asset the paper reads that no entry covers;\na missing asset;\na reference whose path is not static;\na stale entry;\nan unknown kind;\na generated entry without an existing generator;\na generator that does not write its asset;\na generated entry without a config of record (with require_config);\na missing config, or one that its generator does not read;\na static entry without provenance;\nan entry filed under the label of another float;\na number defined by a generated macro file and re-typed as a literal in the prose. This check is best effort. A coincidence is exempted with % reproduce: literal-ok on its line.\n\nA paper built outside its own directory — a main.tex in paper/src/ compiled from paper/ that writes \\input{src/sec/intro} — resolves its references through search_dirs (project-relative), the build's compile directory and TEXINPUTS: reproduce_index_violations(dir; papers, search_dirs = [\"paper\"]), or paper_project_violations(dir; papers, reproduce = (; search_dirs = [\"paper\"])).","title":"The enforcing test"},{"category":"section","location":"reproduce/#Verify,-deploy-and-the-numbers-file","page":"Reproducible figures","text":"A generator renders into its results, never into the paper. The last stages of a regeneration compare each render with the submitted copy and replace a submitted asset only with the author's sign-off:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce\n\nverify_assets(outdir, paperdir, names)          # writes outdir/verify.tsv, prints each verdict\ndeploy_assets(outdir, paperdir, names;          # verifies first; a differing submitted copy is\n              replace_submitted = false)        # refused unless signed off\n\nA .tex asset is compared by its numbers as well as its bytes (compare_fragments: its pgfplots coordinates and its macros), so a reformatted fragment reports \"same numbers\". The numbers file is one more asset under the same rule: gather_numbers writes it beside the generator's results from the drivers' fragments, one \\newcommand per number, and deploy_assets puts it into the paper. uncited_macros lists the numbers the paper defines but never cites, over the files the paper reads (the ones paper_asset_graph visits), with an allow table of reasons.\n\nA driver defines top-level constants meant for a process of its own; run_isolated includes it in a module of its own, so one regeneration script can run every driver in one warm session.\n\nsrc/reproduce/reproduce_index.jl and src/reproduce/paper_assets.jl depend on Base only, so an environment that cannot load this package can include them by path.","title":"Verify, deploy and the numbers file"},{"category":"section","location":"api/evaluation/#API:-Evaluation","page":"Evaluation","text":"The coarse-to-fine search, the bootstrap tuning harness and the composition evaluator.","title":"API: Evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/evaluation/#Public-names","page":"Evaluation","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/evaluation/#Internals","page":"Evaluation","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation","page":"Evaluation","text":"Evaluation\n\nThe evaluators. The composition evaluator runs a scorer composed with a local optimizer (LO) on stored pool pairs (build_pair_ctx, refit_imc!, apply_lo_refits, evaluate_sample_budget, live_runs, Roster). It is written against VisualGeometryToolkitCore's scoring and local-optimization contracts only, so it runs any scorer with any optimizer. The module also holds the coarse-to-fine search of every cross-validation (coarse_fine_search, coarse_fine_cv) and the bootstrap tuning harness (WindowedGridMethod, compute_method_rows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.NativeAxis","page":"Evaluation","text":"NativeAxis\n\nThe native width axes, Union{OutlierHalfwidth, LossWidth} (VisualGeometryToolkitCore WidthAxis): what a selector grid, a selection sweep or a refiner's own native lives on. ScaleMultiple() is a width parameterization, never a native.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.NativeAxis"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE","page":"Evaluation","text":"OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE\n\nThe refit protocol's operating scale multiple, 2.5 by default: the scale multiple at which the outlier-half-width ↔ loss-width crossover is inverted when a loss-width-consuming refiner rides an outlier-half-width selector grid (consumed_native). A definition of the refit protocol, not a tuned value: configs/default.toml [refit] operating_scale_multiple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.BootstrapMethod","page":"Evaluation","text":"BootstrapMethod\n\nA tuner the bootstrap harness runs (bootstrap_trials): it implements tune and evaluate. WindowedGridMethod is the one in this package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.BootstrapMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ComposedSpec","page":"Evaluation","text":"ComposedSpec(base, sel, selection::WidthAxis, kind::WidthAxis, ctor; source = TunedWidth())\n\nOne composed score+LO pipeline. sel is the key of its selection sweep (the selections idxs_of[sel] a refit seeds from); selection is that sweep's native axis — VisualGeometryToolkitCore's width_axis of the selection scoring (OutlierHalfwidth() for the Student-t marginal, LossWidth() for a threshold score); kind is the arm's width parameterization; ctor(value) builds the LO strategy at a width value (a scale-multiple arm bakes it in; the other kinds deliver it through their port, see width_delivery); source is where the width value comes from (WidthSource): a SelectionWidth arm requires kind == selection, and the tuning skips it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ComposedSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.FlatProtocol","page":"Evaluation","text":"FlatProtocol(val_pair_idx, test_pair_idx)\n\nFixed deterministic pair-level split, bootstrap pairs only. Trial-level variance reflects pair-sampling variance only (no scene component). Used for ScanNet1500 and other \"uniform sample of pairs\" datasets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.FlatProtocol"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.LoRefitSpec","page":"Evaluation","text":"LoRefitSpec(label, lo, axis, grid, idxs; bundle=false)\n\nOne post-LO refit of a selection sweep: refine the selected model idxs[p, t] with strategy lo at native parameter consumed_native(lo, axis, grid[t]). axis is the SELECTOR grid's native axis (a NativeAxis: OutlierHalfwidth() | LossWidth()) — cross-axis conversion happens per column inside the refit layer, so callers never pre-convert grids. lo = NoLocalOptimization() records the selection imc (no refit).\n\nColumn t refits the model selected at column seedcols[t] of idxs at the EXPLICIT native value natives[t] when those fields are set (no consumed_native conversion — the value is already in the refiner's units). Both default to the standard coupling (seedcols[t] = t, natives === nothing ⇒ derive from grid[t]). This is what the tuning grids (one fixed width parameter per spec) and the fixed-width deployment evaluations (seed at the ζ-scaled column, refit at the exactly-scaled native) are built from.\n\nwidths[t] (an absolute loss width): refit at the loss width widths[t] whatever the refiner — the BITWISE-identical call solo_refit_imc! makes, so the tuning and the ladder's test side deliver the width identically. A native refiner sweeps its seed at the column's nat′ (from grid[t] — scoring only, never a width) under the context's solo template and deploys with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, widths[t]²)); an external refiner refines at widths[t]. Mutually exclusive with natives.\n\ncolumns restricts which grid columns are EVALUATED (default: all). The others keep the imc matrix's NaN fill. The matrix stays full-width and every column index keeps its meaning, so boundary detection, the evaluation's seed columns and the checkpoint are untouched — this only stops computing cells that a windowed tune never reads, because it argmins inside the standardized window and the grid is far wider than that window. Measured on the PT-E 5% run of 2026-10-02: 45,567 of 386,000 cells per pair were inside the searched windows; the other 88% were computed and discarded. (A cell outside the searched window is never read by a windowed argmin.)\n\nfixed_s2s[t] (a fixed external scale): refit at the column's native exactly as the default path (the refit template), with the strategy deployed at that scale, with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, fixed_s2s[t])) — the refit_imc!(…; s2) conduit — so its loss width is scale_multiple·√s2 instead of the consensus set's scale_multiple·ŝ. The consensus-anchoring ablation evaluation (miscal_evaluation_refit with plugin_scale) is built from it. Combines with natives; mutually exclusive with widths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.LoRefitSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.MsacSpec","page":"Evaluation","text":"MsacSpec(name, loss_width, lo; refinement_loss_width = nothing)\n\nOne MSAC column of evaluate_sample_budget: MSAC selection (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's MsacScoring at threshold loss_width, pool frame), its running best refit by its own refiner lo (a paper's truncated refiner — an ExternalRefit strategy such as PoseLib's TLS-LM) at refinement_loss_width, the arm's tuned refinement loss width; nothing refits at the selection loss width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.MsacSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.OracleSelector","page":"Evaluation","text":"OracleSelector(name, outlier_halfwidth)\n\nOracle selector: the ground-truth-best SINGLE model by model_imc. Recomputes the imc per model (pruned by VisualGeometryToolkitCore's pool_imc, the stored lower bound on that imc) and refines that one model under LO (at a representative outlier half-width, the first method's, unless the config names the arm's refinement native).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.OracleSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.PairCtx","page":"Evaluation","text":"PairCtx\n\nEverything the refit layer needs about one pair. Built once per pair by build_pair_ctx and reused across every (LO refit spec × grid column), it holds the pair itself (a light PoolPair), its pool-frame problem (ep), the optional RelposeBundleProblem (bp, nothing unless bundle=true), the geometry, the problem class (problem_type) and the refit scoring TEMPLATES: scoring, the scoring every native refit is built from at its own outlier half-width (with_width(scoring, OutlierHalfwidth(nat′))), and solo_scoring, the one an absolute-width (solo) refit sweeps its seed with (by default scoring).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.PairCtx"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.PairGeom","page":"Evaluation","text":"PairGeom\n\nPer-pair calibration and ground truth, the context that scores a pool model's pose error. It scores any stored pool-frame fundamental's coherent imc pose error and holds the pool-frame csponds cs (x1 => x2) as a concretely typed Csponds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.PairGeom"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.RefitHalfwidth","page":"Evaluation","text":"RefitHalfwidth(lo, outlier_halfwidth, scoring_of)\nRefitHalfwidth(lo, outlier_halfwidth, template::AbstractScoring)\n\nlo refitting under the refit scoring at ITS OWN outlier half-width instead of the selection's: the in-loop form of the refit layer's native port (refit_imc!'s nat′, refit_model). The self-scaled arms (Stu-tU IRLS, BCD) read their half-width from the scoring the loop hands them, so inside a RANSAC loop they would otherwise refit at the SELECTION half-width — not the refit half-width their sequential CV tuned at the fixed a*. scoring_of(problem) builds the refit scoring; the template form is with_width(template, OutlierHalfwidth(outlier_halfwidth)).\n\nIn the loop, each call re-scores the model under the refit scoring in the loop's own buffers and runs the loop's own Phase-2 step on that consensus set (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's locally_optimize_rescored!): at outlier_halfwidth = the selection's it is the score the loop just ran, so the wrapped arm reproduces the bare arm exactly. The refined model goes back to the loop, which re-scores it under the SELECTION scoring and keeps it only if it beats the incumbent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.RefitHalfwidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.Roster","page":"Evaluation","text":"Roster(composed, solo, basin)\n\nA paper's arm roster, the argument of every reader that rebuilds arms from a tuned sidecar (deployed_budget_settings, deployed_basin_settings, audit_operating_points). Each field is the paper's builder:\n\ncomposed(problem::Symbol) -> Vector{ComposedSpec} — the composed score+LO roster (scale-multiple / outlier-half-width / natively delivered loss-width arms) of the composed miscal and budget evaluations;\nsolo(problem::Symbol; role::Symbol) -> Vector{SoloSpec} — the solo roster;\nbasin(problem::Symbol) -> Vector{ComposedSpec} — the basin roster (loss-width and outlier-half-width arms only; a scale multiple has no meaning on the basin axis).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.Roster"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetConfig","page":"Evaluation","text":"SampleBudgetConfig\n\nConfiguration for the sample-budget selection protocol.\n\nFields (the schedule and the LO are REQUIRED: a sample-budget experiment\n\nstates its protocol of record, e.g. a paper's samplebudgetprotocol.toml)\n\nsample_cap::Int — total minimal samples drawn per pair\nbatch_size::Int — selection (non-LO) checkpoint stride\nlo_every::Int   — LO checkpoint stride (after the first batch)\nlo_strategy::AbstractLocalOptimization — LO method\nbudget_unit::Symbol — budget axis: :models counts essential matrices inspected (Shekhovtsov's unit); :samples counts minimal subsets.\n\nCheckpoint schedule (both curves share one cumulative-sample x-axis)\n\nSelection (running-best, no LO): every batch_size — 100, 200, …, cap.\nLO (refit the running best): after the first batch, then the lo_every grid — {batch_size} ∪ {lo_every, 2·lo_every, …, cap} = 100, 500, 1000, ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetMethodSpec","page":"Evaluation","text":"SampleBudgetMethodSpec(name::Symbol, scoring)\n\nA scoring-method column: a label and the scoring it selects with, at its validated width (a Student-t + uniform marginal at its prior and outlier half-width). Its running best is refit by the config's LO at the config's native, else at the scoring's outlier half-width. name is opaque to the evaluator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetMethodSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetResults","page":"Evaluation","text":"SampleBudgetResults\n\nPer-pair error curves for each method column, plus LO-refined error at each LO checkpoint and the LO wall-clock accounting (see summarize_lo_timing).\n\nFields\n\nmethods::Vector{Symbol} — method labels (length M)\nsample_counts::Vector{Int} — cumulative-sample axis for batch curves (B)\nlo_counts::Vector{Int}     — cumulative-sample axis for LO checkpoints (L)\npair_ids::Vector{String}   — per-pair identifiers (P)\nerr_batch::Array{Float32,3} — (P, M, B) running-best imc [deg]\nbest_idx_batch::Array{Int32,3} — (P, M, B) pool index of running best\nerr_lo::Array{Float32,3} — (P, M, L) LO-refined imc [deg]\nlo_ns_total/lo_calls_total/lo_startup_ns::Vector{Int64} — per-method warm LO wall-clock [ns] / call count / cold first-call [ns] (JIT + one call)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleBudgetResults"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleSelector","page":"Evaluation","text":"SampleSelector\n\nA running-best selector of the sample-budget engine. It folds pool models into a running best (update!), reports that best's pose error at a checkpoint (checkpoint) and refits it through the shared refit layer (lo_error!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SampleSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ScoreGuarded","page":"Evaluation","text":"ScoreGuarded(inner::AbstractLocalOptimization)\n\nLO-RANSAC best-so-far monotonicity wrapper for IRLS-family arms. The inner LO runs unchanged, but the refined model is kept only if its SELECTION score (the same marginal that seeded it) does not drop below the seed's (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's score_guard). It preserves score monotonicity while the optimizer descends a different objective.\n\nIts RefitFamily and the external-refit bracket (external_workspace, prepare_external!, refine_external!) are inner's, as on VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's CountedLO and TerminalLO: a guarded external arm is routed, prepared and refined as the bare arm. The guard is score_guard on the native path, so an external refit is forwarded unguarded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ScoreGuarded"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ScoreSelector","page":"Evaluation","text":"ScoreSelector(name, scoring, width, ws, scores; lo = nothing, refinement_width = nothing)\n\nScoring-based selector: the running argmax of scoring's score (VisualGeometryToolkitCore score! on the scorer's workspace ws, per-point scratch scores) over the pool models; width is the native width scoring selects at (its outlier half-width, or its threshold). Its running best is refit by the config's LO (lo = nothing) at the config's native (nat_ref, else width), or by its own LO lo at refinement_width (else the config's nat_ref, else width) — the MSAC + truncated-refiner columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.ScoreSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SelectionWidth","page":"Evaluation","text":"SelectionWidth()\n\nThe arm's refit width IS its selection's tuned width, never tuned (see WidthSource).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SelectionWidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SoloSpec","page":"Evaluation","text":"SoloSpec(base, role, axis::WidthAxis, ctor)\n\nOne role (:selection | :refinement) validated in isolation at one width value on axis (see the block comment above); ctor(value) returns a pool scorer (selection) or an LO strategy (refinement).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.SoloSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.TunedWidth","page":"Evaluation","text":"TunedWidth()\n\nThe arm's own refit width, tuned at its selector's tuned selection width (see WidthSource).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.TunedWidth"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.WidthSource","page":"Evaluation","text":"WidthSource\n\nWhere a ComposedSpec's refit width comes from: TunedWidth or SelectionWidth (see the block comment above).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.WidthSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.WindowedGridMethod","page":"Evaluation","text":"WindowedGridMethod(label, scene_errors, scene_sigma2, native_grid, window, to_native,\n                   criterion)\n\nTunes on the standardized scale-multiple axis (accv §common-cutoff, eq. 28) instead of the raw native grid, with the reference scale estimated from the validation draw so it tracks the validation cardinality n — the protocol of the windowed_bootstrap tuning scheme.\n\nscene_errors[i] — precomputed (pairs × |native_grid|) post-LO error matrix on a fixed grid of native hyperparameter values (native_grid).\nscene_sigma2[i][j] — pair (i,j)'s noise variance σ̂² (e.g. gt_variance), used to form the cardinality-tied scale σ_med(n).\nto_native(scale_multiple, scale) -> native — the eq-(28) map for this scoring, a closure over the caller's native_map(axis; d_g) so this package stays decoupled from the scoring-spec types.\nwindow — the spec's ScaleMultipleWindow; criterion — its objective (no default: both are parameters of the tuning spec).\n\nPer trial, tune draws n val pairs, forms σ_med(n)=√median σ̂² over them, and takes the FREE ARGMIN of the val objective over every native_grid column inside the standardized window [to_native(lo, σ_med), to_native(hi, σ_med)] (the window's covered range at the drawn supplied scale — corrupting the scale slides the window, which is the miscalibration mechanism). evaluate reads the tuned column on the test pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.WindowedGridMethod"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.apply_lo_refits-Tuple{Type, AbstractVector{LoRefitSpec}, Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"apply_lo_refits(problem_type, specs::Vector{LoRefitSpec}, pairs;\n                scoring, solo_scoring = scoring,\n                operating_scale_multiple = OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE, on_fail = nothing)\n    -> Dict{String, Matrix{Float64}}\n\nRun every refit spec over pairs and return each spec's post-LO error matrix. scoring is the refit scoring TEMPLATE (rebuilt at each refit's own outlier half-width), solo_scoring the absolute-width (widths) columns' template. The run is threaded :greedy over the flat (pair × spec) product so neither a subsampled validation side nor the arms' very uneven per-spec cost leaves threads idle (see the comment in the body), with one PairCtx built per task at exactly that spec's bundle. pairs are light pool records (PoolPair): a refit never reads a residual matrix. on_fail = nothing ⇒ seed-error fallback (LO never worsens the running best); supply NaN for the ladder convention (failed refit ≠ success). Returns label => (P × T) imc matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.apply_lo_refits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.audit_operating_points-Tuple{Roster, AbstractString}","page":"Evaluation","text":"audit_operating_points(roster::Roster, path; problem = nothing) -> true\n\nAudit the tuning sidecar at path through its gate-enforced reader. A basin sidecar goes through deployed_basin_settings, a composed one through deployed_budget_settings; it throws on any clipped arm (a boundary argmin never reaches a consumer) and on any other artifact format, and returns true. MANDATORY before a figure or table consumes the sidecar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.audit_operating_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.batch_sample_counts-Tuple{SampleBudgetConfig}","page":"Evaluation","text":"The cumulative sample counts of the selection checkpoints: batch_size:batch_size:sample_cap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.batch_sample_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.bootstrap_trials-Tuple{BootstrapMethod, FlatProtocol, BudgetSampling, Int64}","page":"Evaluation","text":"bootstrap_trials(method, proto::FlatProtocol, budget::BudgetSampling, n;\n                 T = maa_threshold())\n    -> (trial_meds, trial_maas, trial_idxs)\n\nThe per-draw bootstrap: each of the budget.n_draws draws tunes the hyperparameter on n val pairs of proto (tune) and evaluates the tuned column on its test pairs (evaluate): the draw's median test error (a pair without a model counts as Inf) and its mAA@T (the eligibility rule, eligible_maa). Draw r uses budget_rng(budget, n, r).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.bootstrap_trials"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.build_pair_ctx-Tuple{Type, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}","page":"Evaluation","text":"build_pair_ctx(problem_type, pair::PoolPair; scoring = nothing, solo_scoring = scoring, bundle = false) -> PairCtx\n\nBuild the PairCtx of one pool pair, once. It holds the family's pool-frame problem (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's EssentialProblem(pair), FundMatProblem(pair) or HomographyProblem(pair)), Kc, GT pose and pool-frame points; plus the Schur bundle problem when bundle = true. problem_type is the pool's track (problem_type(pool.problem)). scoring is the refit scoring template (any scoring with an outlier half-width: the caller's — a paper's Student-t + uniform marginal at its prior); nothing is a context that only reads selections (no native refit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.build_pair_ctx"},{"category":"function","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.checkpoint","page":"Evaluation","text":"checkpoint(s::SampleSelector, geom::PairGeom, pair) -> (imc::Float32, best_idx::Int32)\n\nThe running best's imc pose error [deg] and pool index, recorded at a checkpoint; (NaN32, 0) before any model was folded in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.checkpoint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_cv-Tuple{Any, SpacedGrid, CoarseFine}","page":"Evaluation","text":"coarse_fine_cv(evalfn, coarse::SpacedGrid, scheme::CoarseFine; method_name, label, kwargs...) -> Dict\n\ncoarse_fine_cv_batched of one cell and a point-wise evalfn(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_cv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_cv_batched-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{Any, AbstractDict{K, <:SpacedGrid}, CoarseFine}} where K","page":"Evaluation","text":"coarse_fine_cv_batched(curvesfn, coarse::AbstractDict{K,<:SpacedGrid}, scheme::CoarseFine;\n                       method_name = Symbol, label = string, param_name = :x, context_tag = \"\")\n    -> Dict{K,Dict{Symbol,Any}}\n\ncoarse_fine_search of one scalar per cell, curvesfn(xs_of) -> Dict(k => [errors at x for x in xs_of[k]]). Per cell the record :coarse_spec, :x_star (coarse), :boundary_hit, :n_extensions, :curve_ys, :fine_spec, :fine_curve, :fine_idx, :fine_bracket, :x_star_fine (the tuned value) and the identity fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_cv_batched"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_search-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict, Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"coarse_fine_search(errorsfn, cells, scheme; label = string) -> Dict{K,NamedTuple}\n\nThe coarse-fine search, both stages: fine_search of coarse_search.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_fine_search"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_search-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{Any, AbstractDict{K, <:NamedTuple}, Union{CoarseFine, JointCoarseFine}}} where K","page":"Evaluation","text":"coarse_search(errorsfn, cells::AbstractDict{K,<:NamedTuple}, scheme; label = string)\n    -> Dict{K,NamedTuple}\n\nStage 1 of the coarse-fine search (module header): per search key k, cells[k] names each axis's coarse SpacedGrid (grid_spec of the spec's axis); the objective over their product, each axis extended on a side while the joint argmin lies within the spec's edge_margin of that face. scheme is a JointCoarseFine or a CoarseFine (one axis, named by the cells). Per key (; names, coarse, coarse_objective, n_extensions, coarse_argmin, boundary_hit, evaluated): the axis names, the final coarse grid specs, the objective over their product (an ndims = d array), the extension rounds (d × 2, lo and hi), the argmin and the per-axis boundary flags as NamedTuples, and every evaluated point => errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.coarse_search"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.compute_method_rows-Tuple{BootstrapMethod, BudgetSampling}","page":"Evaluation","text":"compute_method_rows(method, budget::BudgetSampling; protocol, sizes = budget.sizes)\n    -> (summary::Vector{NamedTuple}, trials::Vector{NamedTuple})\n\nPer-method, per-n evaluation loop over the validation budgets sizes (the schedule's own by default; a miscal evaluation supplies its one fixed budget): budget.n_draws draws per budget (bootstrap_trials), the BCa CIs resampled with resampling_rng(budget, n). Returns two row vectors in canonical DataFrame-friendly shape:\n\nsummary: one row per n carrying the BCa CIs and per-trial argmin diagnostics that were the columns of the per-cell summary DataFrame.\ntrials : one row per (n, trial) carrying the raw (trial_med, trial_mAA, trial_idx) so CIs can be replayed at any α downstream.\n\nprotocol is the val/test split (FlatProtocol). (The produce_or_load cache and the decoupled tune_protocol path were pruned 2026-09-21 with their only caller, evaluate_val.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.compute_method_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.consumed_axis-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLocalOptimization}","page":"Evaluation","text":"consumed_axis(lo) -> NativeAxis\n\nThe native axis refiner lo consumes, by its refit family: a native refiner its scoring outlier half-width (OutlierHalfwidth()), an external one its truncated loss's width (LossWidth()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.consumed_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.consumed_native-Tuple{Any, Union{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.LossWidth, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.OutlierHalfwidth}, Real}","page":"Evaluation","text":"consumed_native(lo, axis::NativeAxis, native::Real;\n                operating_scale_multiple = OPERATING_SCALE_MULTIPLE, d_g) -> Float64\n\nThe native width THIS refiner consumes (consumed_axis), given a selector grid value native on native axis axis. Same-axis is identity; cross-axis converts at the operating_scale_multiple crossover (VisualGeometryToolkitCore outlier_halfwidth_to_loss_width / loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth) for residual codimension d_g (codimension(problem_type): 1 for E/F, 2 for H).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.consumed_native"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_arm_settings-Tuple{Roster, AbstractString}","page":"Evaluation","text":"deployed_arm_settings(roster::Roster, path; problem = nothing) -> Vector{label => (lo, fixed_native)}\n\nEach pipeline's DEPLOYED configuration: the LO strategy at its tuned width and its fixed native. It is the (lo, ref) projection of deployed_budget_settings, under the same interior-argmin gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_arm_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_basin_settings-Tuple{Roster, AbstractString}","page":"Evaluation","text":"deployed_basin_settings(roster::Roster, path)\n    -> Vector{base => (spec::SoloSpec, width::Float64)}\n\nEach basin arm's tuned width from a basin tuning sidecar (mode = \"basin\"). The arms are rebuilt through roster.basin(problem) and repackaged as a refinement SoloSpec whose axis is the arm's parameterization, so tuning and test deliver the width identically via solo_refit_imc!. Refuses a non-basin sidecar. Same interior-argmin gate as the composed readers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_basin_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_budget_settings-Tuple{Roster, AbstractString}","page":"Evaluation","text":"deployed_budget_settings(roster::Roster, path; problem = nothing)\n    -> Vector{label => (lo, sel, ref, kind)}\n\nPer-arm tuned operating points of a composed tuning sidecar, rebuilt through the roster. The sidecar is read through VisualGeometryToolkitEval load_tuning_artifact, which refuses another format; each arm is rebuilt through roster.composed(problem): the LO instance (the tuned scale multiple baked in for scale-multiple arms), the SELECTION native and the REFINEMENT native (scale-multiple arms refit at the selection native — consensus-anchored; native parameterizations at their tuned value) and the arm's width parameterization kind — the roster's, the one source of an arm's kind (a sidecar's own kinds list is not read). problem defaults to the sidecar's own \"problem\".\n\nINTERIOR-ARGMIN INVARIANT: a HARD ERROR on any arm whose persisted boundary_hits is non-empty — expand the window on the flagged side and re-run the tuning (a SelectionWidth arm has no width of its own to clip: its list carries the selection's axis alone, and its recorded width must be its selection's, operating_width). A basin sidecar is refused (deployed_basin_settings reads it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_budget_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_setting-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Evaluation","text":"deployed_setting(path; context = path, allow_edge = false) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe gated reader of a coarse-fine tuning artifact (save_coarse_fine_artifact): the selected value of every axis, as a NamedTuple. HARD ERROR when the spec is not a coarse-fine one or the search hit its window's edge on any axis (enforce_probe_interior; allow_edge = true downgrades to a warning for FORENSIC reads only). A tuned setting is deployed through this, never from a literal.\n\nAlso reads the one-scalar layout written before the joint search (a CV \"record\" and \"x_star_fine\"; BlobBoards' half-width run of record): (; <param_name> = x_star_fine).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.deployed_setting"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.enforce_probe_interior-Tuple{Bool}","page":"Evaluation","text":"enforce_probe_interior(rec_or_flag; context, allow_edge = false)\n\nRead-side gate of the coarse-fine search: HARD ERROR when a record (or an explicit boundary_hit::Bool) is flagged — the coarse window clipped the optimum even after the extension budget, so the tuned value is suspect and must never be silently consumed. Re-run the search (it expands itself; if it still hits the budget, the objective has no interior optimum on any reachable window and the method/cell needs attention). allow_edge = true downgrades to a loud warn — FORENSIC reads only, never production. Mirrors the LO engine's _enforce_interior_argmin gate on the product-grid readers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.enforce_probe_interior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.essential_bearings-Tuple{PairCtx}","page":"Evaluation","text":"essential_bearings(ctx::PairCtx) -> (f₁, f₂)\n\nThe unit bearings of an E context's problem (what a cheirality vote reads). Read from ctx.ep, which already back-projected every cspond through the pool camera, rather than re-derived. Only an E context has bearings: on an F or H context ctx.ep holds pool pixels, so this refuses rather than hand 2-vectors to a ray test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.essential_bearings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate","page":"Evaluation","text":"evaluate(method::BootstrapMethod, test_pairs, params) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe test errors of method at the tuned params on test_pairs (the protocol's (scene, pair) indices).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate_sample_budget-Tuple{Type, AbstractVector{<:SampleBudgetMethodSpec}, AbstractVector{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix{Float32}}}","page":"Evaluation","text":"evaluate_sample_budget(problem_type, methods::Vector{<:SampleBudgetMethodSpec}, pairs, residuals;\n                       pair_ids, msac_specs::Vector{<:MsacSpec}, cfg::SampleBudgetConfig,\n                       sample_starts = nothing, refit_scoring = methods[1].scoring)\n    -> SampleBudgetResults\n\nRun the sample-budget protocol over the pool pairs pairs. The pairs are a flat vector of light pool records (PoolPair) with their residual matrices residuals (residuals[i] is pair i's; VisualGeometryToolkitEval load_pool_residuals, residual_matrix). A :samples budget (cfg.budget_unit) counts minimal samples through sample_starts[i], pair i's minimal-sample starts — VisualGeometryToolkitEval sample_starts(pool), the one reconstruction of sample boundaries; it is required then and ignored for a :models budget. Columns: the scoring methods, then one MSAC column per MsacSpec, then the oracle (last). Every native refit is seeded under refit_scoring (a scoring TEMPLATE, rebuilt at each refit's own outlier half-width) — the methods' own: they must share it. Parallel over pairs; per-method scoring workspaces preallocated per thread.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate_sample_budget"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate_sample_budget_pair!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Float32}, AbstractMatrix{Int32}, AbstractMatrix{Float32}, AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector{Int64}, Tuple, Type, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair, AbstractMatrix{Float32}, SampleBudgetConfig}","page":"Evaluation","text":"evaluate_sample_budget_pair!(out_err_batch, out_best_idx_batch, out_err_lo,\n                             out_lo_ns, out_lo_calls, selectors::Tuple,\n                             problem_type, pair, R, cfg; refit_scoring = nothing,\n                             sample_starts = nothing)\n\nThe per-pair worker of evaluate_sample_budget: walk one pair's pool and fill its slice of the results.\n\npair is the light pool record, R its residual matrix (numpoints × nummodels, Float32, the pool frame). sample_starts is the pair's minimal-sample starts (VisualGeometryToolkitEval sample_starts), required when the budget counts :samples. The selectors — a Tuple, so every per-model call is statically dispatched — are mutated too (reset per pair, then folded), so the problem type follows them. refit_scoring is the per-pair context's refit template — the one every refit's scoring is built from at its own native — so it must be the selectors' own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.evaluate_sample_budget_pair!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.fine_search-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{Any, AbstractDict{K, <:NamedTuple}, Union{CoarseFine, JointCoarseFine}}} where K","page":"Evaluation","text":"fine_search(errorsfn, coarse::AbstractDict{K,<:NamedTuple}, scheme) -> Dict{K,NamedTuple}\n\nStage 2 of the coarse-fine search: per key, each axis's fine spacing over the two coarse neighbours of the coarse argmin on that axis, their product evaluated once (no extension), its free argmin the selection. Each record of coarse_search gains fine (the fine grid specs), fine_objective and selected (a NamedTuple of axis values); evaluated gains the fine cells. A spec's incumbent is evaluated (one more call) and the record gains incumbent, incumbent_errors, gain (objective, p_gain) and kept_incumbent; selected is the incumbent when kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.fine_search"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.fine_stage-Tuple{Any, AbstractDict, CoarseFine}","page":"Evaluation","text":"fine_stage(evalfn, rec, scheme::CoarseFine) -> (; x_star, idx, spec, grid, curve, bracket)\n\nThe fine stage alone (fine_search) of a probe_curve record and a point-wise evalfn(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.fine_stage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.guarded_refit-Union{Tuple{G}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, G, Float64}} where {F, G}","page":"Evaluation","text":"guarded_refit(opt, score, on_fail; warn_label = nothing)\n    -> (err::Float64, lo_ns::Int64)\n\nThe seed-fallback + timing bracket.\n\nopt() -> Union{Nothing, M} — the bracketed optimizer call (data-in → result-out). Throws are caught; nothing signals bail / no consensus.\nscore(m::M) -> Float64 — GT scoring of the refined model, NOT timed. A non-finite score falls back to on_fail (a GT-free decision: the refit produced an unusable model).\non_fail policy is the caller's (refit_imc!'s fallback): budget/producer supply the seed's error (LO-RANSAC invariant — refinement never worsens the running best); the basin ladder supplies NaN (a failed refit did not travel).\nlo_ns spans exactly opt().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.guarded_refit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.is_tuned-Tuple{ComposedSpec}","page":"Evaluation","text":"Is arm's refit width tuned (a TunedWidth arm), rather than its selection's?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.is_tuned"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.live_row-Tuple{Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"live_row(runs; T) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe summary of one live_runs result. Its fields are med the finite median error [deg], maa the mAA@T under the eligibility rule (VisualGeometryToolkitEval eligible_maa: every pool pair is eligible, a run without a model scores 0; T defaults to its maa_threshold()), med_trials the median trials, med_wall_ms the median wall [ms], and the outcome counts (n_eligible, n_failed, n_skipped).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.live_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.live_runs-Tuple{Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLocalOptimization, AbstractVector{<:PairCtx}}","page":"Evaluation","text":"live_runs(scoring, lo, ctxs; problem_of, seed_of, config = RansacConfig(), warmup = true,\n          lo_of = nothing, on_improvement_of = nothing, score_guard = false,\n          cancelled = () -> false, finalize = nothing)\n    -> (; err, trials, wall_s)            # plus `extras` when `finalize !== nothing`\n\nRun one live production ransac per pair context ctxs[j], threaded over pairs. Each run uses the caller's problem of the pair, problem_of(ctxs[j].pair), the selection scoring, the LO strategy lo, adaptive termination under config. Each pair's stream is StableRNG(seed_of(j)), handed to ransac explicitly, so a run is independent of the thread schedule, identical across calls that share seed_of (seed_of should be a stable_seed). Per pair: err[j] the imc pose error [deg] of the final model, Inf when the run yielded no model (converged false) or the pair was cancelled; trials[j] the trials drawn; wall_s[j] the wall clock of the ransac call alone. warmup first runs one short solve on pair 1 (result discarded) so no bracket pays JIT.\n\nThe keywords below default so that the result is exactly the plain run's.\n\nlo_of(j) returns the LO strategy of pair j, overriding lo. The warmup uses lo_of(1) (an LO holds no recorded state).\non_improvement_of(j) returns the ransac improvement callback (on_improvement(trial, score, ws), fired when the incumbent improves). It is called with j = 0 once for the warmup (return a throwaway callback of the same type as for real pairs, writing to scratch storage, so the loop compiles once and the warmup records nothing) and with j = 1:n for the pairs.\nscore_guard is forwarded to ransac: per-candidate ScoreGuarded acceptance.\ncancelled() is polled before each pair; a cancelled pair is not run and keeps err = Inf, trials = 0, wall_s = NaN. Pairs are threaded, so a pair already started finishes. VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.cancelled is the kaimon-aware poll.\nfinalize(j, r) runs after pair j's ransac result r, on the pair's thread and outside its wall bracket; its values come back as extras::Vector{Any} (nothing for a pair not run). Without finalize the result has no extras field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.live_runs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_error!","page":"Evaluation","text":"lo_error!(ctx::PairCtx, s::SampleSelector, cfg) -> (imc_error::Float32, lo_ns::Int64)\n\nLO refit of the running best through the shared refit layer (refit_imc!). lo_ns is the bracketed optimizer wall-clock (0 when there is no model to refine).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_error!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_iteration_budget-Tuple{}","page":"Evaluation","text":"lo_iteration_budget() -> Int\n\nThe equal LO model-update budget of the evaluation: configs/default.toml [local_optimization] iteration_budget, so arms compare at equal cost. A paper with its own protocol supplies its budget explicitly instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_iteration_budget"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_sample_counts-Tuple{SampleBudgetConfig}","page":"Evaluation","text":"lo_sample_counts(cfg::SampleBudgetConfig) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe cumulative sample counts of the LO checkpoints: an early point after the FIRST batch, then the regular lo_every grid up to sample_cap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.lo_sample_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.miscal_evaluation_refit-Tuple{ComposedSpec, Dict{Symbol, Matrix{Int32}}, Any, Vector{Float64}, AbstractDict{Symbol}}","page":"Evaluation","text":"miscal_evaluation_refit(arm, idxs_of, tuned, zetas, grids; d_g, plugin_scale = nothing) -> LoRefitSpec\n\nThe miscalibration evaluation refit of one arm, one column per ζ in zetas. Column j seeds at the selection column nearest ζj·selection* and refits at the exactly-scaled refinement native (ζj·width* for the native parameterizations — both formed from the corrupted plug-in — while a scale multiple is consensus-anchored and NOT corrupted). tuned is the tuned point as NATIVE VALUES, (; selection, width), its width read through operating_width (a SelectionWidth arm: width is nothing or the selection, and column j refits at ζ_j·selection*, the selection's own corrupted native); grids supplies the arm's selection grid, which the seed columns are found in.\n\nplugin_scale (the consensus-anchoring ABLATION, scale-multiple arms only): the same operating point and seeds, the scale multiple's loss width delivered at the FIXED external plug-in scale ζj·`pluginscaleinstead of the consensus set's ŝ —withwidth(lo, fixedwidth(lo, (ζj·pluginscale)²))through the LoRefitSpecfixed_s2s` column. A native parameterization's width already is the corrupted plug-in; it is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.miscal_evaluation_refit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.model_imc-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.EssentialMat, PairGeom}","page":"Evaluation","text":"model_imc(model, geom::PairGeom) -> Float64\nmodel_imc(model, ctx::PairCtx)   -> Float64\n\nThe pose error [deg] of model against the pair's ground truth. By the model's type: a metric EssentialMat → max(Δr, Δt) of its best decomposition; a pool-frame FundMat → the same after the pool-frame calibration upgrade; a pool-frame HomographyMat → the optimistic decomposition ROTATION error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.model_imc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.n_batches-Tuple{SampleBudgetConfig}","page":"Evaluation","text":"Number of selection checkpoints of cfg: sample_cap ÷ batch_size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.n_batches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.n_los-Tuple{SampleBudgetConfig}","page":"Evaluation","text":"Number of LO checkpoints of cfg (see lo_sample_counts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.n_los"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.operating_width-Tuple{ComposedSpec, Real, Union{Nothing, Real}}","page":"Evaluation","text":"operating_width(arm::ComposedSpec, selection, tuned) -> Float64\n\nThe refit width of arm at the tuned selection width selection. By its WidthSource: a TunedWidth arm's tuned value tuned; a SelectionWidth arm's selection itself — tuned is nothing (the tuning stage, which has none) or a recorded width, which must equal selection exactly (a sidecar read back).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.operating_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.perturbed_seed_E-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Any, Any, Real}","page":"Evaluation","text":"perturbed_seed_E(rng, R_gt, t_gt, δ) -> EssentialMat\n\nThe δ-perturbed metric essential seed [t]ₓR of VgtkCore.Synthetic.perturbed_pose — the seed model every basin-style E refit starts from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.perturbed_seed_E"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.probe_curve-Tuple{Any, SpacedGrid, CoarseFine}","page":"Evaluation","text":"probe_curve(evalfn, coarse::SpacedGrid, scheme::CoarseFine; method_name, label, param_name = :x) -> Dict\n\nThe coarse stage alone (coarse_search) of one cell and a point-wise evalfn(x): the record of coarse_fine_cv_batched without its fine keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.probe_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.problem_type-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Evaluation","text":"problem_type(p::Symbol) -> Type\n\nThe RANSAC problem type of a two-view pool track: :E → EssentialProblem, :F → FundMatProblem, :H → HomographyProblem. The one map from a track's letter (a pool's problem) to its problem class — the value a PairCtx carries as problem_type; any other symbol is an ArgumentError. Inside a function, name the result P (a local problem_type would shadow this function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.problem_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_imc!-Tuple{Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.NativeRefit, Vararg{Any, 7}}","page":"Evaluation","text":"refit_imc!(ctx, family::RefitFamily, lo, seed, nat′, scoring, fallback, bundle, s2)\n    -> (imc::Float64, lo_ns::Int64)\n\nThe execution path of one RefitFamily (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's Holy trait: NativeRefit, ExternalRefit). refit_imc!(ctx, lo, seed, nat′; …) resolves the keywords and calls this with RefitFamily(lo). A new strategy plugs in through the contracts (locally_optimize!, or refine_external! with its external_workspace), never by a method here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_imc!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_imc!-Tuple{PairCtx, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLocalOptimization, Any, Float64}","page":"Evaluation","text":"refit_imc!(ctx::PairCtx, lo, seed, nat′::Float64;\n           scoring = ctx.scoring, on_fail = nothing, bundle::Bool = false,\n           s2 = nothing) -> (imc::Float64, lo_ns::Int64)\n\nRefine the seed model seed with strategy lo at ITS native parameter nat′ (see consumed_native) and return the refined model's imc error [deg] plus the bracketed optimizer wall-clock.\n\nscoring (NativeRefit): the scoring TEMPLATE the refit is seeded under, rebuilt at nat′ (with_width(scoring, OutlierHalfwidth(nat′))) — the context's refit template by default; ctx.solo_scoring for an absolute-width (solo) refit.\non_fail: value on optimizer bail / non-finite refit. Default = the seed's own imc (never-worsen); the ladder supplies NaN.\nbundle: route the native refit through ctx.bp (Schur reprojection bundle) instead of the pose-only ctx.ep.\ns2: an externally supplied squared scale — the one conduit for a non-consensus width:\nNativeRefit: the strategy is deployed at the absolute width with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, s2)) (its scale multiple at the scale √s2) — the role-isolated controls (solo CV, oracle-scale controls, the anchoring ablation). The seed sweep still runs; only the LO's width moves.\nExternalRefit: the fixed scale FixedS2(s2) replaces nat′ (by default the loss width √s2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_imc!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_model-Tuple{Any, Any, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.AbstractLocalOptimization, Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"refit_model(problem, seed, lo, scoring; workspace = nothing, s2 = nothing)\n    -> Union{Nothing, model}\n\nThe native (locally_optimize) refit of the refit layer, returning the refined MODEL — nothing when the strategy produced none. lo may be ScoreGuarded (the guard becomes score_guard). s2 deploys the same strategy at an absolute squared scale (with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, s2)); the scoring sweep is untouched, only the LO's width moves); nothing keeps the strategy's own width mode. The refit layer's imc rows wrap exactly this in guarded_refit; a caller that needs the refined model itself (a polish warm start) calls it directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_seed-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"refit_seed(ctx::PairCtx, model) -> seed\n\nHow a stored pool model becomes the seed of a refit in the pair's problem: an E pool's pool-frame fundamental is upgraded to the metric essential Kc₂ᵀ·F·Kc₁; F and H pool models seed as stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.refit_seed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.rigid_invariance_errors-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Evaluation","text":"rigid_invariance_errors(poses; targets, units = sort!(collect(keys(poses))), min_obs = 3)\n    -> (; translation::Matrix{Float64}, rotation::Matrix{Float64}, units, pairs)\n\nThe invariance errors of rigidly mounted targets. poses[u][m] is target m's estimated pose in unit u (a value with fields R, the rotation, and t, the translation: target → camera, x_cam = R·X + t, e.g. an Extrinsics3); a target absent from poses[u] was not observed there. targets lists every target that could be observed; pairs are its sorted pairs (m, n), m < n.\n\nFor each pair and each unit observing both targets, the relative pose T_m⁻¹ T_n is compared with a LEAVE-ONE-OUT reference over the other units observing that pair: the rotation MEDOID (the observation minimising summed geodesic distance to the others — robust to the two-fold flips a mean would be dragged by) and the componentwise MEDIAN translation. Leave-one-out is not optional: a reference containing the scored observation scores it zero by construction and rewards observing each pair in fewer units.\n\nReturns length(units) × length(pairs) matrices: translation (the translation's distance from the reference, in the poses' units) and rotation (geodesic angle, degrees). An entry is Inf where the pair was not observed in that unit, or was observed in fewer than max(min_obs, 3) units (no reference) — so under eligible_maa (the mAA rule: a non-finite error scores 0 and stays in the denominator) a missed pair counts as a failure, and eligible_maa(e, isfinite.(e)) is the accuracy over what was observed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.rigid_invariance_errors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.save_coarse_fine_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString, TuningSpec, NamedTuple}","page":"Evaluation","text":"save_coarse_fine_artifact(path, spec::TuningSpec, rec; unit, code, data...) -> path\n\nPersist one search record rec (coarse_fine_search, one key's NamedTuple) under its coarse_fine / joint_coarse_fine spec as a tuning artifact (save_tuning_artifact): the grids coarse|<axis> and fine|<axis>, and\n\naxes              the axis names, the search's order\nselected          axis => the tuned value (the fine argmin)\ncoarse_argmin     axis => the coarse argmin\nboundary_hit      axis => the coarse argmin within the margin of a face\n                  after the extension budget (refused by the reader)\nn_extensions      axis => [lo rounds, hi rounds]\ncoarse_objective, fine_objective   the objective over each product grid\ncells             every evaluated cell, one row per cell (columns: axes)\nunit_statistics   per cell, Tuning `unit_statistics` of its errors — when\n                  every cell's errors are a `units × items` matrix\nincumbent, kept_incumbent, gain, incumbent_unit_statistics\n                  with a declared incumbent (`selected` is it when kept)\n\ndata are the caller's further keys (the bootstrap of the selection, say).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.save_coarse_fine_artifact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.solo_refit_imc!-Tuple{Any, SoloSpec, Any, Float64, Float64}","page":"Evaluation","text":"solo_refit_imc!(ctx, spec::SoloSpec, seed_E, width, selection_halfwidth; on_fail = NaN)\n    -> (imc°, lo_ns)\n\nThe width-delivery dispatch of a :refinement solo evaluation, one mechanical map from spec.axis to the refit call, shared by the basin tuning's refits and the ladder's solo mode so tuning and test deliver the width identically. The seed is swept under the context's solo template (ctx.solo_scoring):\n\nLossWidth() — the absolute loss width width: a native refiner's strategy runs at it (with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, width²)), its seed swept at selection_halfwidth — scoring only, never a width), an external refiner refines at it;\nOutlierHalfwidth() — the LO at its own native nat′ = width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.solo_refit_imc!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.stream_selections-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Function, Type{T}, Type, Any, AbstractVector{<:Pair}, Any, Any, Int64}} where T","page":"Evaluation","text":"stream_selections(refits_of, T, problem_type, loaders, grids, scoring, backend, batch;\n                  solo_scoring = scoring)\n    -> (M::Dict{label, Vector{Matrix{T}}}, s2_by_scene, np_per)\n\nThe scene stream of every selection+LO experiment. loaders[i]() returns scene i's (pool, residuals) (VisualGeometryToolkitEval scene_loaders(dir, scenes; residuals = true)). Per scene: stage each batch of pairs once per staging kind (VisualGeometryToolkitCore stage_selection on backend, shared by the grids whose scorings declare the same selection_staging), evaluate every selection grid of grids (key => (widths, scoring): the scoring's native widths and the scoring itself — VisualGeometryToolkitCore select_on_grid!) on it, then one apply_lo_refits call over refits_of(idxs_of, scene_index) (idxs_of[key] = that grid's per-(pair, column) selections) on the light records. M[label] holds each refit's per-scene matrix in element type T; s2_by_scene the records' σ̂², np_per the pairs per scene. scoring and solo_scoring are the refits' scoring templates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.stream_selections"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.summarize_lo_timing-Tuple{SampleBudgetResults}","page":"Evaluation","text":"summarize_lo_timing(res::SampleBudgetResults) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nPer-method wall-clock summary: mean warm LO call [µs] (Render's lo_us_per_call), total warm calls, and the cold startup cost [ms]. Per-call numbers are measured in-situ under the parallel run, so they include thread contention (run single-threaded for isolated per-call cost).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.summarize_lo_timing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.tune","page":"Evaluation","text":"tune(rng, method::BootstrapMethod, val_pairs, n) -> params\n\nTune method on n validation pairs drawn by rng from val_pairs (the protocol's (scene, pair) indices); params is method-specific and is handed back to evaluate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.tune"},{"category":"function","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.update!","page":"Evaluation","text":"update!(s::SampleSelector, geom::PairGeom, pair, R, j, col_j, n)\n\nFold pool model j (its residual column R[1:n, j], minimal sample col_j) into the selector's running best.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.width_delivery-Tuple{VisualGeometryToolkitCore.ScaleMultiple, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Evaluation","text":"width_delivery(P::WidthAxis, lo, value, n) -> NamedTuple\n\nHow a width value reaches the refit of lo over n columns, as LoRefitSpec keywords. A scale multiple is baked into the LO object; an outlier half-width is the refit's native (natives); a loss width is an absolute width (widths), the solo delivery bitwise whatever the refiner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.width_delivery"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.window_columns-Tuple{ScaleMultipleWindow, AbstractVector{<:Real}, Any, Real}","page":"Evaluation","text":"window_columns(window::ScaleMultipleWindow, grid, tonat, scale) -> Vector{Int}\n\nColumn indices of the native grid inside the standardized window [tonat(window.lo, scale), tonat(window.hi, scale)] — the candidate set of every windowed free argmin (the bootstrap tuner here, the miscalibration calibration tune of LocalOptimizationDoneRight). Falls back to the EDGE CLAMP (the grid boundary the window crossed) when the window slid entirely off the grid (extreme miscalibration on an unwidened grid); never a snapped candidate set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.window_columns"},{"category":"method","location":"api/evaluation/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation._summarize_trials-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Int64}","page":"Evaluation","text":"_summarize_trials(rng, meds, maas, idxs, T) -> NamedTuple\n\nBCa-bootstrap mean/std + 95% CI per criterion, plus per-trial argmin diagnostics (idx_mean, idx_std, edge_frac; NaN when idxs is empty or T == 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation._summarize_trials"},{"category":"section","location":"api/paper_projects/#API:-PaperProjects","page":"PaperProjects","text":"The paper-project check and template. See Paper projects.","title":"API: PaperProjects"},{"category":"section","location":"api/paper_projects/#Public-names","page":"PaperProjects","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/paper_projects/#Internals","page":"PaperProjects","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects","page":"PaperProjects","text":"PaperProjects\n\nThe paper-project conventions: paper_project_violations (the one check every paper project's suite calls) and new_paper_project (the template, on DrWatson's initialize_project). The conventions are stated on the documentation page Paper projects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PAPER_PROJECT_CHECKS","page":"PaperProjects","text":"The checks paper_project_violations runs by default (:contract is opt-in).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PAPER_PROJECT_CHECKS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PAPER_PROJECT_TEMPLATE","page":"PaperProjects","text":"PAPER_PROJECT_TEMPLATE\n\nThe folder template new_paper_project hands DrWatson's initialize_project: configs/, scripts/, src/, test/, paper/figures/ (for the default paper), data/{exp_raw,exp_pro,sims} (the inputs, the results, the smoke runs — Experiments data_root()/results_root()) and plots/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PAPER_PROJECT_TEMPLATE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PaperProjectViolation","page":"PaperProjects","text":"PaperProjectViolation(check, rule, file, line, message)\n\nOne breach of the paper-project conventions: the check it belongs to (:layout, :data_policy, :reproduce, :configs, :provenance, :catalog, :contract, :allow), the rule (\"L1\", \"D2\", \"missing_asset\", \"C2\", …; see paper_project_violations), the project-relative file and line (0 when not line-specific) and a human-readable message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.PaperProjectViolation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.new_paper_project-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PaperProjects","text":"new_paper_project(path; name = basename(path), papers = [\"paper/main.tex\"],\n                  authors = nothing, git = true, force = false) -> String\n\nCreate a paper project at path that follows the stack's paper-project conventions: DrWatson's initialize_project with PAPER_PROJECT_TEMPLATE (plus each paper's figures/ directory), then\n\nProject.toml (DrWatson, VisualGeometryToolkitEval with compat \"1\", StableRNGs, TOML, Test), README.md, CLAUDE.md (the conventions pointer), .gitignore (the data bindings, local plots, LaTeX products; the Manifest is tracked);\nREPRODUCE.md with the asset-index table, configs/example.toml, scripts/example_stage.jl (stage runner, config of record, run key, provenance; it writes a figure, its caption and a macro file), src/<name>.jl (the prelude), test/runtests.jl (calls paper_project_violations);\nthe papers (the first reads the example's assets), Artifacts.toml and the data catalog stub configs/data_artifacts.toml, data/README.md.\n\ninitialize_project activates the new project and adds DrWatson to it; the previously active project is restored afterwards. git = false for a project inside an existing repository (accv2026 lives in LocalOptimizationDoneRight). Then: Pkg.instantiate() in the project, run scripts/example_stage.jl, and test/runtests.jl reports zero violations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.new_paper_project"},{"category":"method","location":"api/paper_projects/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.paper_project_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PaperProjects","text":"paper_project_violations(dir; papers = [\"paper/main.tex\"], index = \"REPRODUCE.md\",\n                         checks = PAPER_PROJECT_CHECKS, scripts = \"scripts\",\n                         configs = \"configs\", catalog = \"configs/data_artifacts.toml\",\n                         catalog_kinds = String[], data_policy = (;), reproduce = (;),\n                         allow = DataPolicyAllow[]) -> Vector{PaperProjectViolation}\n\nEvery breach of the paper-project conventions in project dir, empty when compliant — the one call a paper project's suite asserts empty. papers (the top-level tex files) and index are relative to dir; scripts, configs and catalog name the project's directories and its DataArtifacts catalog.\n\nThe checks (checks selects; PAPER_PROJECT_CHECKS by default, :contract opt-in) and their rules:\n\n:layout — L1 a required path is missing; L2 Project.toml lacks DrWatson or VisualGeometryToolkitEval; L3 git does not ignore data/exp_raw and data/exp_pro; L4 test/runtests.jl does not call this check.\n:data_policy — Experiments data_policy_violations (D1 machine paths, D2 writes into the input tree, D3 unkeyed outputs, D4 artifact\"…\" in code); data_policy forwards its keywords (dirs, exts).\n:reproduce — Reproduce reproduce_index_violations (rule = its kind); reproduce forwards its keywords (search_dirs for a paper built outside its own directory, check_literals, ignore_macros, …).\n:configs — C1 a producing script reads no config of record; C2 it holds a data constant (a literal seed, name list, numeric table or range, data path); C3 a script names a config under configs/ that does not exist.\n:provenance — P1 a producing script (with what it includes and launches) writes no provenance through the shared writer; P2 it keys no output by a run key; S1 a generator of the index has no stage runner.\n:catalog — A1 DataArtifacts catalog_violations, once the catalog or Artifacts.toml binds an artifact (catalog_kinds are the project's own kinds).\n:contract — Experiments contract_violations of every stage file.\n\nA producing script is a .jl under scripts/ that runs stages, declares an output (reproduce: writes), is a generator of the index or calls a result writer. Opt out of one rule where the constant is justified with # paper-project: allow <RULE> <reason> (on the line or the line above for C2/C3; anywhere in the file for C1, P1, P2, S1); a reasonless opt-out is X1. Deferred violations of any rule go in allow (DataPolicyAllow(rule, file, match), match a substring of the violation's text); an entry matching nothing is reported STALE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects.paper_project_violations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#API:-Pools","page":"Pools","text":"Scoring-pool input and output.","title":"API: Pools"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Public-names","page":"Pools","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/pools/#Internals","page":"Pools","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools","page":"Pools","text":"Pools\n\nScoring-pool input and output. A pool (ScoringPool) holds VisualGeometryToolkitCore PoolPair records; its residual matrices (PoolResiduals) are bound to it by the content key pool_key. The module has one writer and one reader of each (save_pool, load_pool), the per-scene loaders (scene_loaders), the scene-pool producer (write_scene_pool), the perspective-n-point (PnP) pool file (save_pnp_pool) and the minimal-sample boundaries of a pool (sample_starts).\n\nA scoring pool is stored in NormalizedImageFrame (frame) and a PnP pool in ImageFrame. Nothing here converts a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.GT_CONVENTION","page":"Pools","text":"The ground-truth pose convention every pool declares (gt_convention): the extrinsic relative pose x₂ = R·x₁ + t, source → target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.GT_CONVENTION"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.PoolResiduals","page":"Pools","text":"PoolResiduals\n\nThe residual matrices of a pool, bound to its light file by pool_key.\n\nFields:\n\npool_key::String: the pool_key of the pool they belong to.\nkind::Symbol: the residual they hold — :sampson (the plane Sampson distance of the pool-frame model; F and H pools) or :tangent_sampson (the tangent Sampson of the metric essential on the pool-frame E problem; E pools).\nmatrices::Vector{Matrix{Float32}}: one (num_points, num_models) matrix per held pair, |r| in NormalizedImageFrame units.\npairs::Vector{Int}: the pool indices of the held pairs, in the order of matrices — every pair (1:n) unless the residuals were read for a subset (load_pool_residuals(path, pool; pairs)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.PoolResiduals"},{"category":"type","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.ScoringPool","page":"Pools","text":"ScoringPool{M}\n\nOne scene's scoring pool in memory: the light records, without their residual matrices (PoolResiduals).\n\nFields:\n\npairs::Vector{PoolPair{M}}: the records (VisualGeometryToolkitCore PoolPair), in NormalizedImageFrame — frame(pool) === NormalizedImageFrame.\nproblem::Symbol: :E, :F or :H (E and F pools store FundMat, H pools HomographyMat).\nscene::String: the scene the file holds.\nscene_names::Vector{String}: each pair's source-scene label (a raw reader's pair_scene, else scene).\npool_key::String: pool_key(pairs), computed at construction.\nstamps::NamedTuple: gt_convention, pool_rng, julia_version, total_samples, total_solutions, mean_solutions_per_sample, provenance (a Dict{String,Any} of plain values).\nScoringPool(pairs, problem, scene; scene_names, stamps)\n\nbuilds one from records and computes its key; the model class must be the problem's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.ScoringPool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.derive_sample_starts-Tuple{ScoringPool, Any}","page":"Pools","text":"derive_sample_starts(pool::ScoringPool, stream; max_trials = 10^7) -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\nThe exact sample_starts of pool by REPLAYING each pair's seeded draw stream: stream(i) returns a zero-argument function yielding the next minimal sample (an AbstractVector{Int}, the sampler's own order) that pair i's generator drew, every trial included. For each run of identical columns the replay counts the draws of that sample before the next stored sample is drawn; the last run of a pair (nothing follows it in the stream) takes its structural count. Every pair's result must be admissible for the record and the total must equal the total_samples stamp, or an ArgumentError names what failed. Pairs are replayed under Threads.@threads.\n\nOnly the pool's own generator knows its stream; the caller supplies it (seed, sampler and stream index).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.derive_sample_starts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.export_pixel_inputs-Tuple{AbstractString, ScoringPool, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Pools","text":"export_pixel_inputs(path, pool, idx; scene = pool.scene, created = string(Dates.now())) -> path\n\nWrite the raw-pixel inputs of the pairs idx (strictly increasing pool indices) of pool to the JLD2 file path, in the layout the external estimators' workers read: per pair matches (an N×4 [x₁ y₁ x₂ y₂] matrix of pixel correspondences, back through the record's own maps, VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.pixel_csponds), the stored Lowe ratios snn, the pixel calibrations K1 and K2 and the ground-truth R_gt and t_gt; plus scene, n_pairs, pair_indices, pool_size, the pool_key the slice was read from, a provenance string and created. The parent directory is created and an existing file is overwritten. A pair without stored Lowe ratios (snn === nothing) is an ArgumentError naming it — the external baselines need them — and nothing is written; a pair without correspondences exports a 0×4 matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.export_pixel_inputs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.frame-Tuple{ScoringPool}","page":"Pools","text":"frame(pool::ScoringPool) -> Type{NormalizedImageFrame}\n\nThe frame a pool's data is stored in: NormalizedImageFrame (image centre, half diagonal, corners at unit radius), per view through each record's maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.frame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pnp_pool-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Pools","text":"load_pnp_pool(path) -> (; pools, misses, score)\n\nThe reader of a PnP pool file (save_pnp_pool is the format): refuses another pnp_pool_format and a frame other than ImageFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pnp_pool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pool-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Pools","text":"load_pool(path) -> ScoringPool\n\nThe reader of a light pool file (save_pool is the format). Refuses, with an ArgumentError naming the file: another pool_format (an older pool is migrated, never read: the error says how — migrate_pool for a format-4 file), missing or unknown keys, a frame other than NormalizedImageFrame, a ground-truth convention other than GT_CONVENTION, a model class that is not the problem's, and a stored pool_key that the loaded pairs do not reproduce.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pool_residuals-Tuple{AbstractString, ScoringPool}","page":"Pools","text":"load_pool_residuals(path, pool::ScoringPool; pairs = nothing) -> PoolResiduals\n\nThe reader of a residuals file (save_pool_residuals is the format), checked against the light pool it serves: refused (an ArgumentError naming the file) unless its pool_key is the pool's — the error names both keys — and it holds one matrix per pair, each (num_points, num_models) of that pair.\n\npairs (pool indices) reads only those pairs' matrices, sliced from the file without reading the rest; residual_matrix still takes pool indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_pool_residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_sample_starts-Tuple{AbstractString, ScoringPool}","page":"Pools","text":"load_sample_starts(path, pool::ScoringPool) -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\nThe reader of a sample-starts file (save_sample_starts is the format), checked against pool: refused (an ArgumentError naming the file) unless its pool_key is the pool's, every pair's starts are admissible for its record and their total is the total_samples stamp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.load_sample_starts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.migrate_pool-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Pools","text":"migrate_pool(path; out, dry_run = false) -> NamedTuple\n\nCarry a format-4 light pool file over to format 5, whose pair key for the destination map is destination_map. The format-5 file goes to joinpath(out, basename(path)); out must be another directory than the original's, which is never touched. The pool's residuals file (residuals_path), when present, is copied beside it unchanged: it is bound to the pool by pool_key, which hashes no map, so it stays valid. A sample-starts file (save_sample_starts) is NOT copied: it is derived data kept in a writable tree, never beside the pool, so the pool's path does not name it; it is bound by the same unchanged pool_key and reads against the migrated pool where it is. Both destinations, the pool's and its residuals', are checked before anything is written: an existing one is an ArgumentError, and a migration that fails leaves out as it was: a residuals file already renamed into it is removed, and so is an out the migration created.\n\nThe migration is verified before anything is written to out: the format-5 file, written through save_pool to a staging directory beside out (a dry run's: a temporary one), must read back through load_pool's checks, hold the same keys, and store every value of the original bit for bit; only then are the residuals copy and the pool renamed into out, the pool last. With dry_run nothing is written. A file of another format is refused with an ArgumentError: a format-5 one needs nothing, an older one is converted first (see load_pool's refusal). Returns (; path, residuals, n_pairs), the output paths nothing in a dry run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.migrate_pool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.pool_key-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Pools.PoolPair}}","page":"Pools","text":"pool_key(pairs) -> String\n\nThe content key of a pool: the lower-case hex SHA-256 of, for each pair in order,\n\nn = num_points(pair) and m = num_models(pair), each an Int64;\nthe csponds as the stored (4, n) Float64 matrix (rows x₁ y₁ x₂ y₂, column-major);\nthe models, each its 9 Float64 entries column-major;\nsize(sample_indices, 1) as an Int64, then sample_indices (Int64, column-major);\n\nevery number little-endian. These are exactly the quantities that index a residual matrix's rows and columns, so a residuals file written for other models, another pair order or other correspondences carries another key and is refused (load_pool_residuals). The residuals themselves are never read to compute it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.pool_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.residual_matrix-Tuple{PoolResiduals, Integer}","page":"Pools","text":"residual_matrix(res::PoolResiduals, i) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nPool pair i's residual matrix, (num_points, num_models). i is the pair's index in its POOL, also for residuals read for a subset; a pair that was not read is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.residual_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.residuals_path-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Pools","text":"residuals_path(path) -> String\n\nThe residuals file of the light pool file path: <scene>.jld2 → <scene>.residuals.jld2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.residuals_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.sample_ids-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"Pools","text":"sample_ids(starts, m) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe minimal-sample number (1, 2, …) of each of a pair's m models, from that pair's sample_starts starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.sample_ids"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.sample_starts-Tuple{ScoringPool}","page":"Pools","text":"sample_starts(pool::ScoringPool; derived = nothing) -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\nThe minimal-sample boundaries of every pair of pool: starts[i] holds the first model column of each minimal sample of pair i, in draw order, so pair i's sample s owns columns starts[i][s]:(starts[i][s+1] - 1) (the last sample runs to num_models). sample_ids turns them into a per-model sample number. Every count of minimal samples in the stack goes through this function.\n\nSibling solutions of one draw share a sample_indices column, and a REPEATED draw of the same sample stores the same column again, adjacent when no other sample was accepted in between. A run of identical columns therefore holds r draws, each storing the same block of siblings (a solve is deterministic): the record admits every r whose block repeats. Without derived, each run takes its largest admissible r, and the result is CERTIFIED: its total must equal the pool's total_samples stamp. Since every run's choice is an upper bound on its true draw count, equal totals make every run exact; a mismatch (a solve that returned a duplicated root) is an ArgumentError.\n\nderived is the path of a sample-starts file of this pool (derive_sample_starts, save_sample_starts), read and checked by load_sample_starts instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.sample_starts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pnp_pool-Tuple{AbstractString, Any, Any}","page":"Pools","text":"save_pnp_pool(path, pools, misses; score) -> path\n\nThe writer of a PnP pool file (artifact kind :pnp_pool, format 2), JLD2, plain data:\n\nkey meaning\npnp_pool_format 2\nframe \"ImageFrame\" — the 2-D observations are pixels\npools per-query plain records: name, camera_model, width, height, camera_params, u (n×2 px), X (n×3), snn, p3d, nobs [+ gt_R, gt_t, gt_variance (px², NaN: untrusted)]\nmisses the listed queries with no pool\nscore what snn holds: \"snn\" (raw Lowe ratio), \"one_minus_lg\" or \"none\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pnp_pool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pool-Union{Tuple{M}, Tuple{AbstractString, ScoringPool{M}}} where M","page":"Pools","text":"save_pool(path, pool::ScoringPool) -> path\n\nThe writer of a light pool file (artifact kind :pool, format 5). The file is JLD2 and holds PLAIN data only — no VisualGeometryToolkitCore type:\n\nkey type meaning\npool_format Int 5\nproblem String \"E\", \"F\" or \"H\"\nscene String the scene the file holds\nmodel_class String \"FundMat\" (E, F) or \"HomographyMat\" (H)\nframe String \"NormalizedImageFrame\"\ngt_convention String \"extrinsic\" (x₂ = R·x₁ + t, source → target)\npool_key String pool_key of the pairs (hex SHA-256)\nscene_names Vector{String} per-pair source-scene label\npool_rng String RNG algorithm of the pool streams (\"LehmerRNG\", the type of StableRNGs' StableRNG)\njulia_version String the Julia that generated the pool\ntotal_samples, total_solutions Int accepted minimal samples and real solutions over the scene\nmean_solutions_per_sample Float64 their ratio (0 when no sample)\nprovenance Dict{String,Any} the generating run's record (plain values)\npairs Vector of NamedTuples one per pair, below\n\nEach pair is a NamedTuple with exactly these fields (n correspondences, m models, s the minimal sample size), every point datum and model in NormalizedImageFrame:\n\nfield type meaning\ncsponds Matrix{Float64} (4, n) rows x₁ y₁ x₂ y₂\nK1, K2 SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9} PIXEL calibrations\nR_gt SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9} GT rotation, source → target\nt_gt SVector{3,Float64} GT translation (unnormalized)\nmodels Vector{SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}} (m) pool-frame model matrices of class model_class\nsample_indices Matrix{Int} (s, m) 1-based minimal sample of each model\nerrors_r, errors_t Vector{Float64} (m) rotation / translation error [deg] vs GT\ngt_inliers BitVector (n) GT inlier mask\ngt_variance Float64 σ̂² in pool units² (NaN: none)\nsource_map, destination_map SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9} pixel → NormalizedImageFrame affine maps (last row [0 0 1])\nsnn Vector{Float32} (n) or nothing per-correspondence SNN the hypotheses were sampled by\n\nThe residual matrices are a separate file (save_pool_residuals).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pool"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pool_residuals-Tuple{AbstractString, PoolResiduals}","page":"Pools","text":"save_pool_residuals(path, res::PoolResiduals) -> path\n\nThe writer of a residuals file (artifact kind :pool_residuals, format 1), JLD2, PLAIN data only:\n\nkey type meaning\npool_residuals_format Int 1\npool_key String the pool_key of the light file it belongs to\nresidual_kind String \"sampson\" (F, H) or \"tangent_sampson\" (E)\nresiduals Vector{Matrix{Float32}} per pair, (n, m): `\n\nBy convention the file of <scene>.jld2 is <scene>.residuals.jld2 (residuals_path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_pool_residuals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_sample_starts-Tuple{AbstractString, ScoringPool, AbstractVector}","page":"Pools","text":"save_sample_starts(path, pool::ScoringPool, starts; derivation, provenance = Dict()) -> path\n\nThe writer of a sample-starts file (artifact kind :pool_samples, format 1), JLD2, plain data: pool_key (the pool it belongs to), scene, total_samples, starts (Vector{Vector{Int}}, per pair, as sample_starts returns them), derivation (how they were obtained, e.g. the replayed stream) and provenance. The starts are checked admissible and certified against the stamp before writing. It is DERIVED data: it lives in a writable tree, never beside the pool it describes (pool trees are inputs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.save_sample_starts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.scene_loaders-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Pools","text":"scene_loaders(pool_dir, scenes; residuals = false) -> Vector{Function}\n\nZero-argument scene loaders over <pool_dir>/<scene>.jld2 — the loader constructor shared by every streaming driver. Each returns every pair of its scene (a subset is a cross-validation's units, Tuning validation_units / test_units, never a prefix): the ScoringPool, or with residuals = true the tuple (pool, residuals::PoolResiduals) read from <scene>.residuals.jld2 and checked against it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.scene_loaders"},{"category":"function","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.synthetic_e_pool_pair","page":"Pools","text":"synthetic_e_pool_pair(rng = MersenneTwister(7); n = 30, sibling_counts = vcat([2, 2], fill(1, 23)),\n                      noise = 0.0, δ_rot = 0.1, t_jitter = 0.05, gt_variance = 1.0e-4)\n    -> (pair::PoolPair{FundMat{Float64}}, residuals::Matrix{Float32})\n\nA calibrated two-view pool pair in the pool frame (K = I, identity maps): n correspondences of points 3–5 units ahead under the fixed GT motion (rotation 0.15 rad about y, unit translation along (0.8, 0.1, 0.3)), target coordinates jittered by noise·N(0, 1) when noise > 0. One minimal sample (5 indices) per entry of sibling_counts, carrying that many sibling models: each the GT rotated by U(−δrot/2, δrot/2) about z, the translation jittered by t_jitter·N(0, I₃) and renormalized. Every model's optimistic (Δr, Δt), all-inlier GT and the stated gt_variance; the residual matrix holds every model's Sampson distances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.synthetic_e_pool_pair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/pools/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.write_scene_pool-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Pools","text":"write_scene_pool(path, raws, build; problem, residual_kind, seed,\n                 skip_existing = true, provenance = Dict{String,Any}(),\n                 scene = <basename of path>) -> path | nothing\n\nBuild one scene's pool from its raw pairs raws (VisualGeometryToolkitCore StereoRawPairs, in pixels) and write it: the light file path (save_pool) and its residuals (save_pool_residuals at residuals_path(path)).\n\nbuild(rng, raw) builds one pair and returns either nothing — the pair is refused (e.g. fewer correspondences than the problem's minimal sample) and dropped — or a NamedTuple (; pair::PoolPair, residuals::Matrix{Float32}, samples::Integer, solutions::Integer): the record, its (num_points, num_models) residual matrix, and the accepted minimal samples and real solutions its draw counted. Pair i of raws draws from its OWN stream POOL_RNG(stable_seed(seed, i)) — keyed by its index in raws, not by the thread — so the pool is a pure function of (seed, raws), independent of thread count, scheduling and Julia version. Pairs are built under Threads.@threads.\n\nproblem (:E, :F, :H) and residual_kind (:sampson | :tangent_sampson, what build's matrices hold) are stamped into the files, with the scene, the per-pair pair_scene labels (else scene), the sample and solution totals, pool_rng, the Julia version and the caller's provenance. With skip_existing (the default: a pool is hours of compute) a scene whose two files both exist is skipped. Returns path, or nothing when no pair survives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools.write_scene_pool"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This page walks through the two things most users do first: summarize the pose errors of an evaluation, and tune a hyperparameter by cross-validation. The examples run without any data.\n\nEach part of the package is a submodule, and its names are imported from it:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats: finite_median, eligible_maa, bootstrap_ci, stable_seed\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning: tuning_spec, grid_spec, grid\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation: coarse_fine_cv\nusing Random: Xoshiro\nusing TOML","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Summarizing-pose-errors","page":"Getting started","text":"An evaluation produces one pose error per image pair, in degrees. A pair where the estimator returned no model has the error NaN.\n\nerrors = [0.4, 1.2, NaN, 0.8, 3.5, 0.6, 2.1]\nfinite_median(errors)\n1.0\n\nfinite_median drops the non-finite errors before it takes the median. The mean average accuracy (mAA) instead counts a failed pair as zero accuracy, because the pair was eligible to run:\n\neligible_maa(errors; T = 10.0)\n0.7342857142857142\n\nbootstrap_ci gives a bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap interval of any statistic. The random number generator (RNG) seeds the resampling, so the interval is reproducible:\n\nbootstrap_ci(finite_median, Xoshiro(1), errors)\n(0.4, 2.1)\n\nEvery random sample of the stack is seeded by stable_seed, which derives a seed from a spec's seed and a name. Adding or removing another scene therefore leaves a scene's sample unchanged:\n\nstable_seed(2026, \"scene_a\") == stable_seed(2026, \"scene_a\")\ntrue","title":"Summarizing pose errors"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Tuning-a-threshold","page":"Getting started","text":"A tuning is specified in a [tuning.<name>] table of a TOML config. The table names a scheme with type and states only what differs from the defaults in the package's configs/tuning.toml. Every spec names its data through a registered scene split; here, the PhotoTourism split of configs/splits.toml.\n\nconfig = TOML.parse(\"\"\"\n    [tuning.threshold]\n    type = \"coarse_fine\"\n    coarse = { type = \"spaced\", window = { type = \"fixed\", lo = 0.1, hi = 10.0 }, spacing = { type = \"log_per_decade\", per_decade = 4 } }\n    cross_validation = { scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"phototourism\" } }\n    \"\"\")\nspec = tuning_spec(config, \"threshold\")\nspec.scheme\nCoarseFine(SpacedAxis(FixedWindow(0.1, 10.0), LogPerDecade(4)), GridExtension(4, 2), 1, LogCount(200), FiniteMedian(), CrossValidation{RegisteredSceneSplit, ExhaustiveSampling, ExhaustiveSampling}(RegisteredSceneSplit(\"phototourism\", [\"brandenburg_gate\", \"british_museum\", \"buckingham_palace\", \"colosseum_exterior\", \"florence_cathedral_side\", \"grand_place_brussels\", \"lincoln_memorial_statue\", \"milan_cathedral\", \"mount_rushmore\", \"trevi_fountain\"], [\"notre_dame_front_facade\", \"palace_of_westminster\", \"pantheon_exterior\", \"piazza_san_marco\", \"reichstag\", \"sacre_coeur\", \"sagrada_familia\", \"st_pauls_cathedral\", \"st_peters_square\", \"taj_mahal\", \"temple_nara_japan\"]), ExhaustiveSampling(), ExhaustiveSampling()))\n\nThe coarse grid spans two decades at four points per decade:\n\ncollect(grid(grid_spec(spec.scheme.coarse)))\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n  0.1\n  0.1778279410038923\n  0.31622776601683794\n  0.5623413251903491\n  1.0\n  1.7782794100389228\n  3.1622776601683795\n  5.623413251903491\n 10.0\n\ncoarse_fine_cv runs the coarse-to-fine search on an objective. The objective returns the validation errors at a threshold x; the spec's criterion (the median error, by default) reduces them to one number. Here a stand-in objective is smallest at x = 2:\n\nvalidation_errors(x) = [abs(log(x / 2)) + 0.1 * k for k in 1:5]\nresult = coarse_fine_cv(validation_errors, grid_spec(spec.scheme.coarse), spec.scheme;\n                        method_name = :threshold)\nresult[:x_star_fine]\n2.0022003718155847\n\nThe search first evaluates the coarse grid. When the best point lies near an edge of the window, the grid extends on that side. A fine grid over the neighbours of the coarse optimum then gives the tuned value. A result whose optimum is still on the boundary after every extension is flagged, and the readers of tuned values refuse it:\n\nresult[:boundary_hit]\nfalse","title":"Tuning a threshold"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"The experimental protocol states how the stack's papers tune, evaluate and report.\nThe [tuning] schema lists every parameter of a tuning spec.\nThe composition evaluator evaluates a scorer together with a local optimizer on stored pools.\nPaper projects describes how a paper's figures are produced and checked.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#Paper-projects","page":"Paper projects","text":"Every paper of the stack is produced by a paper project. A paper project is a DrWatson project, not a package. Each of its figures, tables and numbers comes from a tracked driver that runs on a config of record and reads declared data, and each result has a provenance record beside it.\n\nThe worked example is the project accv2026 of the ACCV 2026 paper (Asian Conference on Computer Vision), kept in LocalOptimizationDoneRight, with its REPRODUCE.md. One check enforces the rules below, and a template starts a project that already satisfies them.","title":"Paper projects"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#Layout","page":"Paper projects","text":"Path Holds\nProject.toml a DrWatson project depending on DrWatson and VisualGeometryToolkitEval; Manifest.toml is tracked, as the environment of record\nconfigs/ the configs of record (TOML), one per driver or cell\nscripts/ the drivers, one file per paper asset or cell, plus scripts/tools/ for what they launch\nsrc/ the shared prelude that the drivers include with include(srcdir(…)): project code, never a data constant\ntest/runtests.jl the suite; it calls paper_project_violations\npaper/ the manuscript, with generated assets under paper/figures/\ndata/exp_raw, data/exp_pro the per-machine bindings of the input and results trees (links or directories, ignored by git); data/sims for smoke runs\nplots/ local renderings that are not paper assets\nREPRODUCE.md the asset index (see Reproducible figures)\nArtifacts.toml, configs/data_artifacts.toml the published input data and their catalog\n\nPaths come from DrWatson (projectdir, srcdir, datadir, plotsdir) and from the data layer of Experiments, never from a literal machine path.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#Rules","page":"Paper projects","text":"Configs of record. Every value that a result depends on is in a TOML file under configs/ that the driver reads. A driver holds no data constant: no literal seed, scene or frame list, data path or numeric table. A justified constant, such as a published test vector, is marked # paper-project: allow C2 <reason>.\nRun keys from configs. A result directory is named by run_key(cfg), the SHA-256 hash of the effective config without its [render] table. A changed config therefore gets a new directory and never overwrites an old one. A wall-clock stamp or a hand-bumped version suffix is never used. DrWatson's savename is the equivalent for flat configs; run configs are nested, hence the hash.\nProvenance per result. Every result directory carries _config.toml and _provenance.toml, written by write_run_records and write_provenance. The provenance is DrWatson's tag! record: the commit, the patch of a dirty tree, and the declared inputs relative to their roots. cached_result is the keyed cache, with DrWatson's produce_or_load file names, tagged.\nStage runner. A driver declares its inputs (require_input) and its stages (Stage(name, desc, done, run)), and it ends with run_stages. --plan prints what is done, --stage=NAME runs one stage and --force runs stages again.\nData policy. Inputs under data_root() are immutable, and the writers refuse them (assert_not_input). Results under results_root() are add-only. Code holds no machine path and no artifact\"…\" string macro (data_policy_violations, rules D1 to D4).\nPublished data as Julia artifacts. Input data are published on Zenodo as lazy, hash-pinned artifacts (Artifacts.toml). They are catalogued in configs/data_artifacts.toml (see The data catalog) and read through the artifact view that data_root() falls back to. A fresh machine therefore runs the drivers unchanged.\nREPRODUCE.md. Every figure, table and macro file that the paper reads is a row of the asset index. A row is either generated by a driver that declares it (# reproduce: writes <path> and # reproduce: reads <config>) or static with a provenance note.","title":"Rules"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#The-enforcing-test","page":"Paper projects","text":"using Test\nusing DrWatson: projectdir\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects: paper_project_violations\n\n@testset \"paper-project conventions\" begin\n    v = paper_project_violations(projectdir(); papers = [\"paper/main.tex\"])\n    foreach(x -> @info(sprint(show, x)), v)\n    @test isempty(v)\nend\n\npaper_project_violations returns a list of PaperProjectViolations, each with a check, a rule, a file, a line and a message:\n\ncheck rules\n:layout L1 a required path is missing · L2 Project.toml lacks DrWatson or VisualGeometryToolkitEval · L3 git does not ignore data/exp_raw and data/exp_pro · L4 the suite does not call this check\n:data_policy D1 a machine path · D2 a write into the input tree · D3 an unkeyed output · D4 an artifact\"…\" in code (the data_policy keyword forwards keywords)\n:reproduce every kind of reproduce_index_violations (the reproduce keyword forwards keywords)\n:configs C1 a producing script reads no config of record · C2 it holds a data constant · C3 it names a config that does not exist\n:provenance P1 no provenance through the shared writer (in the script, what it includes, or what it launches) · P2 no run key · S1 an index generator without run_stages\n:catalog A1 the findings of catalog_violations, once the catalog or Artifacts.toml binds an artifact\n:contract opt-in: the contract_violations of every stage file\n\nA producing script is a .jl file under scripts/ that runs stages, declares an output, is an index generator or calls a result writer. The scans of configs and provenance are static and conservative: a necessary condition, not a proof.\n\nA file opts out of one rule with # paper-project: allow <RULE> <reason>. For C2 and C3 the comment goes on the line or the line above; for C1, P1, P2 and S1 it may go anywhere in the file. An opt-out without a reason is rule X1. A project's deferred violations go in the allow keyword as DataPolicyAllow(rule, file, match) values, each with its fix in a comment beside it. An entry that matches nothing is reported as stale, so the list only shrinks.","title":"The enforcing test"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#The-template","page":"Paper projects","text":"using VisualGeometryToolkitEval.PaperProjects: new_paper_project\nnew_paper_project(\"neurips2026\"; papers = [\"paper/main.tex\"])   # git = false inside a package repository\n\nnew_paper_project runs DrWatson's initialize_project with PAPER_PROJECT_TEMPLATE and writes the files of the layout above: Project.toml, README.md, CLAUDE.md (a pointer to these conventions), .gitignore, REPRODUCE.md with the index table, configs/example.toml, scripts/example_stage.jl, src/<name>.jl, test/runtests.jl, the papers, Artifacts.toml, the catalog stub and data/README.md. The example stage shows the stage runner, a config of record, a run key and provenance; it writes a figure, its caption and a macro file. Then:\n\ncd neurips2026\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=. scripts/example_stage.jl      # the example's assets\njulia --project=. test/runtests.jl              # zero violations","title":"The template"},{"category":"section","location":"paper-projects/#The-data-catalog","page":"Paper projects","text":"configs/data_artifacts.toml is read by load_catalog in the DataArtifacts module:\n\n[catalog]                                    # optional\nartifacts_toml     = \"../Artifacts.toml\"     # the bindings, relative to the catalog\nartifacts_toml_env = \"ACCV2026_ARTIFACTS_TOML\"   # a variable naming another copy\n\n[records.<group>]      # one hosted record: title, placeholder, license, host (zenodo | huggingface) and its keys\n[artifacts.<name>]     # kind, mount, record, required, what, provenance, and the kind's keys\n\nThe variable named by artifacts_toml_env points to another copy of the bindings, with other URLs and the same hashes; the fresh-machine test uses it for a file:// copy. A record's host is where its tarballs are downloaded from. On Zenodo (the default) it has an id once the record exists; on Hugging Face it names its dataset repo and, once the tarballs are uploaded, the revision — a commit, never a branch, so a binding stays immutable while the dataset is edited. Until then the record carries its placeholder, an UPPERCASE token that `fetchartifacts!` refuses to download. A typical use: Hugging Face while the evaluation data is still changing, Zenodo (with its DOI) at publication — the bindings' hashes are the same on both, only the URLs move:\n\n[records.rc]\ntitle = \"…\"\nlicense = \"CC-BY-4.0\"\nhost = \"huggingface\"\nrepo = \"radiometric-camera/radiometric-camera-data\"\nplaceholder = \"HF_REVISION_RC\"\n\nThe kinds of artifact and their keys:\n\nkind keys holds\npool_sources config, splits the raw files that the pool producer config reads for the scenes of splits\npools config, splits the pool tree that config writes\nstored config the directory that the [stored] table of config names\ntree dir, optional filter every file under dir; with filter, a list of globs over paths relative to the mount root, the files an include glob matches (every file when there is none) and no ! glob matches\n\nA filter glob uses * for a run of characters other than /, ? for one of them, **/ for any run of whole directories (none included) and a trailing /** for everything below; every other character is literal. For example filter = [\"**/*.ARW\", \"!archive/**\"] with dir = \".\" selects every raw file outside archive/. pack_artifacts keeps the comment header of Artifacts.toml (its leading comment lines), which Pkg's writer drops.\n\nAn entry too large to upload as one file can set split_by = \"scene\" (kinds pool_sources and pools). It is then bound as one artifact per scene, <name>_<scene>, plus <name>_meta for the files that no scene owns. The entry stays one logical artifact: required, fetch_artifacts! and pack_artifacts take its name and act on every part.\n\nWith split_by, the optional max_part_bytes = N caps one part at N uncompressed bytes. A scene whose files exceed it is bound as sub-parts <name>_<scene>_part1 to _partk. A file larger than N is cut into the fewest near-equal chunks <file>.chunk<i>of<m>, and the chunks are packed greedily into parts. fetch_artifacts! reassembles each chunked file in the artifact view, byte for byte. The author's file sizes derive the parts, and a consumer reads them from the bindings.\n\nEvery artifact tree holds paths relative to its mount root: data is data_root() and results is results_root(). The author's tree, a packed artifact and a fetched view therefore share one layout. A project adds kinds of its own with the kinds keyword (kind => (cfg, entry, root) -> files).","title":"The data catalog"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-[tuning]-schema","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"This is the one document of the stack's cross-validation specification. Every parameter that influences a tuned result is a field of a strategy type and has its default in configs/tuning.toml. The two are joined by one parser, Tuning.tuning_spec. A tuning run persists the spec it ran under, so a deployed value can be traced back to the exact protocol that produced it.\n\nModule: VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning (src/tuning/*.jl). The protocol these specs serve is The experimental protocol.","title":"The [tuning] schema"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Vocabulary","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"Each quantity has one name, used in types, fields, TOML keys, grid axes and artifact keys:\n\nname meaning\nscale_multiple a width as a multiple of the estimated inlier scale ŝ (dimensionless); width = scale_multiple · ŝ\nloss_width a robust loss's width, supplied directly (no scale estimate): the width of a lifted loss, of a loss minimized by iteratively reweighted least squares (IRLS), or of a threshold score (a threshold is its truncated loss's width)\noutlier_halfwidth the Student-t marginal's uniform outlier half-width\nscale_multiple_window the standardized window [lo, hi] of scale multiples every search window is cut from","title":"Vocabulary"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Where-the-parameters-live:-three-levels","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"A config states [tuning.<name>] tables. Each names its scheme with type and states only what differs from the defaults. A key it leaves out is inherited through three levels:\n\n[tags.<family>.<tag>]: the default parameters of a tagged strategy (for example, log_count has n = 200). The untagged strategies use [types.<type>] instead: the scale-multiple window, the grid extension, the cross-validation and the budget sampling.\n[schemes.<scheme>]: the scheme's own defaults, where they differ from level 1.\nThe config's [tuning.<name>] table.\n\nIf a tagged table names a different type than the table it would inherit, it starts again from its own tag's defaults: a strategy never inherits the parameters of a different strategy. An unknown key or tag is an ArgumentError that names the key path, for example [tuning.miscal.coarse.spacing] has unknown key(s) [\"per_decad\"].\n\nlayer_tuning(lower, upper) layers a paper's protocol-level table under a cell's overrides with the same tag-aware rule; the parser does not layer, so a paper layers its two tables before parsing. resolve_tuning!(cfg) replaces every entry with its complete table. The Experiments run_key and write_run_records both resolve before hashing or writing, so a run key covers every tuning parameter, including defaults the config only inherited.\n\nThe writer tuning_table(x) emits the complete table. The round trips TOML → type → TOML and type → persisted table (read_tuning) → type are both lossless under ==. A persisted table inherits nothing: a missing key is an error.","title":"Where the parameters live: three levels"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Tag-families","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"Every tagged table carries type = \"<tag>\". The registry is TUNING_TAGS.","title":"Tag families"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#spacing:-GridSpacing","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"grid(lo, hi, spacing) is the one grid constructor: a Base range (linear) or logrange (log) of npoints(lo, hi, spacing) points. Both endpoints are exactly lo and hi, and every grid has at least 2 points.\n\ntag type parameters points\nlog_per_decade LogPerDecade per_decade round(log10(hi/lo)·per_decade) + 1\nlog_per_octave LogPerOctave per_octave round(log2(hi/lo)·per_octave) + 1\nlog_count LogCount n n log-spaced points\nlinear_count LinearCount n n linear points\n\nThe +1 is the fencepost: d decades at p per decade make d·p intervals, so they have d·p + 1 points.","title":"spacing: GridSpacing"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#window:-WindowRule","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"window_bounds(rule; scale_multiple_window, native, s2, center) returns (lo, hi). A rule refuses a context it needs and is not given.\n\ntag type parameters window\nfixed FixedWindow lo, hi (required) [lo, hi] in native units\nstandardized StandardizedWindow none the run's scale-multiple window\ncoverage CoverageWindow q_lo, q_hi, log_margin, zeta_octaves [native(lo, ŝ_min/ζ)·e^-m, native(hi, ŝ_max·ζ)·e^m], where (ŝ_min, ŝ_max) = scale_bounds(s2, (q_lo, q_hi)), ζ = 2^zeta_octaves and m = log_margin\ncentered CenteredWindow factor [center/factor, center·factor] around a centre the axis supplies\n\nThe coverage window depends on the data: on the validation pool's σ̂² and on the codimension d_g inside native. The realized window is persisted in the grid spec and the rule in the spec. d_g is not a tuning parameter: it is the problem's codimension, has no default anywhere, and is persisted as the artifact key d_g. q_lo == q_hi is the one-anchor case; at 0.5 it is the reference scale, so { q_lo = 0.5, q_hi = 0.5, zeta_octaves = 0.0 } is the window-matched grid.","title":"window: WindowRule"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#axis:-TuningAxis","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"tag type fields\nspaced SpacedAxis window (a rule), spacing\nlisted ListedAxis values (finite, strictly increasing)\n\ngrid_spec(axis; context...) realizes an axis as a persisted GridSpec.","title":"axis: TuningAxis"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#grid:-GridSpec-(persisted,-never-authored)","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"tag type fields\nspaced SpacedGrid lo, hi, spacing, n_lo, n_hi\nlisted ListedGrid values\n\ngrid(spec) rebuilds the realized grid: one range over the base window's step continued n_lo steps below lo and n_hi above hi (a log step is a ratio). extend(spec, sides; npts) -> (spec′, n_lo) is the one extension: every point of grid(spec) reappears, to rounding, at index + n_lo, so computed values stay valid by index. extension_offset(base, sup) compares specs, never realized values: it returns the offset when sup is base or an extension of it, and nothing otherwise. nearest_column(grid, x) is the evaluation-only lookup of a corrupted native (never a tuning's).","title":"grid: GridSpec (persisted, never authored)"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#criterion:-the-objective-(TuningCriterion)","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"tag type parameters objective (minimized)\nmedian_error MedianError none median, failures as Inf\nfinite_median FiniteMedian none median of the finite errors (Inf when none)\nscene_mean_lower_median SceneMeanLowerMedian none mean over scenes of each scene's lower median\nscene_mean_median SceneMeanMedian none mean over scenes of each scene's finite median (Stats scene_mean_median)\nmean_average_accuracy MeanAverageAccuracy max_error (required) minus the mean average accuracy (mAA) up to max_error, in the errors' own units (Stats eligible_maa)","title":"criterion: the objective (TuningCriterion)"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#scene_split:-SceneSplit","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"tag type parameters\nregistered RegisteredSceneSplit name (required); validation_scenes, test_scenes filled in from configs/splits.toml","title":"scene_split: SceneSplit"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#unit_sampling:-UnitSampling","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"tag type parameters\nexhaustive ExhaustiveSampling none\nstratified StratifiedSampling fraction, cap_per_scene (0 = no cap), seed, rng\nuniform UniformSampling fraction, cap (0 = no cap), seed, rng\n\nThe retired tags all and simple_random (now exhaustive and uniform) are refused as unknown.","title":"unit_sampling: UnitSampling"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Untagged-strategies-([types.key])","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"key type fields\nscale_multiple_window ScaleMultipleWindow lo, hi\ngrid_extension GridExtension npts (per clipped side per round), max_rounds\ncross_validation CrossValidation scene_split (no default), validation, test (both exhaustive by default)\nbudget_sampling BudgetSampling sizes, n_draws, seed, rng","title":"Untagged strategies ([types.<key>])"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Randomness","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"Each rule below is the definition. Every seed of a sample is derived by Stats stable_seed(seed, keys...): the first 8 bytes of the SHA-256 hash of the seed and the keys as text. The algorithm is fixed, unlike Base.hash, which changes across Julia versions, and the seed is keyed by a name rather than an index:\n\nStratified sampling. Scene s draws from rng(stable_seed(seed, s)).\nUniform sampling. The pooled draw is rng(stable_seed(seed, \"simple_random\")) (the salt keeps its original spelling, so a sample did not move with the type's rename).\nBudget draws. Draw r at budget n is rng(stable_seed(seed, \"budget\", n, r)) (budget_rng, budget_units); the interval over a budget's draws resamples from rng(stable_seed(seed, \"resampling\", n)) (resampling_rng).\nScene-clustered bootstrap rows (Stats scene_mean_median_rows!): point j resamples from StableRNG(stable_seed(seed, \"scene_mean_median\", j)) (the mAA's: key \"scene_mean_maa\").\n\nrng names an algorithm in RNG_ALGORITHMS: \"StableRNG\" only (StableRNGs.jl), whose streams are fixed across Julia versions; a permutation is drawn with shuffle, which StableRNGs guarantees (randperm it does not). The bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap itself (Bootstrap.jl) resamples from Julia's default stream seeded by the row's rng, so a reported interval also depends on the Julia version, recorded in every artifact's code identity.","title":"Randomness"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-cross-validation","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"Every scheme holds a cross_validation::CrossValidation: the data a tuning reads and the data its evaluation reports on.\n\nCrossValidation(scene_split::SceneSplit, validation::UnitSampling, test::UnitSampling)\n\nThe scene split. RegisteredSceneSplit(name, validation_scenes, test_scenes): a split of configs/splits.toml, the one file of record of every scene list in the stack. A config names it, scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"eth3d\" }, and never lists scenes (a config that does must list exactly the registry's, else an ArgumentError). The parser fills the lists into the complete table, so tuning_hash, the run key and every artifact carry the exact lists used: editing a list moves every hash that names the split. The constructor refuses an empty side, a duplicate and any scene on both sides; the lists are kept sorted. There is no default split.\nThe unit samplings. ExhaustiveSampling(); StratifiedSampling(fraction, cap_per_scene, seed, rng), each scene sampled separately (the scene mix is preserved); UniformSampling(fraction, cap, seed, rng), the units of all the side's scenes pooled. A sampler always permutes, keeps max(1, round(fraction·n)) and applies the cap. Only then does it sort back into the data's order, so a cap never keeps a prefix of the data. Adding, removing or reordering other scenes leaves a stratified scene's sample unchanged.\nThe sampling unit is a property of the data, never of the spec: sampling_unit(::Type{D}) is PairUnit() (an image pair: the stereo datasets, the scoring pools) or QueryUnit() (a query image of an absolute-pose dataset), declared by each data type with no fallback. The tuning artifact records it.\n\nvalidation_units(cv, units_by_scene) and test_units(cv, units_by_scene) apply a side: units_by_scene maps scene names to their units in the data's order and must hold every scene of that side; the result is scene => kept units, scenes sorted. sample_units(sampling, units_by_scene) is the sampler itself.\n\nA driver states which samplings it supports in the signature of the method it calls, never with a runtime check, so an unsupported spec is a MethodError:\n\ntwo_view_units(cv::CrossValidation{<:SceneSplit,<:Union{ExhaustiveSampling,StratifiedSampling}}, pools) = …\nquery_units(cv::CrossValidation{<:SceneSplit,<:Union{ExhaustiveSampling,UniformSampling}}, queries) = …\n\nThe budget draws of a validation-budget run are the scheme's budget_sampling (BudgetSampling(sizes, n_draws, seed, rng)): for each budget n, n_draws uniform samples of n of the eligible validation units, budget_units(b, n_units) -> [(n, r, indices)].\n\n[tuning.miscal.cross_validation]\nscene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"eth3d\" }\nvalidation = { type = \"stratified\", fraction = 0.25 }\ntest = { type = \"exhaustive\" }","title":"The cross-validation"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Schemes","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"","title":"Schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#joint_coarse_fine:-JointCoarseFine-(Evaluation-coarse_fine_search)","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"The coarse-to-fine search over the product of named axes. It runs in four steps:\n\nthe coarse product grid, every cell scored;\nwhile the joint argmin lies within edge_margin points of a face, that axis extends on that side (extension.npts points, at most extension.max_rounds per side) and only the new slab is scored;\nthe product of each axis's fine spacing over the coarse argmin's two neighbours on that axis; its free argmin is the selection;\nbootstrap_argmin over the evaluated cells' unit_statistics gives the joint selection's sampling distribution, read per axis.\n\nAn axis still within the margin after its budget is a boundary hit, refused by the one reader deployed_setting. An expensive axis is asked for one value at a time: the objective is called once per round per distinct expensive value, so a caller that keeps its expensive run (a detection run) per value pays one run per value visited.\n\nfield meaning\naxes name → CoarseFineAxis: coarse (window rule + spacing), extension (npts, max_rounds per side), fine (spacing over the bracket), expensive\nedge_margin extend when the argmin lies within this many points of a face\ncriterion the objective (the errors at a cell; the criterion aggregates them)\ncross_validation the scene split and the validation / test unit samplings\n\nA new search backend (a surrogate model, say) is a new scheme whose search returns the same record (selected, boundary_hit, evaluated); the artifact, the reader and the bootstrap serve it unchanged.","title":"joint_coarse_fine: JointCoarseFine (Evaluation coarse_fine_search)"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#coarse_fine:-CoarseFine-(Evaluation-coarse_fine_cv)","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"The one-axis case of joint_coarse_fine, its axis spelled flat (the same search; coarse_fine_cv and its batched form return its record in the scalar keys).\n\nfield meaning\ncoarse axis: the coarse probe's window rule and spacing\nextension npts per extension, max_rounds per side\nedge_margin extend when the argmin lies within this many points of an edge\nfine the fine spacing over the coarse argmin's bracket\ncriterion the objective (evalfn(x) returns the errors; the criterion aggregates them)\ncross_validation the scene split and the validation / test unit samplings","title":"coarse_fine: CoarseFine (Evaluation coarse_fine_cv)"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#windowed_bootstrap:-WindowedBootstrap-(Evaluation-WindowedGridMethod)","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"field meaning\nscale_multiple_window the standardized window every search window is cut from\noutlier_halfwidth, loss_width the native grids (axes)\nscale_multiple the standardized display axis\ncriterion the objective of each draw's windowed free argmin\ncross_validation the scene split and the validation / test unit samplings\nbudget_sampling the validation budgets and draws (compute_method_rows)","title":"windowed_bootstrap: WindowedBootstrap (Evaluation WindowedGridMethod)"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#factorial:-Factorial","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"Every combination of named settings of a method's configuration keys: listed axes, no reference scale, no boundary extension.\n\nfield meaning\naxes named axes (configuration key → TuningAxis, usually listed)\ncriterion the objective\ncross_validation the scene split and the validation / test unit samplings","title":"factorial: Factorial"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Examples","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"A scalar probe:\n\n[tuning.polish]\ntype = \"coarse_fine\"\ncoarse = { window = { type = \"fixed\", lo = 0.5, hi = 16.0 }, spacing = { type = \"log_per_decade\", per_decade = 4 } }\nextension = { npts = 4, max_rounds = 2 }\nfine = { type = \"log_count\", n = 200 }\ncross_validation = { scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"phototourism\" } }\n\nThe bootstrap harness:\n\n[tuning.lo_sensitivity]\ntype = \"windowed_bootstrap\"\nscale_multiple_window = { lo = 1.0, hi = 3.3 }\nscale_multiple = { spacing = { type = \"linear_count\", n = 17 } }\nbudget_sampling = { n_draws = 120 }\ncross_validation = { scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"phototourism\" } }\n\nA joint detector tuning (one detection run per peak threshold):\n\n[tuning.detection]\ntype = \"joint_coarse_fine\"\ncriterion = { type = \"mean_average_accuracy\", max_error = 10.0 }\naxes.peak_threshold = { coarse = { type = \"spaced\", window = { type = \"fixed\", lo = 0.005, hi = 0.02 }, spacing = { type = \"log_per_octave\", per_octave = 2 } }, fine = { type = \"log_count\", n = 3 }, expensive = true }\naxes.min_similarity = { coarse = { type = \"spaced\", window = { type = \"fixed\", lo = 0.3, hi = 0.8 }, spacing = { type = \"linear_count\", n = 6 } }, fine = { type = \"linear_count\", n = 5 } }\ncross_validation = { scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"<a registered split>\" } }","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#The-tuning-artifact","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"save_tuning_artifact(path, spec; grids, unit, code, data...) is the one writer of every artifact a tuning run persists (a tuning sidecar or its tune-phase checkpoint, a probe CV artifact, a bundle of validation matrices); load_tuning_artifact(path) -> TuningArtifact is the one reader.\n\nkey content\ntuning_format the :tuning artifact format (src/artifact_format.jl): the constant, not a number copied here\ntuning_name the [tuning.<name>] entry\ntuning_spec its complete scheme table, the cross-validation's resolved scene lists included\ntuning_hash tuning_hash(spec): SHA-256 (16 hex) of the sorted TOML of the scheme table; names and spelling do not move it, any parameter does\ngrid_specs axis → GridSpec table; the realized grids are rebuilt from these\nsampling_unit \"pair\" or \"query\": the data's sampling_unit (art.unit)\ncode_identity version and git commit (-dirty when uncommitted) of this package and of every module the caller names as the code its result came from; julia: its version and the rng algorithms\n… the run's own data (the arms, their tuned points as native values, the boundary hits, d_g, …)\n\nThe reader refuses a file of another format first, with an ArgumentError naming the file and the format change (regenerate it by re-running its tuning). The same mechanism stamps pools, with pool_format. The reader then refuses a stored spec whose hash does not match its table, and finally rebuilds every grid: art.grids[:axis] is the realized grid.","title":"The tuning artifact"},{"category":"section","location":"tuning/#Provenance:-from-a-deployed-constant-to-the-run","page":"The [tuning] schema","text":"A deployed cross-validated constant carries tuning = { config, name, hash, arm }: the config of record holding the [tuning.<name>] spec, the spec's tuning_hash, and the arm it was tuned for. A transferred constant names the constant it transferred from.\nParsing the config's [tuning.<name>] (layered as the paper layers it) gives the spec; its hash must equal the constant's.\nThe run's artifact records the same spec and hash, its grid specs, its code identity and its run key, and the tuned point.\nThe run key (run_key: the SHA-256, 16 hex, of the resolved config's sorted TOML) names the directory that holds the artifact and its _config.toml / _provenance.toml records.","title":"Provenance: from a deployed constant to the run"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Developer-notes","page":"Developer notes","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval is developed in the VisualGeometryToolkit.jl repository, a Julia workspace that holds the stack's packages, one directory each. The repository's root CLAUDE.md describes the workspace, and the conventions every stack package follows are in VisualGeometryToolkitCore's conventions/ folder.","title":"Developer notes"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Running-the-tests","page":"Developer notes","text":"From the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'          # once per clone\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitEval -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\njulia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitEval -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(; test_args = [\"pool_io|tuning\"])'\n\nThe suite uses ReTestItems. Every test/*_tests.jl file holds one test item named after its file, and the items run in parallel on worker processes. The test_args filter items by name; check the reported total, because a pattern that matches nothing succeeds with zero tests. Fixtures that several items share are @testsetup modules in test/*_testsetup.jl.\n\nThe default suite includes the quality items: Aqua, ExplicitImports, a JET ratchet at zero reports, the calling conventions, a scan for undefined globals and the stack's naming check. The readme item runs every julia block of the README.","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Building-the-documentation","page":"Developer notes","text":"julia --project=VisualGeometryToolkitEval/docs --startup-file=no VisualGeometryToolkitEval/docs/make.jl\n\nThe site is written to VisualGeometryToolkitEval/docs/build/.","title":"Building the documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Dependency-boundaries","page":"Developer notes","text":"The package depends on VisualGeometryToolkitCore, VisualGeometryToolkitIO and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation only. The evaluator is written against VisualGeometryToolkitCore's contracts, so RANSACScoringDoneRight, LocalOptimizationDoneRight and PoseLib never appear among its dependencies; the projects that evaluate them depend on this package instead.\n\nFour source files are free of dependencies by contract, so that an environment that cannot load this package can include them by path: src/render/axis_style.jl, src/evaluation/cv_probe.jl, src/experiments/stages.jl and src/reproduce/reproduce_index.jl.","title":"Dependency boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Frames","page":"Developer notes","text":"A scoring pool (ScoringPool) is stored in NormalizedImageFrame and says so (frame); a perspective-n-point (PnP) pool is stored in ImageFrame. The pool and residual files hold plain data together with the stored maps (source_map, destination_map), and a value leaves the pool frame only through those maps. Nothing in this package converts a frame. The raw readers of Datasets return pixels.","title":"Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data_artifacts/#API:-DataArtifacts","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"Published input data as Julia artifacts. The catalog format is described in The data catalog.","title":"API: DataArtifacts"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data_artifacts/#Public-names","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data_artifacts/#Internals","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"DataArtifacts\n\nA paper project's published input data as lazy Julia artifacts. The catalog (load_catalog, DataCatalog) states what each artifact holds, where it mounts, which Zenodo record carries it and which config of record its file list derives from. The author packs and binds the artifacts (pack_artifacts); a consumer fetches them into the artifact view that the drivers read (fetch_artifacts!). catalog_violations checks a catalog against its bindings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.DataCatalog","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"DataCatalog\n\nA loaded data-artifact catalog (load_catalog): path (the catalog TOML), artifacts_toml (the Artifacts.toml its artifacts are bound in, after the artifacts_toml_env override), configs (the directory its config names resolve in: the catalog's own), records and artifacts (the tables).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.DataCatalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.artifact_files-Tuple{DataCatalog, AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"artifact_files(cat, name; kinds = Dict()) -> (; mount, root, files, pieces)\n\nThe files of artifact name — a catalog entry (all its parts), or one part of a split entry (artifact_parts): paths RELATIVE to its mount root root (dataroot() for mount = \"data\", resultsroot() for \"results\"), derived from the config of record the entry names (or, for tree, its directory). kinds adds project kinds, kind => (cfg, entry, root) -> files (cfg is nothing when the entry names no config). Every file must exist under root — the author's tree the artifact is packed from. pieces maps each of files (its path in the packed tree) to the bytes of the author's file it holds: the whole file, or one chunk of a sub-part (max_part_bytes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.artifact_files"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.artifact_parts-Tuple{DataCatalog, AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"artifact_parts(cat, name) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe Artifacts.toml names catalog entry name is bound as: [name], or for an entry with split_by = \"scene\" one artifact per scene of its splits, <name>_<scene>, preceded by <name>_meta (the files no scene owns) when the kind has such files (a pool tree's run records; a roma route's manifest). With max_part_bytes, a scene over the cap is <name>_<scene>_part1..k instead (the rule in the catalog header; the author's sizes derive k, a consumer reads it from the bindings).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.artifact_parts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.catalog_violations-Tuple{DataCatalog}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"catalog_violations(cat; kinds = String[]) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery inconsistency between the catalog and its Artifacts.toml, empty when none (a project's test, in one line): the two name the same artifacts; each entry has the common keys, a known kind (or one of the project's kinds), a filter only on a tree entry and as a non-empty list of globs, a known mount, a defined record and an existing config; each record has a placeholder (A_TOKEN_LIKE_THIS), a known host and its keys — on Zenodo, once it exists, a numeric id; on Hugging Face a repo and, once uploaded, a 40-hex commit revision; each binding is lazy, hash-pinned and downloads from its record's host (download_url) — the record's id or revision when it has one, else its placeholder; never a local path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.catalog_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.download_url-Tuple{DataCatalog, AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"download_url(cat, name; record_ids = Dict()) -> String\n\nThe download URL of artifact name on its record's host: zenodo_url for a host = \"zenodo\" record (the default), huggingface_url for a host = \"huggingface\" one. record_ids maps a record group to its Zenodo id or its Hugging Face revision (the author's rebind after an upload); without one, the record's id/revision, else its placeholder (which fetch_artifacts! refuses to download). A part of a split entry is hosted in its entry's record under its own name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.download_url"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.fetch_artifacts!","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"fetch_artifacts!(cat, names = required_artifacts(cat)) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nInstall names (Experiments.install_artifact: download, verify, unpack into the depot; a placeholder URL is refused with the way out) and link each tree into its mount root: data artifacts into the project's data/exp_raw as an artifact view — skipped when a data root is already bound (VGTKDATAROOT, or an author's own data/expraw) — and results artifacts into resultsroot() (data/exppro is created when nothing binds one). Idempotent. One (; name, tree, mount, dst, linked) per artifact; dst is nothing where nothing was linked. A split entry ([`artifactparts](@ref)) installs and links every part, one row each (the parts' trees share directories; the view links per file); a part's own name fetches that part alone. A chunked file (maxpartbytes`) is reassembled in the view once all its chunks are linked: the chunks concatenated in order into the file (a regular file; the chunk links stay), each chunk already verified by its artifact's tree hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.fetch_artifacts!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.huggingface_url-Tuple{DataCatalog, AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"huggingface_url(cat, name; record_ids = Dict()) -> String\n\nThe Hugging Face URL of artifact name in its record's dataset repo repo: https://huggingface.co/datasets/<repo>/resolve/<revision>/<name>.tar.gz, with the revision record_ids gives the entry's record group, else the group's revision, else its placeholder. The revision is a commit, never a branch: a binding pins its bytes (its sha256), and a branch moves while the data is edited.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.huggingface_url"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.load_catalog-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"load_catalog(path) -> DataCatalog\n\nRead the catalog at path. Its bindings are [catalog] artifacts_toml (relative to the catalog; default ../Artifacts.toml, the project root's), unless the variable [catalog] artifacts_toml_env names another copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.load_catalog"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.pack_artifacts","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"pack_artifacts(cat, names = sort(collect(keys(cat.artifacts))); stage,\n               record_ids = Dict(), local_toml = \"\", kinds = Dict()) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nAUTHOR-SIDE: pack each artifact from the author's data/results roots (artifact_files) with Pkg.Artifacts — create_artifact in a scratch artifacts directory under stage (never the depot), archive_artifact to <stage>/tarballs/<name>.tar.gz — concurrently, then bind_artifact! each into the catalog's Artifacts.toml (lazy = true, the tree hash, the tarball's sha256, its record's download_url). The file's comment header (its leading comment and blank lines), which Pkg's writer drops, is kept. A staged tarball whose file listing is current is reused. local_toml also writes a copy of the bindings with file:// URLs to the staged tarballs (the fresh-machine test's; never committed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.pack_artifacts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.required_artifacts-Tuple{DataCatalog}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"The names of the artifacts fetched by default (required = true), sorted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.required_artifacts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.split_scenes-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"split_scenes(side) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe scenes of one side of a registered scene split, side = \"<split>:validation\" or \"<split>:test\" (configs/splits.toml, Tuning.registered_split): how a catalog entry's (and a pool producer config's) splits names its scenes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.split_scenes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.zenodo_url-Tuple{DataCatalog, AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"zenodo_url(cat, name; record_ids = Dict()) -> String\n\nThe Zenodo URL of artifact name: https://zenodo.org/records/<id>/files/<name>.tar.gz?download=1, with the id record_ids gives the entry's record group, else the group's id, else its placeholder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts.zenodo_url"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data_artifacts/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts._glob_regex-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"DataArtifacts","text":"_glob_regex(glob) -> Regex\n\nThe whole-path regex of a tree entry's filter glob over /-separated relative paths: **/ any run of whole directories (none included), a trailing /** everything below, * any run of characters but /, ? one of them; every other character literal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DataArtifacts._glob_regex"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stats/#API:-Stats","page":"Stats","text":"Summary statistics of pose errors and the seed derivation of every random sample.","title":"API: Stats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stats/#Public-names","page":"Stats","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stats/#Internals","page":"Stats","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats","page":"Stats","text":"Stats\n\nSummary statistics of the evaluation: finite_median and lower_median, the validation reference_scale and its robust extremes (scale_bounds), the mean average accuracy (mAA) under the eligibility rule (eligible_maa, pair_outcomes), bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap intervals (bootstrap_ci), means of per-scene medians (scene_mean_median, scene_mean_median_rows!) and the seed derivation of every random sample (stable_seed, POOL_RNG), the quantile of a possibly empty sample (quantile_or) and the replay of persisted improvement traces (ImprovementTrace, trace_error_at, anytime_curve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.ImprovementTrace","page":"Stats","text":"ImprovementTrace(offsets, t_ns, hyp, err)\n\nThe persisted improvement events of n pairs in flat columns: pair i owns the events offsets[i]:offsets[i+1]-1 (so length(offsets) == n + 1), each with the elapsed time t_ns (nanoseconds), the hypothesis count hyp (number of hypotheses drawn so far) and the ground-truth error err of the best model so far. Events of a pair are in time order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.ImprovementTrace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.POOL_RNG","page":"Stats","text":"POOL_RNG\n\nThe RNG algorithm of every pool stream (a reader's per-scene pair subsample and the per-pair minimal-sample draws of write_scene_pool): StableRNGs' StableRNG, whose streams are fixed across Julia versions, seeded by stable_seed. Recorded in each pool file (pool_rng).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.POOL_RNG"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.anytime_curve-Tuple{ImprovementTrace, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Stats","text":"anytime_curve(tr, tgrid; stat = eligible_maa) -> Vector\n\nThe accuracy-versus-time curve of a trace: for each budget t (seconds) of tgrid, stat of the per-pair errors trace_error_at(tr, i; t_ns = 1e9t). Pairs with no event by t enter as Inf, so a failure counts in stat (eligible_maa scores it 0; finite_median drops it; e -> count(isfinite, e) is the number of pairs reached).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.anytime_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.bootstrap_ci-Tuple{Function, Random.AbstractRNG, AbstractVector}","page":"Stats","text":"bootstrap_ci(stat, rng, x; n_resamples, alpha) -> (lo, hi)\n\nBCa (bias-corrected and accelerated) bootstrap CI of stat(x) via Bootstrap.jl — Shekhovtsov's scipy.stats.bootstrap(..., method='BCa') protocol. For a REAL sample the non-finite entries are dropped first and a constant sample returns its value as a degenerate interval. Any other element type is resampled as given (e.g. a scene-cluster bootstrap over per-scene error matrices, with stat reducing the resampled scenes). Fewer than 3 elements → (NaN, NaN) (BCa's jackknife needs them).\n\nrng seeds the global Random stream before the Bootstrap.jl call (the library resamples from it and takes no rng), so a fresh seed per call keeps a run reproducible on one Julia version: the resampling itself is Julia's task-local default stream, whose algorithm Julia does not fix across versions (record julia_version beside a reported CI). n_resamples and alpha default to configs/default.toml [metrics] (bootstrap_resamples, ci_alpha).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.bootstrap_ci"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.eligible_maa","page":"Stats","text":"eligible_maa(errors, eligible = nothing; T = maa_threshold()) -> Float64\n\nThe mAA@T of a set of pairs under the eligibility rule: the continuous AUC (VgtkCore mean_average_accuracy) over the ELIGIBLE pairs, where a non-finite error (a run that yielded no model) scores 0 and stays in the denominator, and an ineligible pair (eligible[i] == false: no ground truth, or fewer valid correspondences than a minimal sample) is excluded. eligible = nothing treats every pair as eligible. NaN when no pair is eligible. T defaults to maa_threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.eligible_maa"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.finite_median-Tuple{Any}","page":"Stats","text":"finite_median(v; empty = NaN) -> Float64\n\nMedian over the finite entries of v. empty is returned when no finite entries exist — use the default NaN for REPORTING (propagates as missing) and Inf for ARGMIN OBJECTIVES (an all-NaN column must lose the argmin, never win it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.finite_median"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.lower_median-Tuple{Any}","page":"Stats","text":"lower_median(v) -> eltype(v)\n\nThe ⌈n/2⌉-th order statistic of v under isless: for an even n the LOWER of the two middle values, never their mean, so the result is always an element of v. NaN sorts last under isless — filter first when it should not count. Empty v → NaN. The statistic of record of the PnP supplement tables and the pool-size audits (an observed value, not an interpolation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.lower_median"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.maa_threshold-Tuple{}","page":"Stats","text":"maa_threshold() -> Float64\n\nThe mAA threshold of the reported statistics, in degrees: configs/default.toml [metrics] maa_threshold_deg (the 10 of mAA@10°). Every reader of that threshold calls this; none spells the number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.maa_threshold"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.pair_outcomes","page":"Stats","text":"pair_outcomes(errors, eligible = nothing) -> (; n_eligible, n_failed, n_skipped)\n\nThe counts behind eligible_maa: eligible pairs, eligible pairs whose run yielded no model (non-finite error), and skipped (ineligible) pairs — reported beside every mAA so a reader sees why runs fail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.pair_outcomes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.quantile_or-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Stats","text":"quantile_or(v, p; empty = NaN) -> Real\n\nquantile(v, p), or empty when v has no element. The report cells' quantile: a sample can be empty (every pair failed), and the caller chooses what that reads as.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.quantile_or"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.reference_scale-Tuple{Any}","page":"Stats","text":"reference_scale(s2) -> Float64\n\nThe validation reference scale s0 = √median σ̂² over the finite entries of the per-pair inlier variances s2 — the scale every tuning window is positioned at. Throws on an input with no finite entry: a window cannot be placed without a scale, and a NaN scale would silently select nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.reference_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scale_bounds-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Tuple{Real, Real}}","page":"Stats","text":"scale_bounds(s2, q) -> (ŝ_min, ŝ_max)\n\nRobust scale extremes of a validation pool: √ of the q = (q_lo, q_hi) quantiles of the per-pair noise variances s2 (σ̂², e.g. gt_variance; non-finite entries are dropped). Restricting drawable validation pairs to σ̂² ∈ [ŝ_min², ŝ_max²] makes the plug-in ŝ(n) = √median σ̂² over ANY drawn subset lie in [ŝ_min, ŝ_max], for every n. The coverage window rule (Tuning.CoverageWindow, which carries q) and the validation-eligible mask are built from these bounds, so a windowed tuner's window provably stays on its grid. q has no default: it is a parameter of the tuning spec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scale_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Stats","text":"scene_mean(stat, scene_errors) -> Float64\n\nThe equal-weight MEAN over scenes of each scene's stat (scene_errors iterates the scenes' value vectors); a scene whose statistic is not finite is skipped, and the result is NaN when no scene has one.\n\nThe scene-level aggregation: a per-scene statistic weights scenes, not pairs, which is what a cross-validation split BY SCENE is asking for. isfinite is the right skip predicate for either statistic this takes — finite_median filters non-finite values first and so returns a finite value or NaN, while lower_median does not and can return ±Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean_median-Tuple{Any}","page":"Stats","text":"scene_mean_median(scene_errors) -> Float64\n\nShekhovtsov's \"mean median\": scene_mean of each scene's finite_median.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean_median"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean_median_rows!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractString, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Stats","text":"scene_mean_median_rows!(rows, method, xs, cols, scene_ranges; seed, ci_min = [metrics] ci_min_scenes)\n\nShekhovtsov's \"Mean median\": per x, split the per-pair error view cols[j] by scene_ranges: point = scene_mean_median, CI = scene-clustered bootstrap_ci(mean, …) over the per-scene medians, resampled under StableRNG(stable_seed(seed, \"scene_mean_median\", j)) (the mAA's with key \"scene_mean_maa\"). Companion statistic: the per-scene eligible_maa (every stored pair is eligible — the pool only holds processable pairs), averaged over scenes. Schema (method, tuning_criterion, n, mean_med, lo95_mean_med, hi95_mean_med, mean_mAA, lo95_mean_mAA, hi95_mean_mAA); NOT pooled over all pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_mean_median_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_ranges-Tuple{Any}","page":"Stats","text":"scene_ranges(sizes) -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\n\nContiguous per-scene index ranges partitioning 1:sum(sizes) (pairs are laid out scene-by-scene). sizes[i] = number of pairs in scene i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.scene_ranges"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.stable_seed-Tuple{Integer, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Stats","text":"stable_seed(seed, keys...) -> UInt64\n\nThe seed derivation of every random sample in the evaluation stack: the first eight bytes (big-endian) of the SHA-256 of seed and keys printed as text, separated by the unit separator \u001f. A fixed algorithm, unlike Base.hash (which changes across Julia versions), and keyed by NAME, unlike seed + i: a scene's draw depends on that scene's name only, so adding, removing or reordering other scenes leaves it unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.stable_seed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.trace_error_at-Tuple{ImprovementTrace, Integer}","page":"Stats","text":"trace_error_at(tr, i; t_ns = nothing, hyp = nothing) -> Float64\n\nThe ground-truth error of pair i's best model once the budget is spent: the error of its LAST event whose time is at most t_ns and whose hypothesis count is at most hyp (a budget left nothing does not bind). Inf when no event is within the budget: a pair with no model yet counts as a failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.trace_error_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/stats/#Base.length-Tuple{ImprovementTrace}","page":"Stats","text":"Number of pairs of a trace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.length"},{"category":"section","location":"api/toplevel/#API:-the-top-module","page":"The top module","text":"The driver command line, the artifact formats and the path of the defaults of record.","title":"API: the top module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/toplevel/#Public-names","page":"The top module","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/toplevel/#Internals","page":"The top module","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.ARTIFACT_FORMATS","page":"The top module","text":"ARTIFACT_FORMATS\n\nThe format version this package writes and reads, per artifact kind (:pool, :pool_residuals, :pool_samples, :pnp_pool, :tuning), with the change the version introduced and the remedy for an older file. The file key is \"<kind>_format\"; an absent key is version 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.ARTIFACT_FORMATS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DEFAULTS_PATH","page":"The top module","text":"The defaults of record (configs/default.toml).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.DEFAULTS_PATH"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.cli_option-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"The top module","text":"cli_option(args, key[, default])\n\nThe string value of --key=value in args. With default, the default when the option is absent; without it, the option is REQUIRED and its absence throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.cli_option"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.start_output_flush!-Tuple{}","page":"The top module","text":"start_output_flush!(; interval = 2) -> Timer\n\nFlush stdout and stderr every interval seconds for the rest of the session (idempotent: a second call returns the running timer). The one flusher of every driver, GPU drivers included — it installs no logger type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.start_output_flush!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval._artifact_format-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"The top module","text":"The format version an open JLD2 file f declares for kind (absent key = 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval._artifact_format"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval._check_artifact_format-Tuple{AbstractString, Symbol}","page":"The top module","text":"_check_artifact_format(path, kind)\n\nRefuse path unless it declares the current kind format (ARTIFACT_FORMATS): an ArgumentError naming the file. Every reader of a kind artifact calls this before reading anything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval._check_artifact_format"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval._config_default-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"The top module","text":"_config_default(table, key)\n\nThe default key of [table] (a dotted path, \"pool.phototourism\") in configs/default.toml. Throws a KeyError naming a missing table or key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval._config_default"},{"category":"method","location":"api/toplevel/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval._save_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString, Symbol}","page":"The top module","text":"_save_artifact(path, kind; keys...) -> path\n\nThe writer of a kind artifact: keys to path (JLD2), stamped <kind>_format at the current version. A key named like the stamp is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval._save_artifact"},{"category":"section","location":"evaluator/#The-composition-evaluator","page":"The composition evaluator","text":"The composition evaluator measures a RANSAC scorer (the selection step) composed with a local optimizer (LO, the refinement step) on stored pool pairs. It lives in the Evaluation module, in src/evaluation/engine/.\n\nAn evaluated method is called an arm. Each arm is a pair of values that implement VisualGeometryToolkitCore's contracts:\n\na scoring, which implements score!, select_on_grid! and with_width;\na local optimization, whose RefitFamily routes it to one of two paths. NativeRefit runs locally_optimize! inside VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's RANSAC loop. ExternalRefit hands the model to a third-party refiner that maps a model to a model (refine_external!).\n\nThe evaluator holds no scorer or optimizer of its own. A project that evaluates particular scorers or optimizers depends on their packages and supplies their values.","title":"The composition evaluator"},{"category":"section","location":"evaluator/#The-pieces","page":"The composition evaluator","text":"Piece What it does\nPairCtx, build_pair_ctx builds, once per pool pair, the pool-frame problem, the calibrations, the ground-truth (GT) pose and the refit-scoring template that every refit shares\nrefit_imc!, guarded_refit the one refit layer: one refit of a seed model, with its pose error, its wall time and a fallback to the seed\nLoRefitSpec, apply_lo_refits turns a selection sweep into post-LO error matrices (pairs by grid columns)\nstream_selections streams the scenes: it stages each scene, runs select_on_grid! per grid, then one apply_lo_refits\nSampleBudgetMethodSpec, MsacSpec, evaluate_sample_budget the sample-budget engine: running-best selectors and LO checkpoints along each pair's hypothesis stream\nlive_runs, live_row end-to-end ransac runs on pool pairs, with a problem the caller builds per pair\nComposedSpec, SoloSpec, Roster, deployed_arm_settings, audit_operating_points a paper's arms, and the gated readers of their tuned operating points\n\n\"MSAC\" is the M-estimator sample consensus score, the truncated-quadratic baseline scorer of VisualGeometryToolkitCore.","title":"The pieces"},{"category":"section","location":"evaluator/#Widths","page":"The composition evaluator","text":"Widths are VisualGeometryToolkitCore width values. Each path receives its width in the form it consumes:\n\nA native refit is seeded under the scoring template rebuilt at its own outlier half-width, with_width(scoring, OutlierHalfwidth(a)).\nAn absolute width reaches the strategy as with_width(lo, fixed_width(lo, s2)).\nAn external refiner receives LossWidth(w), or FixedS2(s2) when the caller supplies the scale.","title":"Widths"},{"category":"section","location":"evaluator/#A-refit,-step-by-step","page":"The composition evaluator","text":"The snippet below shows the shape of one refit. It needs a pool pair, a scoring template with an outlier half-width, a local optimization and a seed model, which come from a scoring pool and from the packages that implement the scorer and the optimizer.\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation: build_pair_ctx, refit_imc!, problem_type\n\nP = problem_type(:E)                              # the essential-matrix problem type\nctx = build_pair_ctx(P, pair; scoring = template)\nimc, lo_ns = refit_imc!(ctx, lo, seed, nat)       # nat: the width of lo on the axis it consumes\n\nimc is the pose error of the refit model in degrees, max(ΔR, Δt) over the best decomposition, as the Image Matching Challenge (IMC) defines it. lo_ns is the optimizer's wall time in nanoseconds. consumed_axis and consumed_native give the width that lo consumes.","title":"A refit, step by step"},{"category":"section","location":"api/experiments/#API:-Experiments","page":"Experiments","text":"The stage runner, run records, provenance and the data layer of a paper project.","title":"API: Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"api/experiments/#Public-names","page":"Experiments","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/experiments/#Internals","page":"Experiments","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments","page":"Experiments","text":"Experiments\n\nThe paper-generic experiment-codification framework: the Stage table + run_stages entrypoint (--plan/--stage/--force), the run_driver shell-out and its in-process form run_isolated, require_input/fresher declarations, the run records (the configuration-of-record layer), the executable codification contract (contract_violations) and the one data layer of every paper project: the root ladder and artifact view (set_project_root!, data_root, ArtifactView), the input write guard (assert_not_input), the provenance record (write_provenance, read_provenance), the keyed cache (cached_result) and the data policy (data_policy_violations).\n\nThis module holds what every paper uses unchanged: the runner mechanics and the contract rules. A paper's own decisions stay in that paper's project: its stage rosters and input paths, its driver wiring (set with set_driver_context!), its artifact destinations, splits and oracle constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.RUN_CONFIG_KEYS","page":"Experiments","text":"RUN_CONFIG_KEYS :: Vector{Pair{String,Tuple}}\n\nAllowed keys of the run-config sections whose vocabulary THIS package defines, as dotted paths (\"render.main\" is the nested table). A section absent here belongs to the PROJECT that ships the config and is not key-checked — [source], [extract], [pool], [arm], [ransac] and the rest are read ad hoc by its drivers, and [source.recon_special] is keyed by scene name, so a fixed vocabulary would be wrong. [tuning.<name>] has its own, stricter check (Tuning _read_struct → VisualGeometryToolkitCore.check_keys, driven by the struct fields) and is therefore not restated here.\n\nNested tables are listed in their own right: a check on [render] alone sees main as one known key and nothing inside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.RUN_CONFIG_KEYS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.RUN_KEY_EXCLUDED","page":"Experiments","text":"Top-level config sections that never touch a run's data and so never key it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.RUN_KEY_EXCLUDED"},{"category":"type","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.ArtifactView","page":"Experiments","text":"ArtifactView(toml; dir, mount = name -> \"\", default = String[])\n\nA project's published input data as Julia artifacts: toml is the Artifacts.toml binding them (lazy, Zenodo-hosted tarballs), dir the view directory their trees are linked into (default <project>/data/exp_raw, so the view IS the project's DrWatson data directory), mount(name) the subdirectory of dir artifact name mounts at (trees overlap directories, so the view is per-file links), and default the artifacts data_root installs when asked for none in particular.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.ArtifactView"},{"category":"type","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.DataPolicyAllow","page":"Experiments","text":"DataPolicyAllow(rule, file, match)\n\nOne allowlisted (deferred) violation: rule \"D1\"…\"D4\", file relative to the scanned project root, and a substring match of the offending line. Each entry should name what fixes it, in a comment beside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.DataPolicyAllow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.Stage","page":"Experiments","text":"Stage(name::Symbol, desc::String, done, run)\n\nOne idempotent step of a codified experiment: done() is a CHEAP predicate (no GPU, no heavy loads) — a stage whose done() holds is skipped unless --force; run() performs the work (often a run_driver shell-out) and may assume every earlier stage completed. Declared in figure/table order and executed by run_stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.Stage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.TagRename","page":"Experiments","text":"TagRename(path::Regex, old, new)\n\nOne tag rename: a string VALUE old whose dotted key path matches path becomes new. The path of a value is its keys joined by ., an array element adding [i] (\"selectors[1]\", \"geometry.local_optimization.type\"), so a rename can be scoped to the family that owns the tag: \"fixed\" is one tag in nuisance.scale and another in window.type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.TagRename"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.artifact_view!-Tuple{ArtifactView, AbstractVector}","page":"Experiments","text":"artifact_view!(view, names) -> String\n\nInstall the artifacts names of view (install_artifact) and link each tree into the view directory at its mount (link_tree!), marking the directory .artifact_view. Idempotent; returns the view directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.artifact_view!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.assert_not_input-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"assert_not_input(path; producer = false)\n\nThe runtime guard of every writer here: refuse a write at or under data_root — the read-only input tree — unless the caller declares itself a producer (a pool generator writing a NEW tree under the data root). A results root nested inside the data root (one author binding has data_root()/results/…) is not input. Nothing is guarded when no data root is bound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.assert_not_input"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.assert_primary_copy-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"assert_primary_copy(what)\n\nProducers that write INTO the data tree (calibration generators, converters, packagers) call this first: an artifact view is read-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.assert_primary_copy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.cached_result-Tuple{Any, AbstractString, Any, AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"cached_result(f, prefix, cfg, dir; key = legacy_hash_key, force = false,\n              gitpath = project_root()) -> (data, file)\n\nThe keyed result cache: <dir>/<prefix>_<key(cfg)>.jld2 is loaded (DrWatson wload) when present, else f(cfg) (a String-keyed Dict) is computed and saved tagged like DrWatson's tagsave (gitcommit + gitpatch ride along) and written with DrWatson's safesave, so a forced re-run keeps the previous version as _#n. The file name is exactly the one DrWatson's produce_or_load(…; filename = hash, prefix) builds, so its existing caches are found. Justified extension: produce_or_load itself swallows save errors (DrWatson saving_files.jl:107-116 — a failed write returns the data as if cached) and forces safe = false; here a save error propagates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.cached_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.cancelled-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"cancelled() -> Bool\n\nWhether a kaimon cancel_eval was requested for the running evaluation (Main.KaimonGate.is_cancelled()); false outside a kaimon session. Poll it from long loops, e.g. live_runs(...; cancelled = Experiments.cancelled).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.cancelled"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.commit_drift-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Experiments","text":"commit_drift(prov; gitpath = project_root()) -> Union{Nothing, Tuple{String,String}}\n\n(recorded, current) when the commit a provenance record names is not the current one of gitpath, nothing when they agree or either is unknown. Compared as COMMITS, through DrWatson's gitdescribe(gitpath; dirty_suffix = \"\"): a describe string with an annotated tag (v0.7.0-12-g89f65bd), a bare sha and an exact tag all name their commit, and a dirty suffix is not a different commit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.commit_drift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.contract_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"contract_violations(file) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe codification-contract check for one experiment file: returns a human-readable violation per broken rule (empty ⇒ compliant). Support files (registry.jl and *common.jl, e.g. a paper's shared external-arm plumbing) are exempt from R3; *_worker.jl subroutines are exempt from its stage-runner requirement (they are driven by their parent experiment) but still carry a provenance line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.contract_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.data_policy_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"data_policy_violations(root; dirs = [\"src\", \"scripts\"],\n                       exts = [\".jl\", \".sh\", \".sbatch\"], allow = DataPolicyAllow[])\n    -> Vector{NamedTuple{(:rule, :file, :line, :text)}}\n\nThe data-policy findings (rules D1–D4, see the file header) in the dirs of project root, minus the allowed ones, plus a STALE finding for every allow entry that matched nothing. Empty ⇔ compliant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.data_policy_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.data_root-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"data_root(; tiers = nothing) -> String\n\nThe project's input tree (read-only): ENV[\"VGTK_DATA_ROOT\"] → the project's data/exp_raw (DrWatson datadir(\"exp_raw\"); a link, a directory, or the artifact view) → the project's fallback rung → its ArtifactView, installing tiers (default: the view's default). Configs state input paths RELATIVE to it; nothing under it is written except by a declared producer (assert_not_input).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.data_root"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.experiment_dir-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Experiments","text":"experiment_dir(cfg) -> String\n\nThe results directory of the config's [experiment]: <results root>/<dataset>/<matcher>/<problem>. A run's keyed directory lives under it, named by the project.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.experiment_dir"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.fresher-Tuple{AbstractString, Vararg{AbstractString}}","page":"Experiments","text":"fresher(out, inputs...) -> Bool\n\nThe standard Stage done-predicate: out exists, every input exists, and out is at least as new as each input — a stale panel from a missing/renewed input is NOT done.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.fresher"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.install_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"install_artifact(name, toml) -> String\n\nInstall artifact name of toml (download, verify sha256 and git-tree-sha1, unpack into the depot) unless present, and return its tree. A Hugging Face tarball is downloaded with the user's Hugging Face token when there is one (huggingface_token), so a private dataset installs for whoever has access to it. A placeholder URL (an UPPER_CASE token in place of the Zenodo id or the Hugging Face revision: the record is not published or uploaded) and a failed download (an unpublished Zenodo draft, a private dataset without a token) are refused with the way out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.install_artifact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.is_artifact_view-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"Whether dir is an artifact view (a directory carrying the .artifact_view marker).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.is_artifact_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.is_primary_copy-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"is_primary_copy() -> Bool\n\nWhether the data root is a writable primary copy (bound by ENV, the project's data/exp_raw or its fallback) rather than an artifact view, whose trees are content-addressed and read-only. Same resolution as data_root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.is_primary_copy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.legacy_hash_key-Tuple{Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"legacy_hash_key(x) -> String\n\nLEGACY, version-UNSTABLE key: string(hash(x)). It names the existing caches of bmvc2026 and other paper projects (<prefix>_<hash(cfg)>.jld2, bmvc2026's detection run_key), which must not move before their next full regeneration — that one re-keys them to run_key (SHA-256 of the sorted TOML). Never use it for a new key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.legacy_hash_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.link_tree!-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"link_tree!(tree, dst) -> Int\n\nLink every file of tree into dst (per-file symlinks: artifact trees share directories, so directory links would not compose); an existing path — a link made earlier or an author's own file — is left alone. Returns the number of new links.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.link_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.load_run_config-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"load_run_config(path) -> Dict\n\nParse a run config: it must carry [experiment] with dataset, matcher, problem and pool_dir, and an unknown key in a section this package owns (RUN_CONFIG_KEYS) is refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.load_run_config"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floor_done-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"oracle_floor_done(path, tag; source) -> Bool\n\nWhether cell tag has its floor in the sidecar path AND the floor was computed on source (oracle_floor_source): every key of source must equal the recorded one. Presence of the key alone is not enough: floors computed on a retired pool tree must not read as done.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floor_done"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floor_source-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"oracle_floor_source(pool_dir, problem, scenes) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe record of what an oracle floor was computed on: the pool tree, the problem family and the (sorted) scenes. write_oracle_floor stores it as the floor's source; oracle_floor_done compares it with the current one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floor_source"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floors_note-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"oracle_floors_note() -> String\n\nThe note stored in the oracle-floor sidecar. The mAA threshold in it is the configured one (VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Stats.maa_threshold), not a literal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floors_note"},{"category":"function","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floors_path","page":"Experiments","text":"The pseudo-GT oracle-floor sidecar of a results tree (the floors' producer writes it): <root>/oracle_floors.jld2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.oracle_floors_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.progress-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"progress(msg) -> nothing\n\nReport msg as the running evaluation's progress (Main.KaimonGate.progress(msg)); a no-op outside a kaimon session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.progress"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.project_root-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"The project the drivers run from: set_project_root!'s root, else DrWatson's projectdir().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.project_root"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.provenance_inputs-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Experiments","text":"provenance_inputs(prov) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe declared inputs of a provenance record as absolute paths: relative entries joined to the roots recorded beside them; a retired record's absolute list as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.provenance_inputs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.read_provenance-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"read_provenance(dir) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe provenance record of dir as the one flat dict of write_provenance, whatever layout wrote it: the current one; the VisualGeometryToolkitEval ≤ 0.1 one (its _dirty.patch read back as gitpatch); bmvc2026's [provenance] + [config] tables (merged; created read as timestamp). Errors when the record is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.read_provenance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.read_rekey_map-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"read_rekey_map(path) -> Dict{String,String}\n\nThe old-key → new-key map of a written re-key file (moved keys only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.read_rekey_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.rekey_map-Tuple{Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"rekey_map(configs; renames, keyed_table, key) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nFor each config path in configs: keyed_table(path) is the table the project's key function hashes, computed with the current code, and key(table) that hash. Returns one row per config — config, old_key, new_key, moved and the applied substitutions. A config whose keyed_table throws is recorded with error set and no keys (it is not keyed by this function).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.rekey_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.rename_tags-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractVector{TagRename}}","page":"Experiments","text":"rename_tags(table, renames) -> (renamed, applied)\n\nA deep copy of table with every matching rename applied, and the list of path => (old, new) substitutions made. A value matched by two renames is an ArgumentError (the renames must be unambiguous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.rename_tags"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.require_input-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"require_input(path; what, provenance)\n\nDeclared data input of an experiment: hard error (with the provenance recipe) when absent. Inputs are declared at the top of each experiment file so the dependency graph is readable without running anything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.require_input"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.results_root-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"results_root() -> String\n\nThe project's results tree: ENV[\"VGTK_RESULTS_ROOT\"] → the project's data/exp_pro (DrWatson datadir(\"exp_pro\")), created when the project binds create_results = true, an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.results_root"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_complete-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"run_complete(outdir; sidecar) -> Bool\n\nKeyed skip-if-complete: a run counts as done only when its result sidecar (a file name under outdir) AND its _provenance.toml exist; when skipping, WARN if the recorded commit differs from the project's current one (warn_commit_drift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_complete"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_driver-Tuple{AbstractString, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Experiments","text":"run_driver(script, args...; driver_dir = default_driver_dir(),\n           project = default_driver_project(),\n           env = Dict(), threads = get(ENV, \"SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK\", \"auto\"))\n\nShell out to a paper driver in a fresh Julia process (the drivers parse ARGS at include time, so in-process include is not composable). A relative script resolves against driver_dir; the child runs --project=<project>. Errors propagate (run throws on nonzero exit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_driver"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_isolated-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"run_isolated(path; args = String[], env = Dict{String,String}(),\n             bindings = Dict{Symbol,Any}()) -> Module\n\nInclude the script path in a fresh module of its own, in this process: a paper driver defines top-level constants meant for a process of its own, so several drivers included into one session collide, while a throwaway module gives each the clean top level it expects and keeps the loaded packages warm (no new process, no recompilation). While the script's top level runs, ARGS is args and the environment holds env (both restored afterwards, also on error); bindings are constants defined in the module first (a name the script uses unqualified). The module's exit throws instead of ending the session. Returns the module: the functions the script defined live in a newer world, so call them through Base.invokelatest. A separate process (run_driver) is the other way to run a driver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_isolated"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_key-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Experiments","text":"run_key(cfg) -> String\n\nThe run identity: the SHA-256 (hex, first 16) of the effective config printed as sorted TOML (Tuning _toml_hash, the one content hash behind tuning_hash too), with the RUN_KEY_EXCLUDED sections removed ([render] holds panel knobs of record, not experiment inputs) and every [tuning.<name>] entry resolved to its COMPLETE table (resolve_tuning!: a default of this package's configs/tuning.toml that the config inherits keys the run like a stated one). Key order does not move it, the Julia version does not move it, and a TOML round trip of the effective config reproduces it, so run_key(TOML.parsefile(\"_config.toml\")) names the directory that holds it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_stages-Tuple{AbstractString, Vector{Stage}}","page":"Experiments","text":"run_stages(experiment::AbstractString, stages; argv = ARGS)\n\nThe uniform experiment entrypoint: --plan prints the stage table and exits (the structural check); otherwise runs every pending stage in declaration order (--stage=NAME restricts to one, or to a comma-separated subset — declaration order is still honored; --force re-runs done stages). The printed table is a snapshot; each stage's done() is RE-EVALUATED immediately before it would be skipped, because an earlier stage of the same run may have invalidated it. A stage failure aborts the run (later stages depend on earlier ones).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.run_stages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.set_driver_context!-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Experiments","text":"set_driver_context!(driver_dir; project = driver_dir)\n\nRegister the default script directory (and Julia project) run_driver resolves against — called once by a paper's experiments/common.jl shim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.set_driver_context!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.set_project_root!","page":"Experiments","text":"set_project_root!(root = projectdir(); resolve_links = false, data_fallback = nothing,\n                  artifacts = nothing, create_results = false)\n\nBind the project the drivers run from (its git record, its data/exp_raw and data/exp_pro). The keywords are the per-project rungs of the ladder, kept so that every existing path resolves byte-identically:\n\nresolve_links: return realpath of the data/exp_raw link rather than the link path itself (bmvc2026: its run keys hash paths under it);\ndata_fallback: a () -> path | nothing rung after the link (bmvc2026's BlobBoards.data_dir() preference);\nartifacts: the ArtifactView of last resort;\ncreate_results: create data/exp_pro when nothing binds a results root (accv2026 errors instead).\n\nset_project_root!(\"\") unbinds (the root then follows DrWatson's projectdir()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.set_project_root!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.warn_commit_drift-Tuple{AbstractDict, Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"warn_commit_drift(prov, what; gitpath = project_root()) -> Bool\n\nSkip-if-complete's WARNING (code is not keyed; the record restores it): warn when prov was recorded under another commit (commit_drift) — re-run with force if that change was result-affecting. Returns whether it warned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.warn_commit_drift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_oracle_floor-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Any, Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"write_oracle_floor(path, tag, floor, maa; source, npairs, driver) -> path\n\nRecord the pool-best floor floor (median model error) and ceiling maa (mean pool-best mAA) of cell tag in the sidecar path (oracle_floors_path), which holds \"floors\" (tag => floor, tag * \"_maa\" => maa), \"sources\" (tag => source plus npairs, driver and the time computed) and \"note\" (oracle_floors_note). The other cells of the file are kept. The read-modify-write is serialized by a pid lock (path * \".lock\"), because the oracle stages of concurrent chains write one file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_oracle_floor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_provenance","page":"Experiments","text":"write_provenance(dir, fields = Dict(); driver, inputs = nothing,\n                 gitpath = project_root(), producer = false) -> String\n\nWrite <dir>/_provenance.toml: fields (the caller's record — run key, config source, network hash, frame counts, …) plus driver, timestamp, hostname, julia, and DrWatson's tag! record of gitpath (gitcommit, gitpatch on a dirty tree, with uncommitted_files — the paths that patch touches). inputs (paths) are recorded relative to the results or data root they live under, the roots beside them (inputs = {data = […], data_root = …}). Refuses to write into the data root unless producer (assert_not_input). Returns the record's path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_provenance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_rekey_map-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Experiments","text":"write_rekey_map(path, rows; project, renames, commits = Dict()) -> path\n\nWrite a rekey_map as TOML: the project, the renames, the commits it was computed at, and per config its old and new keys and the substitutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_rekey_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_run_records-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict}","page":"Experiments","text":"write_run_records(outdir, cfg; driver, config_source, key, inputs = String[], producer = false)\n\nWrite the run's _config.toml (the effective config, its tuning specs resolved to their complete tables) and its provenance record (write_provenance: config_source, run_key, the declared inputs relative to their roots, and DrWatson's git record). producer = true is a pool generator's declaration that outdir is a NEW tree under the data root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.write_run_records"},{"category":"method","location":"api/experiments/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.huggingface_token-Tuple{}","page":"Experiments","text":"huggingface_token() -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nThe user's Hugging Face token: ENV[\"HF_TOKEN\"], else the file hf auth login writes ($HF_HOME/token, HF_HOME defaulting to ~/.cache/huggingface), else nothing. install_artifact sends it to huggingface.co only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Experiments.huggingface_token"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reproduce/#API:-Reproduce","page":"Reproduce","text":"The reproducible-figures contract. See Reproducible figures.","title":"API: Reproduce"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reproduce/#Public-names","page":"Reproduce","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reproduce/#Internals","page":"Reproduce","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce","page":"Reproduce","text":"Reproduce\n\nThe reproducible-figures contract: paper_asset_graph (what a paper reads), read_reproduce_index (what REPRODUCE.md declares) and reproduce_index_violations (where the two disagree, plus generators that do not write their asset, static assets without provenance, and generated numbers re-typed in prose); and the regeneration's last stages: verify_assets and deploy_assets (a submitted asset is replaced only with sign-off), gather_numbers (the numbers file) and uncited_macros (numbers the paper defines but never cites).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.VERIFY_REPORT","page":"Reproduce","text":"VERIFY_REPORT\n\nThe file name of the verify report verify_assets writes beside the renders: \"verify.tsv\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.VERIFY_REPORT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.IndexEntry","page":"Reproduce","text":"IndexEntry\n\nOne row of a REPRODUCE.md asset index: the paper label it serves, the asset (project-relative path or glob), its kind (:generated, :static, or :invalid for an unrecognised kind cell), the generators (project-relative paths), the configs of record they read (project-relative; from an optional config column), the data it is generated from, the provenance note (required for :static), and the index line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.IndexEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.PaperAsset","page":"Reproduce","text":"PaperAsset\n\nOne file the paper reads: path (relative to the project directory), kind (:tex for \\input/\\include, :graphics for \\includegraphics, :data for a pgfplots/tikz data file), the referencing source file and line, the \\labels of the enclosing float (inherited through \\input), and whether the file exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.PaperAsset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.PaperAssetGraph","page":"Reproduce","text":"PaperAssetGraph\n\nWhat a set of paper sources reads: the project directory root, the visited tex files (project-relative), every PaperAsset, every \\label key, and the references whose target could not be resolved statically (a macro or #1 in the path) as (source, line, text).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.PaperAssetGraph"},{"category":"type","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.ReproduceViolation","page":"Reproduce","text":"ReproduceViolation\n\nOne breach of the reproducible-figures contract: a kind (see reproduce_index_violations), the subject (an asset, generator or macro), a human-readable message and a location (file:line).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.ReproduceViolation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.assets_deployed-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Reproduce","text":"assets_deployed(outdir, paperdir, names) -> Bool\n\nWhether every rendered outdir/<name> is byte-identical to the paper's paperdir/<name>: a cheap done predicate of a deploy stage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.assets_deployed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.compare_fragments-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"compare_fragments(new, old) -> NamedTuple\n\nHow a regenerated tex fragment new differs from the submitted old (texts): identical (byte for byte); same_numbers, every coordinates block equal in order (fragment_coordinates) and every argument-free macro definition equal (a numbers file); the block counts blocks_new and blocks_old; and max_abs_diff, the largest absolute coordinate difference over blocks of equal length (Inf when the block structure differs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.compare_fragments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.declared_inputs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"declared_inputs(generator_path) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe configs / inputs a generator declares, one per reproduce: reads <path> line in its source (in any comment syntax), project-relative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.declared_inputs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.declared_outputs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"declared_outputs(generator_path) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe outputs a generator declares, one per reproduce: writes <path-or-glob> line in its source (in any comment syntax), project-relative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.declared_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.deploy_assets-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Reproduce","text":"deploy_assets(outdir, paperdir, names; replace_submitted = false) -> Vector{String}\n\nCopy each rendered outdir/<name> into paperdir when the paper has no copy or holds a different one, after verifying them all in one report (verify_assets writes a fresh outdir/verify.tsv first). A submitted copy that differs is replaced only with the author's sign-off on the difference the report states: replace_submitted = true signs off every asset of the call, a collection of names only those (per-asset sign-off, so one call deploys every asset and the report keeps every row). Without it the deploy is refused, as an error naming each differing asset and the report, and copies nothing. Returns the assets copied (an identical one is not copied).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.deploy_assets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.fragment_coordinates-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"fragment_coordinates(text) -> Vector{Vector{NTuple{2,Float64}}}\n\nEvery coordinates {…} block of a pgfplots fragment, in order, parsed to numbers: the quantitative content of a panel, for comparing a regenerated fragment with the submitted one independently of its formatting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.fragment_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.gather_numbers-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"gather_numbers(fragments, out; generator, root, root_label = \"<results_root()>\") -> out\n\nWrite the paper's numbers file out from the generators' number-macro fragments (tex files): every argument-free macro of every fragment, one \\newcommand{\\<name>}{<value>} per line, sorted by name, under a header naming the generator and each fragment as <root_label>/<path relative to root> (no machine path). Never \\providecommand: a macro the manuscript also defines must clash. A macro that two fragments define, and a fragment macro with arguments (a number is a value, not a template), are an ArgumentError.\n\nWrite out beside the generator's results, not into the paper: a submitted numbers file is replaced under the same sign-off as a figure, by deploy_assets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.gather_numbers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.macro_literal_violations-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reproduce","text":"macro_literal_violations(prose_files, macro_files; root, ignore = String[],\n                         min_integer_digits = 3) -> Vector{ReproduceViolation}\n\nBest-effort check that a number a generated macro file defines is not re-typed as a literal in the paper's prose: every argument-free macro of macro_files whose rendered value carries a number is searched for in the comment-stripped prose of prose_files, with number boundaries. A bare integer value is searched only from min_integer_digits digits (the default 3 skips the two-digit counts and percentages that coincide everywhere; 2 is strict and needs literal-ok marks). A line holding reproduce: literal-ok (in a comment) is exempt, as is any macro named in ignore.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.macro_literal_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.paper_asset_graph-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"Reproduce","text":"paper_asset_graph(tex_files; root = dirname(first(tex_files)), search_dirs = String[]) -> PaperAssetGraph\n\nEvery file a paper reads, found by following \\input/\\include/ \\subfile/\\includestandalone (recursively), \\includegraphics (with the \\graphicspath directories and the default extensions tried in LaTeX's order), and pgfplots/tikz data files (\\addplot table {file}, \\pgfplotstableread{file}, \\pgfplotstabletypeset{file}). Paths resolve against the directory of the top-level file, then against each of search_dirs in order (the compile directory and the TEXINPUTS of a build run elsewhere, e.g. [\"paper\"] for a main.tex in paper/src/ built from paper/ that writes \\input{src/sec/intro}; a relative entry is relative to root), and are reported relative to root (the project directory). Graphics try the \\graphicspath directories before search_dirs. Comments are ignored; a reference whose path holds a macro or a # parameter is recorded in unresolved, not guessed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.paper_asset_graph"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.read_reproduce_index-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"read_reproduce_index(path) -> Vector{IndexEntry}\n\nThe asset index of a REPRODUCE.md: every markdown table whose header has the columns asset, kind and generator (case-insensitive; label, config, data and provenance are read when present, any other column is ignored). A cell's backticked tokens are its values (several assets in one row expand to one entry each); a row without backticks uses the cell text. kind is the first word of its cell: generated or static.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.read_reproduce_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.reproduce_index_violations-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"reproduce_index_violations(project_dir; papers, index = \"REPRODUCE.md\", check_literals = true,\n                           ignore_macros = String[], min_integer_digits = 3,\n                           require_config = false, search_dirs = String[]) -> Vector{ReproduceViolation}\n\nThe reproducible-figures contract of a paper project, as a list of violations (empty = compliant). papers are the top-level tex files and index the REPRODUCE.md, both relative to project_dir; every asset path in the index is project-relative. search_dirs (project-relative) are the directories a reference that does not resolve against a paper's own directory is looked up in (paper_asset_graph): the compile directory of a build run outside the paper's directory. Violation kinds:\n\n:unindexed_asset — the paper reads a file no index entry covers\n:missing_asset — the paper reads a file that does not exist\n:unresolved_reference — a reference whose path is not static (macro, #1)\n:unreferenced_entry — an index entry the paper does not read (stale row)\n:invalid_kind — a kind other than generated / static\n:no_generator — a generated entry naming no generator\n:missing_generator — a named generator does not exist\n:generator_does_not_write — no named generator writes the asset: none declares it (reproduce: writes <glob>) and none mentions its file name\n:static_without_provenance — a static entry with no provenance note\n:no_config — with require_config = true, a generated entry naming no config of record (a config column cell)\n:missing_config — a named config does not exist\n:generator_does_not_read_config — no named generator reads the config: none declares it (reproduce: reads <path>) and none names its file\n:label_mismatch — the entry's label is a \\label of the paper, but not of the float the asset sits in\n:macro_literal — a number a generated macro file defines, re-typed as a literal in the prose (macro_literal_violations, with ignore_macros and min_integer_digits); off with check_literals = false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.reproduce_index_violations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.tex_macro_values-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reproduce","text":"tex_macro_values(path) -> Vector{Pair{String,String}}\n\nThe argument-free macros a tex file defines (\\newcommand, \\renewcommand, \\providecommand, \\def), as name => value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.tex_macro_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.uncited_macros-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"Reproduce","text":"uncited_macros(numbers_path, papers; root = dirname(first(papers)), search_dirs = String[],\n               allow = Dict{String,String}()) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe macros the numbers file numbers_path defines that the paper does not cite: the tex files the paper reads are the ones paper_asset_graph visits from the top-level papers (with root and search_dirs as there), comments ignored, the numbers file itself excluded; a citation is the macro name not followed by a letter (\\nA is not cited by \\nAb). The allow table (macro name without backslash => reason) exempts a macro; an allow entry naming no macro of the file is returned as stale allow: <name>. Sorted. A paper that does not read numbers_path is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.uncited_macros"},{"category":"method","location":"api/reproduce/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.verify_assets-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Reproduce","text":"verify_assets(outdir, paperdir, names) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nCompare each rendered outdir/<name> with the submitted paperdir/<name> and write the report outdir/verify.tsv (VERIFY_REPORT): one row per asset with asset, submitted, identical, same_numbers, blocks_new, blocks_old, max_abs_diff, bytes_new and bytes_old. A .tex asset is compared by its numbers as well as its bytes (compare_fragments); any other asset by its bytes. Each verdict is printed. A missing render is an error; a missing submitted copy is reported (submitted = false), not an error. Returns the rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Reproduce.verify_assets"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Defaults-and-file-formats","page":"Defaults and file formats","text":"","title":"Defaults and file formats"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Defaults-of-record","page":"Defaults and file formats","text":"Every default of the package lives in one TOML file under configs/ and is read through one function. An unknown key or tag is refused.\n\nFile Holds Read by\nconfigs/default.toml every value that changes a reported statistic, a rendered panel or a raw dataset read: the [metrics], [render], [pool.<dataset>], [gt.<dataset>.<matcher>], [datasets.<dataset>], [local_optimization], [refit] and [selection] tables the package's internal default lookup, which throws on a missing table or key\nconfigs/tuning.toml every cross-validation parameter: grid spacings, window rules, extension rules, the objective, the cross-validation and the budget draws tuning_spec (see The [tuning] schema)\nconfigs/splits.toml every scene list of the stack, named by a spec's cross_validation.scene_split and never restated registered_split\n\nDEFAULTS_PATH is the path of configs/default.toml. The file is parsed when the package is precompiled, so an edit takes effect after Julia restarts.\n\nEvery random sample is seeded by stable_seed from a spec's seed and a name, such as a scene name. See The experimental protocol.","title":"Defaults of record"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Environment-variables","page":"Defaults and file formats","text":"Two environment variables bind a paper project's data trees elsewhere than the project's own data/ directory. Nothing else in the package reads the environment.\n\nVariable Binds Otherwise\nVGTK_DATA_ROOT data_root: the read-only input tree the project's data/exp_raw (a link, a directory or the artifact view), then the project's fallback, then its ArtifactView\nVGTK_RESULTS_ROOT results_root: the add-only results tree, one directory per run key the project's data/exp_pro\n\nThe prefix is the stack's shorthand (VisualGeometryToolkit). The names CAU_DATA_ROOT and CAU_RESULTS_ROOT of earlier versions are not read.","title":"Environment variables"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#Artifact-formats","page":"Defaults and file formats","text":"Every persisted artifact carries a format stamp, the key \"<kind>_format\" in its JLD2 file. One writer stamps each kind and one reader refuses any other format. An older file is converted or regenerated; it is never read through a compatibility shim.\n\nARTIFACT_FORMATS lists the current version of each kind (:pool, :pool_residuals, :pool_samples, :pnp_pool, :tuning), the change that the version introduced and the remedy for an older file.","title":"Artifact formats"},{"category":"section","location":"configuration/#The-driver-command-line","page":"Defaults and file formats","text":"A driver reads its options as --key=value with cli_option, and calls start_output_flush! so that buffered output survives a batch job that is killed.","title":"The driver command line"},{"category":"section","location":"api/render/#API:-Render","page":"Render","text":"Figures and tables through PGFPlotsX.","title":"API: Render"},{"category":"section","location":"api/render/#Public-names","page":"Render","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/render/#Internals","page":"Render","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render","page":"Render","text":"Render\n\nFigure and table rendering through PGFPlotsX: the panel record (PanelSpec), a paper's styling as one value (PaperStyle), the faceted-arm grammar (arm_style), the house axis style (HOUSE_AXIS_COMMON), the shared faceted group-plot emitter (open_faceted_groupplot) and the panel and table emitters (emit_miscal_faceted, emit_basin_faceted, emit_tuning_table), and the table-file helpers (fmt3, write_tsv, read_tsv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.FAMILY_ORDER","page":"Render","text":"The loss-family facet order, left to right, of every faceted figure and legend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.FAMILY_ORDER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_AXIS_COMMON","page":"Render","text":"HOUSE_AXIS_COMMON\n\nThe common axis-option string every NEW (post-ACCV) panel starts from: no grids, plain-number tick labels on log axes, house fonts and legend style. Panel-specific options (labels, limits, modes) are appended after.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_AXIS_COMMON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_LINE_WIDTH","page":"Render","text":"House default line width for curve plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_LINE_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_MARK_SIZE","page":"Render","text":"House default marker size for curve plots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.HOUSE_MARK_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PANELS","page":"Render","text":"PANELS\n\nThe summary panels, keyed by id: (id, yfn, ylabel, log_y, y_spacing, y_minor_subdiv). y_spacing is the major-tick (labeled) gradation on a linear y-axis (NaN = off); y_minor_subdiv the number of minor ticks BETWEEN majors (0 = none). eR_mean is a legacy id: for E and F the metric is the imc POSE error max(Δrot,Δtrans); a homography's imc is the decomposition's rotation alone (VgtkCore pool_imc on an H pool), so an H panel overrides the label (build_axis(...; ylabel)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PANELS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PATH_RANK","page":"Render","text":"The within-family sort rank of each optimization path (arm_style_records' path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PATH_RANK"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PanelSpec-Tuple{PanelSpec}","page":"Render","text":"PanelSpec(base::PanelSpec; overrides...) -> PanelSpec\n\nCopy base with the given fields replaced (non-mutating derivation, e.g. PanelSpec(spec; xlabel = x_label)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PanelSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PaperStyle","page":"Render","text":"PaperStyle(; colors = Pair{String,String}[], rows = [], arms = Dict(),\n           grid, zeta_x, zeta_med_y, zeta_maa_y, med_ylabel, maa_ylabel)\n\nA paper's figure style, built once by the paper (accv2026 builds it from its palette, arm table and figure-style blocks) and given as the first argument of every renderer that draws arms or paper colours.\n\nFields:\n\ncolors::Dict{String,String}: colour NAME => hex \"RRGGBB\" — the paper palette (default_palette is the house base) plus every arm colour whose record carries a hex; emitted by paper_color_defs.\nrows::Vector{Tuple{String,String,String,String,Bool}}: the summary-panel rows of build_axis, in legend order: (method key, display label, colour name, mark, dashed).\narms::Dict{String,NamedTuple}: the faceted-arm style records, arm_style_records — family, path, scale, label, colour, hex, marker, dashed, shade.\naxes::NamedTuple: the paper's axis blocks of the faceted emitters — grid (the grid options spliced into the budget/basin/time panels; \"\" is the house rule, no grid), zeta_x (x block of the ζ panels), zeta_med_y (a function (ymin; ymax, ytick_spacing) -> String, the median row's y block), zeta_maa_y (the mAA row's y block), med_ylabel and maa_ylabel (the two rows' y labels).\n\nA paper whose figures were frozen at an earlier style (the ACCV panels are byte-compared) supplies those exact blocks here; nothing in this package defaults to them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.PaperStyle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_addplot-Tuple{IO, Any, Any, Bool, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"arm_addplot(io, color, marker, dashed, coords; line_width = \"0.8pt\")\n\nOne per-arm curve: \\addplot[<arm_plot_opts>, forget plot] coordinates {<coords>}; (legends are image-only — see arm_legendimage).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_addplot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_legendimage-Tuple{IO, Any, Any, Bool, Any}","page":"Render","text":"arm_legendimage(io, color, marker, dashed, label;\n                line_width = \"0.8pt\", mark_size = nothing)\n\nOne legend entry: \\addlegendimage{color=…, mark=…, mark options={solid,fill=…}, line width=…[, mark size=…][, dashed]}\\addlegendentry{…}. The faceted 3×2 panels omit mark size in legend images (historical bytes); the paired layout forwards its plot options (mark_size = \"1.6pt\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_legendimage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_order-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"arm_order(style, key) -> Tuple\n\nThe within-facet sort key of arm key of style: its ramp step (shade) when the paper declares one, else its optimization path's rank; then its label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_plot_opts-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"arm_plot_opts(color, marker; dashed = false, line_width = \"0.8pt\",\n              mark_size = \"1.6pt\") -> String\n\nThe shared per-arm plot options: color=…, mark=…, mark options={solid,fill=…}, line width=…, mark size=…[, dashed].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_plot_opts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_record-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"Full style record (NamedTuple) of arm key of style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_record"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_style-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"Style tuple (family, label, color, marker, dashed) of arm key of style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_style_records-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"arm_style_records(probes, table) -> Dict{String,NamedTuple}\n\nThe faceted-arm style records of a paper, keyed by arm: one record (family, path, scale, label, color, hex, marker, dashed, shade) per arm_key => probe_instance pair of probes (one LO instance per deployed arm; ctor kwargs immaterial, the identity traits are type/variant-level), with table[arm_key] the paper-owned part, (color = …, [hex = …], [shade = …], [scale = …]): the colour NAME, optionally its hex (a PaperStyle defines it under that name), the arm's step on its family's light→dark colour ramp (shade, 1 = lightest; it fixes the within-facet legend/plot order) and an optional scale tag, which must agree with the strategy's scale_interface. family, path, label, marker and dashed (FixedScale ⇒ dashed) DERIVE from the probe's identity traits. Errors on a probe with unspecified identity traits (registry hygiene).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.arm_style_records"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.budget_curves-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"Render","text":"budget_curves(files; arms) -> (; budgets, med, maa, oracle_med, oracle_maa, outcomes)\n\nThe statistics of a budget cell from its per-scene budget files (the scene schema above), for each of arms and the oracle, at every LO checkpoint (budgets = the files' lo_counts; the oracle read off its dense batch grid at those budgets):\n\nmed — the mean over scenes of the per-scene finite median (Shekhovtsov's mean-median; a scene without a finite error is skipped);\nmaa — the eligibility-ruled mAA (eligible_maa) POOLED over every pair of every scene — every stored pool pair is eligible, a pair whose run left no model (non-finite error) scores 0.\n\nEvery file must carry the same count axes and every one of arms. outcomes[arm] is pair_outcomes at the last checkpoint (why runs fail).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.budget_curves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.build_axis-Tuple{PaperStyle, Any, Any, String, PanelSpec}","page":"Render","text":"build_axis(style::PaperStyle, panel, data, tune::String, spec::PanelSpec; ylabel = \"\")\n\nThe PGFPlots axis of one (PANELS entry, tuning-criterion label tune): one curve per row of style.rows present in data (load_results). Every display option comes from spec; spec.xlabel === nothing is \"Validation set size\". ylabel overrides the panel's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.build_axis"},{"category":"function","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.color_defs","page":"Render","text":"color_defs(pairs; command = \"definecolor\") -> String\n\nThe colour-definition emitter: one \\<command>{<name>}{HTML}{<hex>} line per name => \"RRGGBB\" pair, newline-separated (no trailing newline). command is \"definecolor\" or \"providecolor\" (idempotent; a host's own definition wins).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.color_defs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.default_palette-Tuple{}","page":"Render","text":"default_palette() -> Vector{Pair{String,String}}\n\nThe house base palette (name => hex, ordered): the standing selector/ reference colors every paper starts from — RGB-primary \"ours\" hues (Jeffreys red e6194b, IG-Robust green 3cb44b, IG-MLE blue 4363d8), baselines chosen so none competes with Jeffreys red (RANSAC pastel pink ffb6c1, MSAC yellow ffe119, GaU cyan 42d4f4, MAGSAC++ orange f58231), and the medium-gray Oracle 808080. Per-arm FAMILY shades (Welsch greens / TLS oranges / GM purples — the faceted grammar's hue-= family rule) are paper-assigned on top of this base in the paper's PaperStyle; papers override or extend freely — this table is the documented default, not a constraint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.default_palette"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_basin_faceted-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_basin_faceted(style::PaperStyle, bundle, out; row_label = \"\", succ, deploy,\n                   exclude = Set{String}(), legend_name = \"basinlegend\",\n                   panels = [(f, panel_title(f)) for f in FAMILY_ORDER],\n                   minsucc = [render] min_success_for_cond_mean, seed_key = \"seed\",\n                   rows = [:succ, :cond])\n\nEmit the δ-perturbation basin faceted groupplot body to out from the ladder bundle (errs [pairs × nδ × nmethod], methods, deltas, refinable). rows selects the statistic per row, in order: :succ — success rate P(e≤succ°); :maa — mAA@succ° under the eligibility rule (eligible_maa: the refinable pairs are the eligible ones, a failed refit — stored as NaN — scores 0); :cond — conditional mean final error given success, masked below success rate minsucc. Success is recomputed at succ degrees; the seed_key method draws as the dashed reference (its success rate, mAA, or unconditional median per row). deploy/exclude/legend_name as in emit_miscal_faceted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_basin_faceted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_budget_faceted_curves-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_budget_faceted_curves(style::PaperStyle, out; row_label = \"\", budgets, med, maa,\n                           med_ylabel, legend_name, deploy, xticks = nothing,\n                           maa_ylabel = style.axes.maa_ylabel,\n                           panels = [(f, panel_title(f)) for f in FAMILY_ORDER],\n                           xlabel = raw\"sample budget\", xlog = true, rows = [:med, :maa],\n                           oracle_med = nothing, oracle_maa = nothing,\n                           oracle_label = \"oracle\")\n\nThe objective-faceted accuracy-vs-budget groupplot, from PRECOMPUTED per-arm curves (budget_curves for a budget cell's scene files; the PnP budget's own aggregation): med[arm] (median error, row label med_ylabel) and maa[arm] (mAA@10°) are vectors aligned with budgets; arms are keys of style.arms, and the grid options are style.axes.grid. xlog draws a log₂ budget axis with plain-number ticks at the given budgets (small budget grids such as 5…2000); the y ranges are derived from the curves. oracle_med/oracle_maa (aligned with budgets) draw the oracle reference line when given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_budget_faceted_curves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_budget_time_panel-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_budget_time_panel(style::PaperStyle, out; row_label = \"\", budgets, maa, percall_us, legend_name,\n                       xmax, xticks, maamax, panels = [(f, panel_title(f)) for f in FAMILY_ORDER])\n\nThe COMPUTE-EFFICIENCY companion of a budget panel: mAA (maa[arm], aligned with budgets, from budget_curves) against the cumulative LO time per pair [ms] — at checkpoint j, j × the arm's deterministic single-thread per-call time percall_us[arm] [µs] (one LO per pair per checkpoint). LO cost only: scoring, sampling and termination are shared and excluded (the caption must keep that scoping); the oracle runs no LO and has no curve. xmax / xticks / maamax are the panel's fixed axis constants. Every arm of maa must have a per-call time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_budget_time_panel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_lo_timing_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_lo_timing_table(figdir; timedir, arm_rows, dataset_names,\n                     rider_labels, filename = \"table_lo_timing.tex\")\n\nEmit the LO per-call wall-clock table (<figdir>/<filename>) from the lo_timing_*.jld2 files under timedir. arm_rows is the ordered [(display_label, run_tag), …] row table; dataset_names maps dataset tag → display name; rider_labels maps a rider method Symbol (a non-:ig column of a run) → its arm label, nothing to skip. Each run's :ig column is keyed by the run tag itself (the timing driver's tags ARE the base names, so a roster extension cannot silently KeyError this emitter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_lo_timing_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_faceted-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_miscal_faceted(style::PaperStyle, sidecar, out; row_label = \"\", oracle, oracle_maa,\n                    oracle_class = \"E\", ymin, deploy, ymax = 6, ytick_spacing = 1,\n                    exclude = Set{String}(), legend_name = \"miscallegend\",\n                    panels = [(f, panel_title(f)) for f in FAMILY_ORDER],\n                    arm_filter = a -> true, rows = [:med, :maa])\n\nEmit the ζ-miscalibration faceted groupplot body to out from the tuning artifact sidecar (zetas + evaluation): the per-arm evaluation_curves through emit_miscal_faceted_curves. Rows: median pose error (oracle FLOOR oracle) and mAA@10° (oracle CEILING oracle_maa); the median row's y block is style.axes.zeta_med_y(ymin; ymax, ytick_spacing), ymin its y floor (string, TeX-interpolated), ymax/ytick_spacing its ceiling and tick spacing (default 6°/1°). deploy wraps in a tikzpicture and leaves the legend to the host (\\pgfplotslegendfromname{<legend_name>}); exclude drops arm keys from THIS render only; arm_filter restricts the roster (e.g. one scoring selector).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_faceted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_faceted_curves-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_miscal_faceted_curves(style::PaperStyle, out; zetas, med, maa, deploy, legend_name, rows,\n                           oracle = nothing, oracle_maa = nothing,\n                           oracle_class = \"\",\n                           x_axis = style.axes.zeta_x,\n                           med_axis = style.axes.zeta_med_y(\"0.4\"),\n                           maa_axis = style.axes.zeta_maa_y,\n                           med_ylabel = style.axes.med_ylabel,\n                           maa_ylabel = style.axes.maa_ylabel,\n                           panels = [(f, panel_title(f)) for f in FAMILY_ORDER],\n                           flat = Set{String}())\n\nThe objective-faceted ζ groupplot from PRECOMPUTED per-arm curves: med[arm] and maa[arm] are vectors aligned with zetas (NaN = masked point); arms are keys of style.arms, so family, mechanism order, label, color and marker all derive from the style. Arms in flat carry a single value that is drawn as a constant line across zetas (width-free controls). The oracle line spans zetas and is drawn only when oracle is given. Axis blocks default to the style's; supply other blocks for other error scales.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_faceted_curves"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_summary_table-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_miscal_summary_table(style::PaperStyle, path; tracks, stat = :med, exclude = Set{String}(),\n                          arm_filter = a -> true, label = a -> arm_record(style, a).label)\n\nThe miscalibration summary table of a set of cells, from their sidecars (the same evaluations the faceted panels draw, so table and figure agree by construction). tracks = [(title, [(column_name, sidecar), …]), …]: one booktabs block per track, rows = the union of the track's arms of style in the faceted order, per dataset three columns — calibration, the deflation extreme and the inflation extreme of the ladder — with the best value per column bold (min for :med, max for :maa) and -- where a cell lacks the arm. Returns path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_miscal_summary_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_tuning_table-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"emit_tuning_table(path, out; axes, labels, stage = :coarse, fmt = \"%.3f\") -> out\n\ntuning_slice as a LaTeX tabular written to out: one axis, a two-column curve (value, criterion); two axes, a surface (rows the first axis, columns the second). labels names the axes in the header; fmt formats the axis values. The argmin cell is starred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.emit_tuning_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.evaluation_curve-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"evaluation_curve(evaluation, arm, stat) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nOne arm's curve over the columns of a deployed evaluation (evaluation[arm], pairs × ζ): stat of each column — finite_median for the median row, eligible_maa for the mAA row (the eligibility rule: every stored pair is eligible, a pair without a model scores 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.evaluation_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.faceted_legend_opts-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"faceted_legend_opts(name; columns = 4) -> String\n\nThe shared legend to name axis-option suffix (prepend to the first cell's \\nextgroupplot[...] options): one shared legend drawn by the host via \\pgfplotslegendfromname{<name>}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.faceted_legend_opts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.family_of-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"Loss family (:welsch/:tls/:gm) of arm key of style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.family_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.family_rank-Tuple{Any}","page":"Render","text":"family_rank(family) -> Int\n\nThe facet position of a loss family in FAMILY_ORDER (1 = leftmost); a family outside it ranks after every facet. The facet-walk key of tables and vocabularies that list arms in figure order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.family_rank"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.fmt3-Tuple{Real}","page":"Render","text":"fmt3(x; nonfinite = nothing) -> String\n\nA number cell: x with three decimals (\"%.3f\"). A non-finite x reads as Julia's own spelling (\"NaN\", \"Inf\", \"-Inf\") by default, or as nonfinite (a String) when one is given: a report that collapses every non-finite value to one text gives it as nonfinite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.fmt3"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.has_arm-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"Is key a faceted arm of style?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.has_arm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.house_colors","page":"Render","text":"house_colors(pairs::Vector{<:Pair}) -> String\n\n\\definecolor block (color_defs) from name => \"RRGGBB\" pairs, newline-terminated — the one way a house figure loads its color registry (names come from the paper's arm/legend vocabulary; one color per arm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.house_colors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.lo_us_per_call-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Render","text":"lo_us_per_call(res, m) -> Float64\n\nThe mean warm LO wall clock per call [µs] of method column m of a budget result (anything carrying lo_ns_total / lo_calls_total per method — a budget engine's result or a run record read back from its sidecar); NaN when the column made no LO call. A pure accessor on persisted data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.lo_us_per_call"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.load_results-Tuple{String}","page":"Render","text":"load_results(path) -> Dict{Tuple{String,String}, SubDataFrame}\n\nLoad a results JLD2 carrying a summary DataFrame (the rows Evaluation.compute_method_rows emits — LocalOptimizationDoneRight's plotlosensitivity writes it as summary.jld2) and bucket that summary into one SubDataFrame per (method_key, tuning_criterion) for the PGFPlotsX renderer.  Method key strips a trailing (median|mAA) suffix so the style rows' bare keys (\"RANSAC\") match.\n\nEach sub-DataFrame is sorted by n so downstream rendering can index column vectors directly (s.nn, s.mean_med, s.lo95_mean_med, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.load_results"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.miscal_summary_rows-Tuple{PaperStyle, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"miscal_summary_rows(style::PaperStyle, sidecar; stat = :med, exclude = Set{String}(),\n                    arm_filter = a -> true)\n    -> (arms, rows, zetas)\n\nThe deployed evaluation of a miscalibration sidecar summarized at three points of its ζ ladder — the deflation extreme, calibration (ζ nearest 1) and the inflation extreme — per arm of style, in the faceted order (facet, then ramp step). stat is :med (finite median of the imc error, deg) or :maa (the eligibility rule, eligible_maa). rows[arm] = (deflation, calibration, inflation); zetas the three ζ values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.miscal_summary_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.open_faceted_groupplot-Tuple{IO}","page":"Render","text":"open_faceted_groupplot(io; cols, rows, width, height, opts,\n                       hsep = \"6pt\", vsep = \"12pt\", edge_labels = true)\n\nEmit the shared groupplot preamble:\n\n\\begin{groupplot}[\n  group style={group size=<cols> by <rows>, horizontal sep=<hsep>[, vertical sep=<vsep>,\n    xticklabels at=edge bottom, xlabels at=edge bottom]},\n  scale only axis, width=<width>, height=<height>,\n  <opts[1]>,\n  ...\n  <opts[end]>]\n\nopts are the remaining option LINES (each printed at 2-space indent; the last gets the closing ]). edge_labels=false drops the edge-tick lines AND the vertical sep (the 1-row paired layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.open_faceted_groupplot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.panel_title-Tuple{Any}","page":"Render","text":"Two-line panel title (TeX) for a loss family.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.panel_title"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.paper_color_defs-Tuple{PaperStyle}","page":"Render","text":"paper_color_defs(style::PaperStyle) -> String\n\nThe \\providecolor{<name>}{HTML}{<HEX>} block of every colour of style (sorted by colour name, hex upper-cased), through the one colour emitter color_defs — what a standalone preview prepends so it is self-contained for any roster (\\providecolor is idempotent and the paper's own definitions win).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.paper_color_defs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.paper_color_name-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"paper_color_name(key) -> String\n\nThe LaTeX colour name of a method key: \"Color\" + the key's alphanumerics (ALL others stripped, so keys with spaces/+ such as \"IG  sel\", \"MSAC +LO\" yield valid names).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.paper_color_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.path_rank-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"Within-family optimization-path sort rank of arm key of style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.path_rank"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.pgf_coords-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"pgf_coords(xs, ys; fmt = \"(%.6f,%.4f)\") -> String\n\nThe pgfplots coordinates {…} body of a curve: one fmt-formatted (x,y) per point with a FINITE y (a non-finite point is dropped — pgfplots cannot draw it), space-separated. fmt is a Printf format of the two coordinates (\"(%d,%.4f)\" for an integer budget axis).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.pgf_coords"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.read_tsv-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"read_tsv(path) -> Vector{Dict{String,String}}\n\nThe rows of a tab-separated table as dictionaries keyed by the header cells. Blank lines and # comment lines are skipped; the first remaining line is the header, and cells are strings (a row shorter than the header lacks the trailing keys).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.read_tsv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.tuning_slice-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"tuning_slice(path; axes, stage = :coarse) -> (; axes, xs, values, argmin)\n\nThe objective of a coarse-fine tuning artifact (Evaluation.save_coarse_fine_artifact) over the stage (:coarse or :fine) product grid, restricted to axes (one or two axis names) through that grid's argmin on every other axis: xs[i] the grid of axes[i], values the criterion's reported value (the mAA for a mean_average_accuracy criterion, else the objective) over them, argmin the index of the stage's argmin within the slice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.tuning_slice"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.write_article_preview-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Render","text":"write_article_preview(out_dir, panel_base, colordefs; legend_ref = \"\")\n\nWrite an article-class preview.tex in out_dir that \\inputs the panel fragment panel_base (its filename WITHOUT .tex), then compile it to PDF with latexmk when it is installed. colordefs is the \\providecolor block for the panel's colours; legend_ref names a legend to \\ref below the panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.write_article_preview"},{"category":"method","location":"api/render/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.write_tsv-Tuple{AbstractString, Any, Any}","page":"Render","text":"write_tsv(path, header, rows; comments = String[]) -> path\n\nWrite a tab-separated table: each of comments as a #-prefixed line, then the header cells, then one line per row of rows (each cell through string), lines ended by \\n. The parent directory is created. read_tsv reads it back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Render.write_tsv"},{"category":"section","location":"api/datasets/#API:-Datasets","page":"Datasets","text":"Raw dataset readers.","title":"API: Datasets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/datasets/#Public-names","page":"Datasets","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/datasets/#Internals","page":"Datasets","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets","page":"Datasets","text":"Datasets\n\nRaw dataset readers. Each returns VisualGeometryToolkitCore StereoRawPairs in pixels, from which a scoring pool is drawn: PhotoTourism (load_pt_pairs), the homography benchmark HEB (load_heb_pairs) and consolidated RoMa or SuperPoint+LightGlue correspondences (load_roma_pairs). The module also produces those consolidated files from depth-validated stereo datasets (ETH3D, ScanNet1500, KITTI, SevenScenes) through stereo_pairs, load_sparse_matches, load_validation_data and consolidate_csponds.\n\nThe read thresholds default to configs/default.toml ([pool.<dataset>]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.CONSOLIDATED_FORMAT","page":"Datasets","text":"The version of the consolidated csponds record consolidate_csponds writes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.CONSOLIDATED_FORMAT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PT_SCENES","page":"Datasets","text":"PT_SCENES :: Vector{Tuple{String,Symbol}}\n\nThe PhotoTourism scenes with both matches (matches.h5 + match_conf.h5) and a COLMAP reconstruction on disk, each with its source tag: which tree of PtRawTrees holds them. The scene SPLITS are configs/splits.toml's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PT_SCENES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.AbstractStereoDataset","page":"Datasets","text":"AbstractStereoDataset\n\nA depth-validated stereo benchmark: a value carrying its on-disk root, on which stereo_pairs, load_validation_data and gt_config dispatch, and which consolidate_csponds drives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.AbstractStereoDataset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.ETH3D","page":"Datasets","text":"ETH3D(scenes_root; min_covisibility = [datasets.eth3d] min_covisibility)\n\nThe ETH3D high-resolution multi-view benchmark with laser depth. scenes_root holds one directory per scene with images/dslr_images_undistorted/, dslr_calibration_undistorted/, dslr_calibration_jpg/ and ground_truth_depth/dslr_images/. A pair needs min_covisibility co-visible triangulated points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.ETH3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.GTConfig","page":"Datasets","text":"GTConfig(; gt_method, metric_tol, rel_tol, bidirectional)\nGTConfig(table::AbstractString)\n\nHow correspondences are validated against ground-truth depth and pose (VisualGeometryToolkitCore validate_gt_inliers): gt_method (a provenance label, :depth_warp_eth3d_laser), metric_tol (the 3D disagreement at the surface below which a correspondence is an inlier, a length quantity: metric, so one number is portable across resolutions and focal lengths), rel_tol (the relative depth-consistency check applied first) and bidirectional (check both warp directions).\n\nGTConfig(table) reads the values of record of [<table>] in configs/default.toml (\"gt.eth3d.splg\"; metric_tol_mm in millimetres).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.GTConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.HebPair","page":"Datasets","text":"HebPair <: StereoRawPair\n\nOne raw HEB pair, in pixels: csponds, SNN ratios and the dataset's GT inlier flags (post-SNN filter), the pixel calibrations, image sizes and the extrinsic GT relative pose x₂ = R·x₁ + t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.HebPair"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.KITTI","page":"Datasets","text":"KITTI(root; sequences = [datasets.kitti] sequences)\n\nThe KITTI odometry benchmark, consecutive left-grayscale (image_0) frames. root holds sequences/<seq>/{image_0,velodyne,calib.txt} and poses/<seq>.txt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.KITTI"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PtPair","page":"Datasets","text":"PtPair <: StereoRawPair\n\nRaw PhotoTourism pair data (post-SNN filter), in pixels: csps from matches.h5, COLMAP intrinsics, the composed relative pose, the GT-inlier mask (pixel Sampson under the GT F), and the SfM-track-paired SIFT detections (from points3D.bin) — the σ̂²_p noise-scale source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PtPair"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PtRawTrees","page":"Datasets","text":"PtRawTrees(; matches_tutorial_val, matches_tutorial_train, matches_ef_test,\n             recon_tutorial, recon_ef_test, recon_special = Dict{String,String}())\n\nThe raw PhotoTourism trees, supplied by the caller. This package ships no data directory to default to — the rule the HEB and RoMa readers follow (their files are arguments), and which PT alone used to break: these five trees were once absolute paths on one machine. Two consequences, both real: a registered package carried one machine's paths, so PT pools could not be generated anywhere else; and a pool producer cell could not DECLARE its raw inputs in _provenance.toml, because no config key named them — every other input of the accv2026 campaign is declared and hashed, and PT-E's was not.\n\nmatches_* hold <scene>/{matches.h5,match_conf.h5}; recon_* hold <scene>/dense/sparse COLMAP models. recon_special maps a scene to a COMPLETE recon directory (it already ends at the model), for the IMC-PT TestData scenes whose model does not sit under recon_ef_test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.PtRawTrees"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.RomaPair","page":"Datasets","text":"RomaPair <: StereoRawPair\n\nOne raw RoMa / SP+LG pair, in pixels: csponds, snn = 1 − confidence, the consolidated GT inlier mask, the pixel calibrations, image sizes and the GT relative pose, with its source scene (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's pair_scene). A raw pair is a pool's input, so it is plain data: t_gt is plain metres (the consolidated record strips the dataset's rig once).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.RomaPair"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.ScanNet1500","page":"Datasets","text":"ScanNet1500(root)\n\nThe ScanNet-1500 evaluation split (1500 pairs over 100 scenes; the pair list of LoFTR and RoMa). root holds test.npz and scans_test/<scene>/{color,depth,intrinsic}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.ScanNet1500"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.SevenScenes","page":"Datasets","text":"SevenScenes(root; split = :test, scene = :all, interval, stride)\n\nMicrosoft 7-Scenes (Kinect). root holds <scene>/<sequence>/frame-*.{color,depth}.png and frame-*.pose.txt. split is :test or :train (SuperANSAC's sequence lists, [datasets.seven_scenes]); scene is :all or one scene name; the pairs are (frame[i], frame[i + interval]) for i = 1, 1 + stride, ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.SevenScenes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.StereoPair","page":"Datasets","text":"StereoPair\n\nOne image pair of a AbstractStereoDataset: the scene, the two image names (source_name, target_name) and paths (source_img_path, target_img_path, under the dataset's root), and the GT rig (VisualGeometryToolkitCore.StereoRig) in the frame of the images the matcher sees, its translations FlexUnits lengths in metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.StereoPair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.consolidate_csponds-Tuple{AbstractStereoDataset, Symbol}","page":"Datasets","text":"consolidate_csponds(ds::AbstractStereoDataset, matcher::Symbol; match_dir, out_dir,\n                    scenes = nothing) -> Vector{String}\n\nConsolidate the sparse matches of every pair of ds (one load_sparse_matches file <match_dir>/<scene>__<src>__<tgt>.h5 per pair; a pair without one is skipped) into <out_dir>/<scene>.jld2, one file per scene (a / in a scene id becomes __), each pair's GT inlier mask from load_validation_data under gt_config(ds, matcher). scenes, when given, restricts the run to those scene ids. Returns the paths written. The record is documented in src/datasets/consolidate.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.consolidate_csponds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.eth3d_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"eth3d_pairs(scenes_root; min_covisibility = [datasets.eth3d] min_covisibility) -> Vector{StereoPair}\n\nEvery unordered pair of undistorted DSLR images of every scene under scenes_root with at least min_covisibility co-visible triangulated points (SuperANSAC's ETH3D protocol), its rig from the undistorted calibration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.eth3d_pairs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.gt_config","page":"Datasets","text":"gt_config(ds::AbstractStereoDataset, matcher::Symbol) -> GTConfig\n\nThe GT-validation tolerances of record for (dataset, matcher) (:roma, :splg): configs/default.toml [gt.<dataset>.<matcher>]. A combination without a calibrated table is a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.gt_config"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.kitti_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"kitti_pairs(root; sequences = [datasets.kitti] sequences) -> Vector{StereoPair}\n\nThe cam0 frame pairs of each KITTI odometry sequence under root with poses, by the [datasets.kitti] pair rule (consecutive frames). A sequence missing its directory or pose file is skipped with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.kitti_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_heb_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"load_heb_pairs(h5_path; min_csponds, max_pairs_per_scene, subsample_overdraw,\n               subsample_seed, snn_threshold = [pool.heb] snn_threshold) -> Vector{HebPair}\n\nIterate all corr_* groups of the raw HEB scene file h5_path that have a pose and both calibrations, in a seeded subsample (_seeded_key_subsample, keyed by the file name) of at most max_pairs_per_scene kept pairs; apply the SNN threshold and drop pairs with fewer than min_csponds correspondences. The subsample sizes and seed are the caller's (its pool configuration).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_heb_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_pt_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString, Symbol}","page":"Datasets","text":"load_pt_pairs(scene, source; trees::PtRawTrees, min_csponds, max_pairs_per_scene,\n              subsample_overdraw, subsample_seed,\n              snn_threshold = [pool.phototourism] snn_threshold,\n              min_track_pairs = [pool.phototourism] min_track_pairs,\n              gt_inlier_sampson_px = [pool.phototourism] gt_inlier_sampson_px) -> Vector{PtPair}\n\nResolve the scene's (matchesdir, recondir) via _scene_paths, read the scene's COLMAP recon once, then iterate over matches.h5 keys (with the seeded subsample _seeded_key_subsample to max_pairs_per_scene) and assemble PtPairs (_load_pt_pair). Drops pairs with too few SfM-track-paired observations to give a stable σ̂²p (`mintrack_pairs). The subsample sizes and seed are the caller's (its pool configuration); the read thresholds default to configs/default.toml[pool.phototourism]`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_pt_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_roma_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"load_roma_pairs(path) -> Vector{RomaPair}\n\nEvery pair of one consolidated RoMa / SP+LG csponds scene file (<dir>/<scene>.jld2), in pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_roma_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_sparse_matches-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"load_sparse_matches(path) -> (; csponds, confidence)\n\nThe correspondences (pixels, Float32 as written) and the per-match confidence (higher = better) of one sparse-match HDF5 file of the match extractor, RoMa v2 or SuperPoint + LightGlue by its matcher attribute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_sparse_matches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_validation_data","page":"Datasets","text":"load_validation_data(ds::AbstractStereoDataset, p::StereoPair, csponds)\n    -> (; rig, csponds, depth_src, depth_tgt)\n\nWhat GT validation of csponds (pixels of the images p names) needs: the rig and correspondences in the frame of the depth maps, and the two depth maps (metres, NaN invalid). Only ETH3D changes frame — its laser depth lives in the distorted DSLR images, its matches in the undistorted ones; every other dataset returns p.rig and csponds unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.load_validation_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.pt_scene_files-Tuple{AbstractString, PtRawTrees}","page":"Datasets","text":"pt_scene_files(scene, trees::PtRawTrees) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe raw files load_pt_pairs opens for PhotoTourism scene (one of PT_SCENES), under the caller's trees: its matches.h5 and match_conf.h5 and the COLMAP model's cameras.bin, images.bin and points3D.bin. The one statement of the reader's path rule outside the reader (the data-artifact catalog derives its file lists from it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.pt_scene_files"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.scannet1500_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"scannet1500_pairs(root) -> Vector{StereoPair}\n\nThe 1500 ScanNet evaluation pairs of <root>/test.npz, each scene's calibration from scans_test/<scene>/intrinsic/intrinsic_color.txt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.scannet1500_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.seven_scenes_pairs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"seven_scenes_pairs(root; split = :test, scene = :all, interval, stride) -> Vector{StereoPair}\n\nThe (frame[i], frame[i + interval]) pairs, i = 1, 1 + stride, …, of every sequence of split (:test, :train) under root, for every scene or the one named by scene. The scene id of a pair is \"<scene>/<sequence>\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.seven_scenes_pairs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.stereo_pairs","page":"Datasets","text":"stereo_pairs(ds::AbstractStereoDataset) -> Vector{StereoPair}\n\nEvery evaluation pair of the dataset, by the dataset's own pair rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets.stereo_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._load_pt_pair-Tuple{HDF5.File, HDF5.File, NamedTuple, String}","page":"Datasets","text":"_load_pt_pair(matches_f, conf_f, recon, pair_key; snn_threshold, min_csponds,\n             gt_inlier_sampson_px = [pool.phototourism] gt_inlier_sampson_px)\n    -> PtPair | nothing\n\nLoad one pair from already-open matches/conf HDF5 handles plus the per-scene recon = (cameras, images, track_index) (built once per scene). Apply the SNN filter (match_conf < snn_threshold; 0=good, 1=bad), extract SfM-track-paired SIFT detections, compose the GT relative pose from the COLMAP per-image (q, t), and mark the GT inliers (pixel Sampson under the GT F below gt_inlier_sampson_px).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._load_pt_pair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._row_major_pose-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Datasets","text":"_row_major_pose(v) -> (R::SMatrix{3,3,Float64,9}, t::SVector{3,Float64})\n\nThe rotation block and the last column of a 3×4 matrix [R | t] stored ROW-MAJOR as 12 numbers (R₁₁ R₁₂ R₁₃ t₁ R₂₁ … t₃) — the layout of the HEB pose_* datasets (and of KITTI's calib.txt/poses/*.txt and ScanNet-1500's rel_pose). The one parser of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._row_major_pose"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._seeded_key_subsample-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractString}","page":"Datasets","text":"_seeded_key_subsample(keys, tag; max_pairs_per_scene, subsample_overdraw, subsample_seed) -> keys′\n\nThe per-scene pair subsample of the raw readers: when the scene has more pair keys than max_pairs_per_scene, a seeded subsample_overdraw × overdraw (shuffle under POOL_RNG(stable_seed(subsample_seed, tag)), order preserved) that the reader trims to the cap after its own rejections; otherwise every key. Stable across Julia versions: the seed is a fixed SHA-256 derivation and the stream StableRNGs'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._seeded_key_subsample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/datasets/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._snn_filtered_matches-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{Float32}, Real}","page":"Datasets","text":"_snn_filtered_matches(m, snn, snn_threshold) -> (csponds, snn_kept, kept)\n\nThe SNN filter of every raw reader: keep the matches with snn < snn_threshold (SNN = Lowe's dNN/d2NN, low = good; a match AT the threshold is dropped). m holds one match per column with the pixel coordinates in rows 1–4 (x₁ y₁ x₂ y₂); returns the kept pixel csponds, their SNN, and the kept column indices (for any per-match column the reader carries along).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Datasets._snn_filtered_matches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/tuning/#API:-Tuning","page":"Tuning","text":"The cross-validation specification. The schema is described in The [tuning] schema.","title":"API: Tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"api/tuning/#Public-names","page":"Tuning","text":"","title":"Public names"},{"category":"section","location":"api/tuning/#Internals","page":"Tuning","text":"These names are not public; they are listed for contributors.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning","page":"Tuning","text":"Tuning\n\nThe cross-validation (CV) specification. It holds the grid spacings and the grid constructor grid, the window rules, the persisted grid specs and their extension (extend), the objectives (TuningCriterion), the scene splits and unit samplings of a CrossValidation, the validation-budget draws (BudgetSampling), the schemes that bundle them (TuningScheme), the [tuning] TOML parser and writer (tuning_spec, tuning_table), the tuning-artifact writer and reader (save_tuning_artifact, load_tuning_artifact), the scale-prior strategies (PriorSpec) and the deployment corruption of the miscalibration protocol (corrupt_native).\n\nEvery strategy is a type whose fields are its complete parameter set, and its defaults live in configs/tuning.toml.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ALL_PRIOR_SPECS","page":"Tuning","text":"ALL_PRIOR_SPECS :: Tuple of canonical (family, fitting) specs probed + evaluated.\n\nSingle source of truth for the prior specs a sweep iterates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ALL_PRIOR_SPECS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RNG_ALGORITHMS","page":"Tuning","text":"RNG_ALGORITHMS\n\nThe RNG algorithms a spec may name (rng = \"StableRNG\"): StableRNGs' StableRNG only — its streams are fixed across Julia versions, which Julia's own MersenneTwister/Xoshiro are not, so a persisted sample or stream reproduces on any Julia. (MersenneTwister and Xoshiro were accepted until tuning format v4.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RNG_ALGORITHMS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SPLIT_REGISTRY","page":"Tuning","text":"The scene-split registry of record: every scene list of the stack (configs/splits.toml, parsed at precompile).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SPLIT_REGISTRY"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SPLIT_REGISTRY_PATH","page":"Tuning","text":"The path of the scene-split registry of record, configs/splits.toml.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SPLIT_REGISTRY_PATH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_DEFAULTS","page":"Tuning","text":"The shipped tuning defaults of record (configs/tuning.toml, parsed at precompile).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_DEFAULTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_DEFAULTS_PATH","page":"Tuning","text":"The path of the shipped tuning defaults of record, configs/tuning.toml.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_DEFAULTS_PATH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_TAGS","page":"Tuning","text":"TUNING_TAGS\n\nEvery tagged strategy family of a [tuning] spec and its tag registry (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's TagRegistry: TOML tag → type, the tag snake_case of the type's name minus the family's suffix; retired tags name their successor). A tagged table names its type with the key type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TUNING_TAGS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.BudgetSampling","page":"Tuning","text":"BudgetSampling(sizes, n_draws, seed, rng)\n\nThe validation-budget draws: for each budget n ∈ sizes, n_draws simple random samples of n validation units (budget_units), draw r from budget_rng(b, n, r); the confidence interval over a budget's draws resamples from resampling_rng(b, n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.BudgetSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CenteredWindow","page":"Tuning","text":"CenteredWindow(factor)\n\n[center / factor, center · factor] about a center the caller supplies: a transferred operating point centres on itself. TOML tag \"centered\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CenteredWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoarseFine","page":"Tuning","text":"CoarseFine(coarse, extension, edge_margin, fine, criterion, cross_validation)\n\nThe coarse-fine search of one scalar (Evaluation cvprobe.jl): the one-axis case of JointCoarseFine, its axis spelled flat — sweep the coarse axis, extend it by extension.npts points on a side whenever the argmin lies within `edgemarginpoints of that edge (at mostextension.maxroundsper side), then sweepfineover the coarse argmin's bracketing neighbours and take the free argmin.criterionaggregates a candidate's validation errors over the validation units ofcrossvalidation. TOML tag\"coarse_fine\"`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoarseFine"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoarseFineAxis","page":"Tuning","text":"CoarseFineAxis(coarse, extension, fine, expensive)\n\nOne axis of a JointCoarseFine search: its coarse axis (window rule\n\nspacing), its expand-on-boundary rule, the fine spacing over the coarse\n\nargmin's bracket, and whether it is expensive — a value of an expensive axis costs a run the cheap axes reuse (a detection run, say), so the search asks for the cells one expensive value at a time. Untagged; defaults [types.coarse_fine_axis].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoarseFineAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoverageWindow","page":"Tuning","text":"CoverageWindow(q_lo, q_hi, log_margin, zeta_octaves)\n\nThe COVERAGE window of a native axis (outlier half-width, loss width): every native the scale-multiple window can request at any reference scale the validation pool can produce, [native(lo, ŝ_min/ζ)·e^{-m}, native(hi, ŝ_max·ζ)·e^{m}] with [lo, hi] the scale-multiple window, (ŝ_min, ŝ_max) = scale_bounds(s2, (q_lo, q_hi)), ζ = 2^zeta_octaves (the scale corruptions the run reads out at, 0 for none) and m = log_margin (log headroom so a request at the bounds sits strictly inside). TOML tag \"coverage\".\n\nThe name is the GUARANTEE, not the mechanism: the realized grid contains every native the window can request at any scale the pool produces, so a windowed free argmin provably cannot leave its grid. That is what the validation-budget mode needs, where 3500 draws share one sweep and each has its own ŝ(n).\n\nq_lo == q_hi is legal and is the ONE-ANCHOR case: both sides anchor on the same √quantile(σ̂², q), and at q = 0.5 that is reference_scale(s2), so coverage { q_lo = 0.5, q_hi = 0.5, zeta_octaves = 0.0 } is the WINDOW-MATCHED grid — the native image of the scale-multiple window and nothing more. A tune has one reference scale, so this is the tuning case; a spread band is for the modes that have many. (It replaced a separate StandardizedNativeWindow rule, which was this rule with its band collapsed and was a distinct type only because the constructor forbade q_lo == q_hi.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CoverageWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CrossValidation","page":"Tuning","text":"CrossValidation(scene_split::SceneSplit, validation::UnitSampling, test::UnitSampling)\n\nThe data side of a tuning: the scenes it tunes on and reports on (scene_split), and the units of each side it reads (validation, test). A driver states which samplings it supports by the signature of the method it calls (say cv::CrossValidation{<:SceneSplit, StratifiedSampling}), so an unsupported spec is a MethodError. Applied by validation_units and test_units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.CrossValidation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ExhaustiveSampling","page":"Tuning","text":"ExhaustiveSampling()\n\nEvery unit of every scene. TOML tag \"exhaustive\" (retired: \"all\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ExhaustiveSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Factorial","page":"Tuning","text":"Factorial(axes, criterion, cross_validation)\n\nEvery combination of the named axes (hand-chosen settings of a method's configuration keys), scored by criterion over the validation units of cross_validation. TOML tag \"factorial\".\n\nNOT a windowed scheme: there is no reference scale and no standardized window, and nothing about it is extended on a boundary. Its consumers live outside this package: a downstream product-grid spec reads its axes through it, and a joint operating-point search is one. Deleting it on the strength of a grep over src/ and test/ alone breaks them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Factorial"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.FiniteMedian","page":"Tuning","text":"FiniteMedian()\n\nThe median over the FINITE validation errors, +Inf when there are none (so an all-failed column loses every argmin). TOML tag \"finite_median\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.FiniteMedian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.FixedWindow","page":"Tuning","text":"FixedWindow(lo, hi)\n\nA window stated in the axis's native units. TOML tag \"fixed\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.FixedWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridExtension","page":"Tuning","text":"GridExtension(npts, max_rounds)\n\nThe expand-on-boundary rule of a GRID axis: on a boundary argmin, extend the grid by npts points on the clipped side, at most max_rounds rounds (a still-clipped argmin is then recorded and refused by the readers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridExtension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridSpacing","page":"Tuning","text":"GridSpacing\n\nHow the points of a tuning grid are spaced over a window [lo, hi]: LogPerDecade, LogPerOctave, LogCount, LinearCount. grid builds the grid; extend continues a spaced grid past either end at the same step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridSpacing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridSpec","page":"Tuning","text":"GridSpec\n\nThe persisted, reproducible description of one realized tuning grid: SpacedGrid (a window, a spacing and its extension) or ListedGrid (explicit values). grid(spec) realizes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.GridSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Incumbent","page":"Tuning","text":"Incumbent(values, confidence, n_resamples, seed)\n\nThe setting in force before a search (values: axis name → value). The search's selection replaces it only when a paired bootstrap of the units (n_resamples draws, StableRNG(seed)) puts the selection's objective below the incumbent's with probability at least confidence. Untagged; defaults [types.incumbent].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Incumbent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.InverseGamma","page":"Tuning","text":"InverseGamma()\n\nProper inverse-gamma prior IG(α₀, β₀); hyperparameters come from a fitting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.InverseGamma"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Jeffreys","page":"Tuning","text":"Jeffreys()\n\nImproper Jeffreys prior — parameterless, equivalent to IGPrior(0, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Jeffreys"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.JointCoarseFine","page":"Tuning","text":"JointCoarseFine(axes, edge_margin, criterion, cross_validation, incumbent = nothing)\n\nThe coarse-fine search over the product of named axes (CoarseFineAxis), jointly: the coarse product grid, each axis extended on a side while the joint argmin lies within edge_margin points of that face, then the product of each axis's fine bracket about the joint argmin and its free argmin. CoarseFine is its one-axis case. An optional Incumbent is kept unless the selection beats it (Evaluation fine_search). TOML tag \"joint_coarse_fine\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.JointCoarseFine"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LinearCount","page":"Tuning","text":"LinearCount(n)\n\nn ≥ 2 linearly spaced points over the window. TOML tag \"linear_count\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LinearCount"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ListedAxis","page":"Tuning","text":"ListedAxis(values)\n\nAn axis of hand-chosen settings (a factorial tuning's axis). TOML tag \"listed\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ListedAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ListedGrid","page":"Tuning","text":"ListedGrid(values)\n\nA grid given by its values (finite, strictly increasing): the factorial tunings whose axes are hand-chosen settings. It has no spacing, so it cannot be extended. TOML tag \"listed\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ListedGrid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogCount","page":"Tuning","text":"LogCount(n)\n\nn ≥ 2 log-spaced points over the window (a COUNT, whatever the window's width in decades). TOML tag \"log_count\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogCount"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogPerDecade","page":"Tuning","text":"LogPerDecade(per_decade)\n\nLog spacing at a DENSITY: d = log10(hi / lo) decades hold d·per_decade intervals, so the grid has round(Int, d·per_decade) + 1 points (at least 2) and its log10 step is d / (n - 1) — 1 / per_decade when d·per_decade is an integer. (d·per_decade POINTS would space them coarser than advertised: the fencepost.) TOML tag \"log_per_decade\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogPerDecade"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogPerOctave","page":"Tuning","text":"LogPerOctave(per_octave)\n\nLog spacing at a density per factor of two: round(Int, log2(hi / lo)·per_octave) + 1 points (at least 2) — the half-octave ladders (per_octave = 2) of the PnP and detector tunings. TOML tag \"log_per_octave\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.LogPerOctave"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MLE","page":"Tuning","text":"MLE()\n\nClosed-form MLE on the inverse-gamma family via the Gamma reparameterisation 1/X ~ Gamma(α, 1/β). Requires length(σ²s) ≥ 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MLE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MeanAverageAccuracy","page":"Tuning","text":"MeanAverageAccuracy(max_error)\n\nThe continuous mAA@max_error of the validation errors (VisualGeometryToolkitCore Synthetic.mean_average_accuracy; a non-finite error is a failure that stays in the denominator), MAXIMISED (the objective is its negative). TOML tag \"mean_average_accuracy\".\n\nUNITS-NEUTRAL: max_error is in the errors' own unit — degrees on a pose error, mm for BlobBoards' bmvc2026 inter-board invariance. The one mAA criterion: a second spelling MAA(max_error_deg) carried the identical statistic under a field name that asserted degrees, which a criterion handed millimetres cannot. It is retired (2026-10-03) along with its \"maa\" tag; the [schemes.factorial] default that named it now names this one. The statistic itself is VisualGeometryToolkitCore's, the stack's one definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MeanAverageAccuracy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MedianError","page":"Tuning","text":"MedianError()\n\nThe median validation pose error with every failure (a non-finite error) counted as +Inf. TOML tag \"median_error\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.MedianError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PairUnit","page":"Tuning","text":"An image pair of a two-view dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PairUnit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PriorFitting","page":"Tuning","text":"PriorFitting\n\nAbstract supertype for \"how do we derive (α₀, β₀) from the per-pair σ̂² data?\" Concrete subtypes implement fit_prior for one or more ScalePriorFamily values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PriorFitting"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PriorSpec","page":"Tuning","text":"PriorSpec(family::ScalePriorFamily, fitting::PriorFitting)\n\nCompound spec: the prior family + the algorithm that turns σ̂² data into its parameters. The named specs are the constructor functions JeffreysPriorSpec and IGRobustSpec; the set a sweep iterates is ALL_PRIOR_SPECS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.PriorSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.QueryUnit","page":"Tuning","text":"A query image of an absolute-pose dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.QueryUnit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RegisteredSceneSplit","page":"Tuning","text":"RegisteredSceneSplit(name, validation_scenes, test_scenes)\n\nA scene split registered under name in configs/splits.toml (the one file of record of every scene list). A config names it (scene_split = { type = \"registered\", name = \"...\" }); the parser fills in the lists from the registry, so the complete table — and with it the spec hash and every artifact — carries the lists the run used. Both lists are nonempty, free of duplicates and DISJOINT; they are kept sorted. TOML tag \"registered\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RegisteredSceneSplit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RobustFit","page":"Tuning","text":"RobustFit(alpha0 = 1.0)\n\nRobust median-based fitting of an inverse-gamma prior: β₀ = α₀ · median(σ²s) with fixed shape α₀ (default 1.0). Median is robust to heavy-tailed σ̂² distributions (HEB).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.RobustFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SamplingUnit","page":"Tuning","text":"SamplingUnit\n\nWhat one unit of a dataset is — what a UnitSampling draws: PairUnit (an image pair of a two-view dataset) or QueryUnit (a query image of an absolute-pose dataset). A dataset declares its own with sampling_unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SamplingUnit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ScaleMultipleWindow","page":"Tuning","text":"ScaleMultipleWindow(lo, hi)\n\nThe standardized window [lo, hi] of scale multiples (a width as a multiple of the estimated inlier scale, σ-units) of a tuning run: the dimensionless window every windowed free argmin searches, mapped to a native axis at the supplied scale (window_columns), and the window of the scale-multiple axis itself (StandardizedWindow). It carries no grid: the grids are built by the spacings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ScaleMultipleWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ScalePriorFamily","page":"Tuning","text":"ScalePriorFamily\n\nAbstract supertype for the scale-prior distribution family. Concrete subtypes identify the kind of prior (its parameter shape and admissible values); the values come from a PriorFitting applied to σ̂² data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.ScalePriorFamily"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneMeanLowerMedian","page":"Tuning","text":"SceneMeanLowerMedian()\n\nThe mean over scenes of each scene's lower_median validation error (scenes without a finite median are left out; NaN when none is left). errors is one error vector per scene. TOML tag \"scene_mean_lower_median\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneMeanLowerMedian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneMeanMedian","page":"Tuning","text":"SceneMeanMedian()\n\nThe mean over scenes of each scene's median FINITE validation error (Stats scene_mean_median: scenes without a finite error are left out; NaN when none is left). errors is one error vector per scene. TOML tag \"scene_mean_median\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneMeanMedian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneSplit","page":"Tuning","text":"SceneSplit\n\nWhich scenes a cross-validation tunes on and which it reports on: RegisteredSceneSplit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SceneSplit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SpacedAxis","page":"Tuning","text":"SpacedAxis(window::WindowRule, spacing::GridSpacing)\n\nAn axis whose grid is spacing over the window window evaluates to. TOML tag \"spaced\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SpacedAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SpacedGrid","page":"Tuning","text":"SpacedGrid(lo, hi, spacing, n_lo = 0, n_hi = 0)\n\nThe grid grid(lo, hi, spacing) extended by n_lo points below lo and n_hi above hi at the same step (log or linear). TOML tag \"spaced\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.SpacedGrid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.StandardizedWindow","page":"Tuning","text":"StandardizedWindow()\n\nThe run's ScaleMultipleWindow itself: the window of the dimensionless scale-multiple axis. TOML tag \"standardized\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.StandardizedWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.StratifiedSampling","page":"Tuning","text":"StratifiedSampling(fraction, cap_per_scene, seed, rng)\n\nA uniform random sample of each scene (the stratum) separately, so the scene mix is preserved: of a scene's n units, max(1, round(fraction·n)) drawn without replacement, then at most cap_per_scene of those (0 = no cap), returned in the scene's own order. Scene s draws from rng(stable_seed(seed, s)) — its NAME, so adding or reordering other scenes leaves its sample unchanged. TOML tag \"stratified\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.StratifiedSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningArtifact","page":"Tuning","text":"TuningArtifact\n\nA loaded tuning artifact (load_tuning_artifact): spec (the TuningSpec it ran under), hash, grid_specs and the realized grids (Dict{Symbol,…}, rebuilt from the specs), unit (the SamplingUnit its cross-validation drew), code (the code identity) and data (the run's own keys). Indexing with a string reads data: art[\"arms\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningArtifact"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningAxis","page":"Tuning","text":"TuningAxis\n\nOne tuning axis of a spec: SpacedAxis (a window rule and a spacing) or ListedAxis (explicit values). grid_spec(axis; context...) realizes it as the persisted GridSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningCriterion","page":"Tuning","text":"TuningCriterion\n\nThe objective a cross-validation minimizes over the validation errors of a candidate (tune_objective(criterion, errors)); each concrete criterion is named in TOML by its tag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningCriterion"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningScheme","page":"Tuning","text":"TuningScheme\n\nA family of cross-validation, with every parameter it runs under: CoarseFine and JointCoarseFine (the one coarse-fine search of Evaluation cv_probe.jl, in one and in N dimensions), WindowedBootstrap, Factorial.\n\nA new search backend (a surrogate model over the same axes, say) is a new TuningScheme whose search returns the record of Evaluation coarse_search / fine_search — selected, boundary_hit, evaluated — so the artifact, its gated reader and the bootstrap serve it unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningSpec","page":"Tuning","text":"TuningSpec(name, scheme)\n\nOne named cross-validation of record: the [tuning.<name>] table of a config, parsed (tuning_spec). Persisted with every artifact the run writes (save_tuning_artifact), with its tuning_hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.TuningSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Unfitted","page":"Tuning","text":"Unfitted()\n\nParameterless families take Unfitted — there's nothing to fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.Unfitted"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.UniformSampling","page":"Tuning","text":"UniformSampling(fraction, cap, seed, rng)\n\nA uniform random sample of the units of all the side's scenes POOLED: of the N units, max(1, round(fraction·N)) drawn without replacement, then at most cap of those (0 = no cap), returned in the data's order (scenes by name, each scene's units in its own order). The draw is rng(stable_seed(seed, \"simple_random\")) over that order — the salt keeps its historical spelling, so a sample is unchanged by the type's rename. TOML tag \"uniform\" (retired: \"simple_random\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.UniformSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.UnitSampling","page":"Tuning","text":"UnitSampling\n\nWhich units of a side's scenes a cross-validation reads: ExhaustiveSampling, StratifiedSampling (per scene) or UniformSampling (pooled over the scenes). Applied by sample_units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.UnitSampling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.WindowRule","page":"Tuning","text":"WindowRule\n\nHow an axis's window (lo, hi) is found: FixedWindow, StandardizedWindow, CoverageWindow, CenteredWindow. window_bounds(rule; context...) evaluates it; each rule takes only the context it needs and refuses a missing one.\n\nFactored, a window is STATED or PLACED, and the placed ones differ only in their ANCHOR and how their endpoints leave it:\n\nrule anchor endpoints\nFixedWindow — its own two native numbers\nStandardizedWindow — the scale-multiple window verbatim\nCoverageWindow (ŝ_min/ζ, ŝ_max·ζ), per side the window through native, ± margin\nCenteredWindow a supplied native point ÷/× factor\n\nNote where ζ sits: it scales the ANCHOR, never the endpoints. A window whose anchor spreads is asserting that the scale could be anywhere in that spread — right for a ζ ladder or a budget sweep, wrong for a tune, which has one reference scale. Anchor spread is bought with the grid's resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.WindowRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.WindowedBootstrap","page":"Tuning","text":"WindowedBootstrap(scale_multiple_window, outlier_halfwidth, loss_width,\n                  scale_multiple, criterion, cross_validation, budget_sampling)\n\nThe bootstrap tuning harness (Evaluation WindowedGridMethod): per draw of budget_sampling from the validation units of cross_validation, the free argmin of criterion over the native-grid columns inside the scale-multiple window at the draw's reference scale. outlier_halfwidth and loss_width are the native grids, scale_multiple the standardized display axis. TOML tag \"windowed_bootstrap\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.WindowedBootstrap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.IGRobustSpec","page":"Tuning","text":"Robust IG prior with shape α₀ and scale fit from median(σ̂²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.IGRobustSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.JeffreysPriorSpec-Tuple{}","page":"Tuning","text":"Improper Jeffreys prior — equivalent to PriorSpec(Jeffreys(), Unfitted()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.JeffreysPriorSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.bootstrap_argmin-Tuple{TuningCriterion, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}, Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"bootstrap_argmin(criterion::TuningCriterion, stats, rng; n_resamples) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe sampling distribution of a cross-validation's selection: stats[c] is candidate c's unit_statistics (one row per sampling unit — a frame, a pair, a scene). Each of n_resamples resamples draws the units WITH replacement (rand(rng, 1:n, n), n the number of units) and returns the candidate whose criterion objective over the resampled rows is smallest (the first such candidate on a tie). The candidates are any flat set — the cells of an N-dimensional search included; the caller maps the indices to their coordinates and reads the interval it wants (per-axis quantiles, say).\n\nThis is the bootstrap of the SELECTION over a fixed candidate set. The windowed budget bootstrap (Evaluation bootstrap_trials) differs in what it resamples — budget draws of n validation units for a tuner, each draw evaluated on the test side — and is not a special case of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.bootstrap_argmin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.budget_rng-Tuple{BudgetSampling, Integer, Integer}","page":"Tuning","text":"The RNG of draw r at budget n: rng(stable_seed(seed, \"budget\", n, r)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.budget_rng"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.budget_units-Tuple{BudgetSampling, Integer}","page":"Tuning","text":"budget_units(b::BudgetSampling, n_units) -> Vector{(n, r, units)}\n\nEvery draw: min(n, n_units) distinct indices of 1:n_units without replacement from budget_rng(b, n, r), sorted. Callers supply the ELIGIBLE unit count and map the indices back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.budget_units"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.code_identity-Tuple{Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"code_identity(code) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe code a tuning result came from: for this package and every module of code (the caller's packages whose code determines the result — the estimator and scorer packages it ran, say), version (the loaded package version) and git (the checkout's HEAD commit, suffixed -dirty for uncommitted changes; empty outside a git checkout); and the julia version with the RNG algorithms the run may draw from (rng, RNG_ALGORITHMS) — the streams are StableRNG's, but a bootstrap CI resamples from Julia's own default stream (Stats bootstrap_ci), which only the Julia version pins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.code_identity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.corrupt_native-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"Tuning","text":"corrupt_native(native::Real, ζ::Real) -> Float64\n\nThe deployment-corruption map (docs/src/protocol.md §3, miscalibration): a tuned-at-CALIBRATION native width (selection outlier half-width, loss width, or the oracle supplied scale a native is formed from) scaled by the deployment error ζ — the model of a mis-estimated inlier scale at deployment time. Every ζ-scaling of a calibrated native goes through this one function so the corruption sites are greppable.\n\nPARTITION-ANCHORED quantities never go through it: a tuned scale multiple, a seed-partition Student-t ŝ², or any quantity re-derived from the deployed data's own partition is immune to calibration error by construction — that immunity IS the claim the miscal experiment measures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.corrupt_native"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.extend-Tuple{SpacedGrid, Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"extend(spec::SpacedGrid, sides; npts) -> (spec′, n_lo)\n\nThe expand-on-boundary extension of a grid: npts more points on each side in sides ⊆ (:lo, :hi), continuing the base step. Every point of grid(spec) reappears (to rounding) in grid(spec′) at index + n_lo, the count prepended, so already-computed columns stay valid by index (see extension_offset). A ListedGrid has no spacing to continue and refuses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.extend"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.extension_offset-Tuple{SpacedGrid, SpacedGrid}","page":"Tuning","text":"extension_offset(base::GridSpec, sup::GridSpec) -> Int | nothing\n\nThe offset n_lo ≥ 0 at which grid(base) sits inside grid(sup) when sup is base or an extension of it — same window and spacing, at least as many points on each side — else nothing. The compatibility test of a resumed checkpoint, a merged sidecar and a pinned grid: specs are compared, never realized values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.extension_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.family_tag-Tuple{Jeffreys}","page":"Tuning","text":"family_tag(family::ScalePriorFamily) -> Symbol\n\nCompact symbol identifying the prior family. :jeffreys (parameterless) or :ig (inverse-gamma). Used in display + sidecar-key construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.family_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fit_prior-Tuple{InverseGamma, MLE, Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"fit_prior(::InverseGamma, ::MLE, σ²s) -> IGPrior\n\nMLE of the inverse-gamma shape/scale (α, β). Distributions.jl does not implement fit_mle(InverseGamma, ·) directly, so we use the closed-form transformation\n\nX ~ InverseGamma(α, β)  ⇔  1/X ~ Gamma(shape = α, scale = 1/β)\n\nand call fit_mle(Gamma, 1 ./ x). Returns nothing for n < 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fit_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fit_prior-Tuple{InverseGamma, RobustFit, Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"fit_prior(::InverseGamma, fit::RobustFit, σ²s) -> IGPrior\n\nRobust empirical prior: fixed shape α₀ = fit.alpha0, scale β₀ = α₀ · median(σ̂²). The IG prior mean is β₀/(α₀−1); with α₀=1 the mean is undefined but the mode β₀/(α₀+1) = α₀ · median(σ̂²) / 2 anchors near the data median.\n\nUsed for HEB-like datasets where per-pair σ̂² estimates are heavy-tailed (low-n_in pairs and non-planar GT-inlier sets contaminate MLE Gamma fits). The median is unaffected by individual outlier σ̂² values, so the prior location stays stable even with single-digit val-pool sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fit_prior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fitting_tag-Tuple{Unfitted}","page":"Tuning","text":"fitting_tag(fitting::PriorFitting) -> Symbol\n\nCompact symbol identifying the fitting algorithm. :none for parameterless families, :robust for median-based, :mle for closed-form MLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.fitting_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.grid-Tuple{Real, Real, GridSpacing}","page":"Tuning","text":"grid(lo, hi, spacing::GridSpacing) -> AbstractRange{Float64}\ngrid(spec::GridSpec) -> AbstractVector{Float64}\n\nThe tuning-grid constructor: npoints(lo, hi, spacing) points of spacing over [lo, hi] — Base range(lo, hi; length) for a linear spacing, Base logrange(lo, hi, n) for a log one (both hit lo and hi exactly). The second form realizes a persisted GridSpec, its extension included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.grid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.grid_spec-Tuple{SpacedAxis}","page":"Tuning","text":"grid_spec(axis::TuningAxis; context...) -> GridSpec\n\nThe realized, persistable grid spec of axis: the window its rule evaluates to under context (window_bounds) with its spacing, unextended; or the listed values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.grid_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.layer_tuning-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"layer_tuning(lower, upper) -> Dict\n\nupper's [tuning.<name>] table layered over lower's, the way the parser layers a config over the defaults: a key of upper wins; a nested table merges key by key — unless both name a type and the types differ, when upper's table replaces lower's (a different strategy does not inherit another's parameters). The one way a paper's protocol-level tuning table and a cell's overrides are combined before tuning_spec / resolve_tuning! read them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.layer_tuning"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.load_tuning_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Tuning","text":"load_tuning_artifact(path) -> TuningArtifact\n\nThe reader of a tuning artifact: refuses another format first (an ArgumentError naming the file: regenerate it by re-running its tuning), then reads the spec (complete — nothing inherited), checks its hash, and rebuilds every grid from its spec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.load_tuning_artifact"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.nearest_column-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Real}","page":"Tuning","text":"nearest_column(grid, x) -> Int\n\nIndex of the grid column nearest x in LOG space — the deployment evaluation helper (e.g. the column of the ζ-scaled tuned native). Evaluation only; tuning is always a windowed free argmin, never nearest-column selection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.nearest_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.npoints-Tuple{Real, Real, LogPerDecade}","page":"Tuning","text":"The number of points grid(lo, hi, s) has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.npoints"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.prior_label","page":"Tuning","text":"prior_label(spec::PriorSpec) -> String\n\nThe display label of a legal prior spec: \"Jeffreys\", \"IG-Robust\", \"IG-MLE\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.prior_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.read_tuning-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractDict}} where T","page":"Tuning","text":"read_tuning(::Type{T}, table) -> T\n\nA persisted COMPLETE table back to its type (no defaults inherited: a missing key is an error). The reader of every spec and grid spec stored in an artifact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.read_tuning"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.registered_split-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Tuning","text":"registered_split(name) -> RegisteredSceneSplit\n\nThe split [splits.<name>] of configs/splits.toml. An unknown name, an unknown key or a split violating RegisteredSceneSplit's invariants is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.registered_split"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.resampling_rng-Tuple{BudgetSampling, Integer}","page":"Tuning","text":"The RNG of the resampled confidence interval over budget n's draws: rng(stable_seed(seed, \"resampling\", n)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.resampling_rng"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.resolve_tuning!-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"resolve_tuning!(cfg) -> cfg\n\nReplace every [tuning.<name>] entry of a parsed config by its COMPLETE table (every parameter, defaults resolved — tuning_spec then tuning_table), in place. Experiments' run_key and write_run_records key and record a config resolved, so its run key and its recorded _config.toml cover every tuning parameter, including the ones it inherits from this package's defaults.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.resolve_tuning!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.sample_units-Tuple{ExhaustiveSampling, AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"sample_units(sampling::UnitSampling, units_by_scene) -> Dict{String,Vector}\n\nThe units sampling keeps of each scene of units_by_scene (an AbstractDict scene name ⇒ that scene's units, in the data's order): a Dict with every scene of the input, each holding its kept units in their original order (possibly none, for a pooled sample).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.sample_units"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.sampling_unit","page":"Tuning","text":"sampling_unit(::Type{D}) -> SamplingUnit\n\nThe unit a cross-validation samples from a dataset of type D (its SamplingUnit). A property of the data: every dataset type defines its own method, and there is no fallback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.sampling_unit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.save_tuning_artifact-Tuple{AbstractString, TuningSpec}","page":"Tuning","text":"save_tuning_artifact(path, spec::TuningSpec; grids, unit, code, data...) -> path\n\nThe writer of a tuning artifact: the spec (complete table — its cross-validation's scene lists included — name, hash), the grids (Dict{Symbol,<:GridSpec}, one per axis the run tuned on), the sampling unit its cross-validation drew (the data's sampling_unit), the code_identity of code (the caller's modules whose code determines the result; this package is always recorded) and the run's data (keyword values: arms, tuned indices, validation matrices, …), stamped with the :tuning format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.save_tuning_artifact"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.stats_objective","page":"Tuning","text":"stats_objective(criterion, stats) -> Real\n\nThe criterion's objective (minimised) over unit_statistics rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.stats_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.test_scenes-Tuple{CrossValidation}","page":"Tuning","text":"The test scenes of a cross-validation (sorted).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.test_scenes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.test_units-Tuple{CrossValidation, AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"test_units(cv::CrossValidation, units_by_scene) -> Vector{Pair{String,Vector}}\n\nThe units an evaluation reads: the test scenes of cv.scene_split (sorted) ⇒ the units cv.test keeps of each (see validation_units).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.test_units"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tune_objective-Tuple{MedianError, Any}","page":"Tuning","text":"tune_objective(criterion::TuningCriterion, errors) -> Real\n\nThe value a cross-validation MINIMIZES for one candidate: criterion applied to its validation errors (one vector per scene for a per-scene criterion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tune_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_criterion_label-Tuple{MedianError}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_criterion_label(criterion) -> String\n\nThe display label of a criterion, as persisted in result rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_criterion_label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_hash-Tuple{Union{TuningScheme, TuningSpec}}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_hash(spec) -> String\n\nThe identity of a tuning protocol: the SHA-256 (hex, first 16) of its complete scheme table printed as sorted TOML. Two specs with equal parameters hash equal whatever their names or how their configs spelled them; any parameter change moves it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_hash"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_spec-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractString}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_spec(cfg, name; under = Dict(), over = Dict()) -> TuningSpec\ntuning_spec(path::AbstractString, name; under, over) -> TuningSpec\n\nThe [tuning.<name>] entry of a config (a parsed TOML Dict or a file path), its absent keys inherited from the shipped defaults (configs/tuning.toml of this package). The entry must name its scheme (type); unknown keys and tags are an ArgumentError naming the key path.\n\nunder and over layer a campaign's protocol around the entry (layer_tuning): under (a protocol-level table of parameters shared by every entry, such as a cross_validation) is layered beneath the entry, keeping only the keys that are fields of the entry's scheme, so one table serves entries of different schemes; over (a cell's overrides) is layered above it, unfiltered. An over that names another scheme type replaces the entry, as layer_tuning replaces a nested table: the new scheme keeps none of the entry's keys (not even those both schemes have, such as cross_validation or criterion), and under is filtered against the new scheme's fields. With both empty (the default) the entry is read as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_specs-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_specs(cfg) -> Dict{String,TuningSpec}\n\nEvery [tuning.<name>] entry of a config, parsed (tuning_spec).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_specs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_table-Tuple{Union{GridExtension, ScaleMultipleWindow, BudgetSampling, CoarseFineAxis, GridSpacing, GridSpec, Incumbent, SceneSplit, TuningAxis, TuningCriterion, TuningScheme, UnitSampling, WindowRule, CrossValidation}}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_table(x) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe COMPLETE TOML table of a tuning strategy, scheme, grid spec or TuningSpec (whose table is its scheme's): every field under its own name (VisualGeometryToolkitCore toml_key), a tagged type's tag under type. Reading it back (tuning_spec, read_tuning) gives an == value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_toml-Tuple{Vararg{TuningSpec}}","page":"Tuning","text":"tuning_toml(specs::TuningSpec...) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe [tuning] table holding specs under their names (write_toml-ready).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.tuning_toml"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.unit_statistics-Tuple{TuningCriterion, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Tuning","text":"unit_statistics(criterion::TuningCriterion, errors::AbstractMatrix) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nWhat the bootstrap of a selection needs of one candidate's validation errors (a units × items matrix, one row per sampling unit): one row per unit, from which the criterion's objective over ANY multiset of units is exact (bootstrap_argmin). For MeanAverageAccuracy — a mean of per-item scores — the row is the unit's score sum and item count (VisualGeometryToolkitCore maa_score), two numbers in place of items; for any other criterion it is the errors themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.unit_statistics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.validation_scenes-Tuple{CrossValidation}","page":"Tuning","text":"The validation scenes of a cross-validation (sorted).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.validation_scenes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.validation_units-Tuple{CrossValidation, AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"validation_units(cv::CrossValidation, units_by_scene) -> Vector{Pair{String,Vector}}\n\nThe units a tuning reads: the validation scenes of cv.scene_split (sorted) ⇒ the units cv.validation keeps of each. units_by_scene maps scene names to their units in the data's order and must hold every validation scene (other scenes are ignored).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.validation_units"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.window_bounds-Tuple{FixedWindow}","page":"Tuning","text":"window_bounds(rule::WindowRule; scale_multiple_window = nothing, native = nothing,\n       s2 = nothing, center = nothing) -> (lo, hi)\n\nEvaluate a window rule. scale_multiple_window is the run's ScaleMultipleWindow, native(scale_multiple, scale) the axis's native map (the caller's native_map(axis; d_g)), s2 the validation pool's per-pair σ̂², center the axis's centre; a rule refuses a context it needs and is not given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning.window_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tuning/#VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning._toml_hash-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Tuning","text":"_toml_hash(d::AbstractDict) -> String\n\nThe content hash of the evaluation stack: the SHA-256 (hex, first 16) of d printed as sorted TOML. Independent of key order and fixed across Julia versions (unlike Base.hash); a TOML round trip of d reproduces it. Behind tuning_hash and Experiments run_key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Tuning._toml_hash"},{"category":"section","location":"#VisualGeometryToolkitEval","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval is the evaluation package of the VisualGeometryToolkit stack. It holds what the stack's research projects share when they benchmark robust estimators: statistics, a codified cross-validation (CV), an experiment framework, figure renderers, scoring-pool input and output, raw dataset readers, and an evaluator that composes a RANSAC scorer with a local optimizer (LO).","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"VisualGeometryToolkitEval\")\n\nThe package requires Julia 1.12 or later. It depends on VisualGeometryToolkitCore, VisualGeometryToolkitIO and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-modules","page":"Home","text":"Each module's names are imported from it, for example using VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools: load_pool. The top module exports the modules and the driver command line (cli_option, start_output_flush!).\n\nModule What it holds\nStats finite and lower medians, the validation reference scale, the mean average accuracy (mAA), bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrap intervals, means of per-scene medians and the seed derivation\nTuning the CV specification: grids, window rules, objectives, scene splits and unit samplings, budget draws, schemes, the [tuning] TOML parser and the tuning artifact\nEvaluation the coarse-to-fine search of every CV, the bootstrap tuning harness and the composition evaluator\nExperiments the stage runner, run keys and run records, provenance, and the data layer of a paper project\nRender PGFPlots figures and tables in the house style\nPools the scoring pool, its residuals, and the perspective-n-point (PnP) pool file\nDatasets raw readers of PhotoTourism, HEB, RoMa and SuperPoint+LightGlue correspondences, and of the depth-validated stereo datasets ETH3D, ScanNet1500, KITTI and 7-Scenes\nDataArtifacts a paper project's published input data as Julia artifacts\nReproduce the reproducible-figures contract of a paper\nPaperProjects the paper-project conventions: one check and one template","title":"The modules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-read-next","page":"Home","text":"Getting started runs the first examples.\nThe experimental protocol is the one statement of how the stack's experiments tune, evaluate and report.\nThe [tuning] schema lists every parameter of a cross-validation and the tuning artifact that persists it.\nThe composition evaluator describes how a scorer and a local optimizer are evaluated together.\nReproducible figures and Paper projects state the conventions every paper of the stack follows.\nDefaults and file formats lists where the defaults live and how artifacts are versioned.\nThe API reference has one page per module.","title":"Where to read next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval is part of VisualGeometryToolkit. Please cite the toolkit. Its CITATION.cff is the source of this entry, and GitHub's \"Cite this repository\" button exports it:\n\n@software{pritts2026vgtk,\n  author = {Pritts, James and Sauer, Hendrik},\n  title  = {{VisualGeometryToolkit.jl}: Julia Packages for Multiple-View Geometry},\n  url    = {https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/VisualGeometryToolkit.jl},\n  year   = {2026}\n}\n\nPlease also cite the papers behind the methods you use. They are listed on the portal.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"module","location":"#VisualGeometryToolkitEval","page":"Home","text":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval\n\nThe evaluation base of the visual-geometry stack: statistics (Stats), the codified cross-validation (Tuning, Evaluation), the stage/run-record framework (Experiments), rendering (Render), pool IO (Pools), the raw dataset readers (Datasets), the published-data catalog (DataArtifacts), the reproducible-figures contract (Reproduce) and the paper-project conventions (PaperProjects), with the artifact formats, the defaults of record and the driver CLI. Depends on VisualGeometryToolkitCore, VisualGeometryToolkitIO and the core estimator VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation (its loop and problems, for the composition evaluator) — never on a paper's scorer or optimizer package.\n\nEach submodule's API is reached through it (using VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Pools); this module exports the submodules and the CLI.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"VisualGeometryToolkitEval"}]
}
